      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <p>McDOUGALL'S Pate SELF—RAISING FLOUR Y RFOR IMPUTE&amp; G The Examiner Saturday, January 23,1909.</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION - HOME.</p>
        <p>THE BECOMING BUSBY.</p>
        <p>The coming of the busby has effected quite a revolution in the world of millinery, more especially so far as fur toclues are concerned, for these large soft shapes, in many instances, are not mounted on any kind of frame or wire, but are left without interior support, and lined only v.ith silk or satin, so that they can be pinned on to the coiffure in any fashion which may happen to be most becom- ing. In sable and in mink these busbies are exceedingly becoming, and they are being .made also in ermine and in chinchilla, in stone-marten, and in Persian lamb, as well as in swansdown and in marabout. They look ,best of all, undoubtedly, declares the Lady's Pirtorial, when they are seen in the company of stoles or pelerines and giant muffs to match.</p>
