      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Earthquake Foundlings.—A pathetic feature of the destruction of Italian towns by the earthquake, is the difficulty, and in many cases the impossibility, of tracing the identity of young children, who have been saved. Many there are too young to know or tell their names, and not a parent or relative is left to claim them or to say to whom they belonged. Not a few of these infants must have belonged to well-to-do people, but they are in danger of losing their birthrights, lineage, property, and all traces of family. The Children's Ilymn.—Sir Walter Parrett has been complaining of the • weak and mawkish "hymn tunes of our time, and it must be admitted that the majority in our hymn books lack a happy combination of words and music. There are, however, many exceptions, and the Sunday School Union is arranging for February 7th a jubilee celebration of the fiftieth anniversary of the composition of that popular 113,mn "There's a Friend for little children." It is suggested thot the hymn should be sung in all the schools of all denominations on that day. Its author' Mr. Albert Midlane—now over eighty—is still living. An irournaster by trade he has written and published over I,COO hymns, and lives in retirement at Newport, in the Isle of Wight. Smoking for Nearly 100 Years.—Juvenile smoking has been restricted by law, in nearly every country, and boys under sixteen years of age will do well to remember that the Childrens' Act comes into force on April Ist, after which they may be summarily dealt with by policeman and park-keepers, if found smoking or in possession of cigarettes. Smoking at a tender age is not however harmful in every case, and a certain Mrs. Catherine Sway who recently celebrated her 107th birthday in America, attributes her long life to the fact that she has regularly smoked tobacco since she was eleven. She belongs to the same State as Mrs. Carrie Nation, who would ban tobacco as well as alcohol, but Mrs. Sway, still smokes several pipes daily, and is to have a huge tobacco pipe carved on her tombstone. Crazes to Avoid.—Shoes and stockings, and hats, are in vogue again for children and happily we see little comparatively of the "hardening process" nowadays. Possibly mothers and nurses have realised at length that the "hardening process" is fraught in the long ran with considerable danger to the unfortunate little ones, or it may be that the craze has simply fizzled out, but any- way it has passed, and let us hope never to return. Comfortable, stout, well-fitting boots and stockings— not socks—should be allowed children during the winter month, and their headgear should be light in weight and of moderate size only, so as not to blow about, and irritate the small wearers in a wind. It has been pointed out by many learned members of the medical profession that the no-hat craze is one we should do well to ignore. and further of this we may rest assured that we, who ' allow our girls to go hatless, are laying up for them a I very fine crop of wrinkes, by the time they are little more than out of their teens.</p>
        <p>Sleeveless Coats.—Among the very up-to-date, the sleeveless coat is likely to enjoy some measure of popularity, and during the coming spring, worn over a double-breasted Princess dress, with tightly-fitting sleeves. The French are very fond of sleeveless wraps, but so far we have never taken the notion enthusiasti- cally to heart. To our eyes it presents an unfinished appearance, and then, save during the summer months, the sleeveless coat is apt to be of little real use in this country. _ _ .</p>
        <p>The Passing of the Collar Band.—Our collar-bands,l ,L lA. tatter elaborately worked with tiny coloured beads, or narrow ribbons, or edged with costly lice—have been growingbigher and higher 1 during the past few months and now we may look for a reaction. Indeed several gowns prepared for Egypt and the Riviera have no collar bands at all and we are promised yet another collarless craze, about the time' .summer arr:ves. We can only lament this state of affairs, as it takes a very refined-looking girl or woman, with a really beautiful throat, to carry off the collar. less gown successfully. The majority of women look somewhat vulgar, and decidely undressed, when minus a collarband. Magpie Effects.—The perfectly plain black tulle scarf is much seen just now, not only with black, and white gowns, but also with coloured toilettes. To the fair- haired girl or woman, the notion is proving highly becoming, but the brunette—especially if she inclines to sallowness—should tread this particular path warily. For jet, modistes bare cultivated a great affection, during the past fey- weeks, and are showering it upon white net, the white net being then lined with white chiffon and mounted over white satin.</p>
        <p>The Murderous flat-Pin.—The huge hat has gone, but it has left a legacy of a large quantity of extra long hat pins, which are more than over dangerous now that they are being made use of forstnaller headgear. What with these, and the scarcely less aggressive quills of gigantic size, shopping at the sales, and mixing in crowds, makes one of necessity an expert at dodging, but this does not always avoid the danger. Two popular diversions in London this winter are roller-skating, and the public Whist drive, yet there are many novices to be seen at the rinks, tumbling about with Jong unguarded hat- pins, which are very dangerous to their companions. An unnecessarily long hat-pin is always liable to do damage, and no where more so than at a skating rink. Flowers in the Sick Room.—The appearance of the early spring flowers on sale in the streets reminds us that it is not in all cases desirable to bring scented flowers into the sick room. Many people are quite unaffected by them, but there are not a few with susceptible temperaments to whom their fragrance is harmful. Not all flowers are condemned in this respect, and some, like the scent of roses, are said to poss. ss a tonics and invigorating quality, but violets, lilies carnations, and oleanders are specially singled out as likely to he inimical to invalids with what the doctors call a " nervous heart." May aud lilac also have a depressing influence upon many people sensitive to the influence of odours, and there is a similar objection to the cultivation of indoor blooms, such as hyacinths and narcissus—a hobby of many floriculturalists who go in for window gardening. This, however, does not apply to all people, and how far injurious effects may result from the presence of certain flowers, is purely a ques- tion of personal susceptibility. About Onions.—Owing to a scarcity in the home crop, and a blight in Vie Continental onion districts, this particular vegetable is likely to remain higher in price for some time to come. This is a matter for regret, as the onion is excellent food, and the advanced price will prevent the poor from enjoyiug it as much as usual. Many fashionable women make a point of eating onions whenever possible, just simply boiled, with a tiny knob of butter tucked into the centre when served. They are supposed to Le excellent for the complexion, as is the apple, eaten raw while dressing in the morning. A Countess's Cookery Book.—Of cookery books there is no end, and yet another is shortly to be published by Georgia, Countess of Dailey. It will contain nearly 500 recipes of various kinds which have either been in Lady Dudley's family for many years or have been collected by her during a wide experiencb of hospitality. She was so often asked by her friends for copies of recipes that she has decided to publish this volume which is entitled " The Dailey Biok of Caokory and Household Recipes," and is shortly to be issued by Mr. Edward Arnold.</p>
        <p>AIADGE.</p>
        <p>INOREHSE YOUR WEIGHT AND RE3TOIIE YOUR STRENGTH. REMARKABLE TESTIMONY TO DR. CASSELL'S TABLETS.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. C. Shadwell, " Nirvana," Mandeville-road, Canterbury, writes :—" The wonderful benefit to n” general health and constitution which I obtained three -Tears ego from the use of Dr. Cassell's Tablets has, I am glad to say, proved permanent. At that time you remember I was exceptionally thin owing to digestive weakness, constipation and stomach troubles. but after a course of Dr. Cassi-11's Tablets I gaired 161bs. in weight, and became strong and healthy." Dr. Cassell's Tablets are a safe and certain cure for all forms of nerve and physical weakness, loss of flesh, prostration, heart weakness, anemia, debility, children's weakness, Indigestion and stomach troubles. Price, 101 d , Is. Id., and 2s. 9d. of all chemists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>SMART NEW COSTUME.</head>
        <p>A SMART NEW COSTUME.</p>
        <p>Pictured in our illustration is a new and particularly smart winter costume, which is carried out in cloth of a delightful tone of highwayman-green. The cloth employed is of medium weight but very firm cicse weave, and has a surface of beautiful satinlike finish. The coat is extremely long, full seven-eighths length, and has a breast-seam running from shoulder to hem on either side. From the neck turn back wide, soft Directoire revers and collar of black satin edged with a bind- ing of heavy, black silk braid. Similar braid</p>
        <p>edges the fronts end bottom of the cent, th 3 braid being carried along the edges of a long slit, which is cut up nearly to the waist on either side just under the arm. In front, on each side of the braided edges, come three large handsome buttons, eech finished with a loop of heavy silk cord. Buttons decorate each side of the braided slit in similar style. The sleeves ere plain and small, and are finished with cuffs to match the collar. The skirt is of walking length, and is of the latest four-piece shape. ONLY A WOMAN'S HAIR. Nothing is more disconcerting to the per- son who is lethargic in regard to fashion, writes Ella Hepworth Dixon in the Sketch, than the swiftness with which the modern woman (or more probably the modern barber) changes the way of dressing the hair. One night, at the play—for the London theatre is the place to see new coiffures—you are sur- rounded with ladies with vast Gainsborough heads piled high on the top. The next week, lo I the women in the stalls and boxes have crowns as flat as pancakes, with distended hair at the sides. A fortnight passes, and the girls are Greek in outline, or have vast protuberant chignons challenging regard at the back. Now the latest word from Paris tells us that lovely woman is about lo wear her locks hanging. as Nature wills, straight down her back. This would be a final solu- tion of the difficulty, and would make life a more simple affair than it is at present, if one could for one moment believe that the hairdressers would allow us to wear this primordial coiffure for more than one month at most. Naturally they would not. Othello's occupation would he gone, indeed, if ladies went to balls and dinners with their hair in a state of nature. Not a doubt of it but the womenfolk will continue to torture and twist their locks at the bidding of the tyrant with the tongs.</p>
        <p>.41 Ivg:. \,,,,:y3i1d.f. „A-41 :.9 . ..;P„..,.:'.4,..,' \ ()(1E 1 / 1 g-', 1 iiio!j-1-4 ir- 1 1 iii, i!iil4./ oil! 0m,72.-, ii (I' •/ pliii iiklkt ' R, -`t,_ ‘ 1 old ti i 111010,'Ii rat 1 .7..ll\Yri lii.lN iII ,L.,1t1 ,:, , -,,, ~• ''•,f , , V , I ‘, -2 .....*...,x,....ei4L-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>NATTY LITTLE COAT.</head>
        <p>A NATTY LITTLE COAT.</p>
        <p>Our sketch illustrates an exceptionally at- tractive coat for the small girl's immediate wear. The original, which came from a well- known Paris house, waa carried out in rather thick faced-cloth of a pretty grey tone. The same idea, however, might be realised with equal success in biscuit, brown, peacock, or crimson cloth, in ivory blanket cloth, or in some pretty frieze or tweed. The coat is quite simple in shape, aria rather locee-fitting,</p>
        <p>though there is a defined waist-line, rather high up, at the back and sides. The front is double-breasted, and consists of a separate piece of material set on with overlaid edges and decorated with six big, handsome but- tons. From the neck turn back big, soft Directoire revers faced with velvet which ex- actly matches the cloth in colour. 'The coat is completed by a Consular collar faced with velvet to match the revers. The sleeves ore small, and are finished with cuffs that corro- spond with the collar. TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>SAVOURY EGGS.—A dish that is always a favourite one on the supper menu, and that is very easily made, requires six hard-boiled eggs, one ounce of butter, some anchovy sauce, and salt and pepper to taste. First of all shell the eggs, then cut them in half and remove the yolks, putting them in a basin with the butter, salt, pepper, and anchovy sauce to taste. Mix these ingredients well together, then rub the mixture through a sieve, taking Care not to crush the feathery curls of the mixture after it has passed through the sieve. Cut a tiny niece of each half of the white of egg so that it will stand firmly, then lightly fill in each case of white with the mixture. Arrange the eggs prettily among lettuce leaves.</p>
