      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>NEON AND TIIE AURORA.</p>
        <p>By means of sounding-balloons, Teissenene de Bort has collected specimen of the air up to more than eight and a-half miles. Analysis shews the presence of helium only in the layers lower than about six miles. Neon, on the contrary, is found at Ml levels, and this fact is regarded as confirming the identifica- tion of several spectroscopic lines of neon in the spectrum of the aurora borealis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>TIIE COLOUR OF LIGHTNING.</head>
        <p>TIIE COLOUR OF LIGHTNING.</p>
        <p>"For five years a tabulated record of the colours of lightning has been kept at Epsom, England, by Spen2er C. Ruc,iel, and he has made fifty-seven observations of forked light- ning in thunderstorms and seventy-eight ob- servations of sheet lightning. Great diversity in colour has been shown. Bed las been commonest in forked lightning, with blu 3 cicsely -following. and orange and green least frequent, and white is seen oftenr2st in sheet lightning, red and yellow coming next. Hail usually conies in a thunderstorm with blua lightning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>TIIE AFTERGLOW.</head>
        <p>TIIE AFTERGLOW.</p>
        <p>It has been shewn that the afterglow that follows the ordinary twilight, and which pro- duces such beautiful effects upon the snowy summits of the. high Alps, is a phenomenon of general occurren C. 2, and the hypothesis has been put forth ihat tbq light may be due to a peculiar form of radiation from the sun, compcsed of waves lying b2yond the ultra- violet of the spectrum, and remaining in the upper air a quarter of an hour after the dis- appearance of the visible sunset rays. The supposition is that these rays, although them- selves invisible, may excite phosphorescence in the atmosphere partiAes, thus producing visible glow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>STEEL CUT BY AT:s,</head>
        <p>STEEL CUT BY AT:s,</p>
        <p>" Diamond cuts diamond," but steel is cut with air. The ingenious method of rapidly cutting through iron or steel plate is based upon the fact that when iron at a high tem- perature is acted upon by a fine jet of oxygen the resulting iron oxide is more fusible than the iron itself, and passing away exposes a fresh surface of the metal ti the attack of the gas, so that a cut is produced along the line of action. In the early attempts to utilise this method in practice the metal was first heated to the required temperature in an oxy-hydro- gen flame, and then subjected to the action of the oxygen jet. Now, however, the heat- ing and oxidation are done at the same time, and the reing cut is much sharper. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>SEVRES BLUE.</head>
        <p>SEVRES BLUE.</p>
        <p>The ram OLIO blue colour given to the porcelain manufactured at Sevrcs has long been believed to be the result of a eecret pro- cess, and many legends exist about it. Louia Franc:let slims, in the Rerun? Scient 1111.1117, that it is an error to suppcse that Sevres blue cannot be produced elsewhere. As a matter of feet, it is produced in many French potteries, where sufficient care is t,.-,ken, and where pure oxide of cobalt is employed. Formerly it W:18 difficult to prccure this article without im- purities, which injured the colour; but chemical science has overcome all the diffi- culties. The same is true of the Chinese green known as ccladon. It was invented in Chins, but it can be perfectly reproduced elseNvhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>ENERGY CONSUMED FOR LIGHT.</head>
        <p>ENERGY CONSUMED FOR LIGHT.</p>
        <p>In a lecture delivered by Sir James Dewar on "Flame" before the Royal Institution in London he shewed the large amount of energy expended in the production of a small amount of light. The follo,Ang figures show how inefficient the various lighting devices now employed are from a scientific point cf view: Candle: Percentage of light, 2; non- luminous energy, 93. Oil: Percentage of light, 2; non-luminous energy, 98. Coal gas: Percent- age of light. 2; non-luminous energy, 98. In- candescent lamp: Percentage of .light, 3; non- luminous energy, 97. Arc lamp: Percentage of I:ght, 10; non-luminous energy, 90. Mag- nesium lamp: Percentage of light, 15; non- luminous energy, 85. Incandescent lamp: Percentage of light, 99; non-luminous energy, 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>ZINC IN PLANTS.</head>
        <p>ZINC IN PLANTS.</p>
        <p>Using very delicate methods of analysis, a French chemist, M. Javillier, has shewn that zinc exists in about fifty plants belonging to widely - separated botanical families, and growing on different kinds of soil. He has made a special study of a species . of mould containing zinc, and finds that the metal plays a very important part in the life of the plant, a trace of zinc being indispensable to its growth and development.. The addition to a culture medium of one ten-millionth part of the element increases the growth three to four times, and even one fifty-millionth exerts an appreciable influence. Besides establish- ing a series of curious facts, Which may serve as a starting point for many theoretical ques- tions, these investigations are of practical use, as pointing to the possible employment of zinc as a fertiliser. Similar investigations on the metal manganese in 'plants have already led to the use of this substance in agriculture as what is called a " complementary ferti- liser," with brilliant success. It is possible that the addition of zinc to this fertiliser would still further improve it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WHY CLOUDS FORM.</head>
        <p>WHY CLOUDS FORM.</p>
        <p>Cloud consists of minute water drops. The drops remain in the air because they are too small to fall quickly, the resistance of the air checking their fall. Really they do fall. Drops 1-I,oooin. in diameter fall about sin. per second; drops 1-10,000 in. in diameter fall about I-130in. per second. Cloud may be formed by the cooling of air by expansion. For instance, air near the surface of the ground may get warm, and may take up water vapour. It may get lighter than the sur- rounding oir and rise up. As it rises, the pres- sure on it lessens, and so it expands. In ig- panding it cools, end if it cools se that it cannot continue to hold all the water vapour it acquired when warm, the excess is changed into liquid in the form of cloud. We may cometimes watch clouds form thus in summer</p>
        <p>at the beginning of a thunderstorm. We may see them form, too, at the top of a mountain when the air blows against the side and is forced to go uphill, expanding and cooling as it goes. Mist is formed by the cooling of the air in contact with the cooled surface of the ground. Cloud may be formed, too. by the mixture of two winds—one warm and vapour- laden, the other cold. The mixture may not, be able to hold all the vapour, and .cloud formed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>PRICE OF WATER.</head>
        <p>THE PRICE OF WATER.</p>
        <p>, While we fully recognise the importance of water as an indispensable condition of life, we seldom realise what quantities of it exist in our daily food, or what high prices we have to pay for it in the ordinary course of •our purchases in shop or market. Take, for instance, the butcher's bill, which is usually the most serious item of domestic expendi- ture. It is a trifle disconcerting to he told that when the thrifty housewife expend 3 money upon the best cuts of licef, no less than three-quarters of the sum is paid for water. Yet such is unquestionably the ease— vouched for by the highest analytical authori- cies. Uncooked beef or mutton contains ,‘.xactly 75 per cent. (or three-fourths of the whole bulk) of water. Milk must be regarded as the type of a complete food. Yet milk, fresh from the con, and before it has paid a visit to the nearest pump or tap, contains between 85 and 83 per cent.; of water. This fact is exceedingly significant of the import- once which Nature attaches to water as a diluent of her food substances. Foods which contain only a small percentage of water are usually unfit for . human constimption until they have been coced. The culinary art, reduced to its simplest terins, - consists mainly in innumerable d s ices for putting water to food in an attractive manner. Bread is a .mpital case in point. Dry Ivheaten flour con- tains, as a rule, about l per cent. of water; ind dry wheaten flour would be voted any- thing but a sati,lfactory article of food by the majority: Bread, on the other hand,, is the acknowledged staff of lile. In this, its 2hanged form, the flour has received an addi- Lion of water until the percentage has risen L o from 45 to N. This increase of moisture vb.+ nrought atiout important chemical :hinges, which have converted the dry and minviting flour into a pleasant and easily. Aigested food.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>Cheat Contrast of a Lifetime.</head>
        <p>The Cheat Contrast of a Lifetime.</p>
        <p>From the Depths of Debility to the Best of Health.</p>
        <p>A cured woman's tribute to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>Lo.king back on the important events of her R life, Mrs. M. Webster, of 124, Chatsworth load, Chester- field, realises to-day that she is one of the happiest of women— happy because she appreciates the wonder- ful change from an existence saddened by the evils of Aggravated Anremia and Acute Debility, to a life of usefulness, activity and freedom from all ailments. Mrs. Webster acknowledges that she owes a debt of gratitude to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People for this change ; the striking contrast in her life is shown in her statements :</p>
        <p>" One of my first troubles was shortness of breath ; often I felt quite stifled as I panted for breath. " Then I became nervous and shaky ; my nerves seemed numbed and I lived in fear of some unexpected calamity. "Also my legs and hands trembled so ranch that it was a struggle to walk, and I could hold nothing steadily.</p>
        <p>"When a friend sent me a supply of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills I took them and certainly, noticed no farther diffi- culty in breathing. " As I continued, my nerves were steadied and new blood seemed to be flowing through my veins.</p>
        <p>" I had no feeling of fatigue after exercise, but a desire possessed me to get about and lead a busy life. "So I persevered and the cAour returned to my cheeks ; I regained my proper weight and appearance, and was free from all neuralgic troubles. "Then I took nourish- ing food again and enjoyed it, for my appetite was restored and I looked forward t meal times. "There was no need for me to take brandy or other stimulants, for Dr. Williams' Pink Pills were a wonderful tonic. "Since I was cured by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills I have not known a day's illness. I am as etrong as a woman should be, sleep well and awake refreshed, and have no thoughts or fears of Anmrnia or Debility now."</p>
        <p>"In time all the colour left my cheeks and I became thin and haggard. Sharp pains of neuralgia attacked my bead so fiercely that I wonier my reason remained. " For months I dragged on,all the time scarcely linowing the taste of solid food ; in fact, the mere sight of it was repugnant to me.</p>
        <p>" At Tast I became so feeble that I was only kept alive on a diet of milk and brandy. "In spite of all the medicines taken nothing seemed able to make me better ; everything failed to enrich my weak watery blood and I felt that I was past all hopes of recovery.</p>
        <p>POVERTY OF BLOOD AMONG ALL CLASSES. People in every station of life suffer because they need the Rich, New Blood that is supplied by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. In both sexes these pills have cured countless cases of Anwrnia, General Weakness, Indigestion, Nervous Disorders, St. Vitus' Dance, Rheumatism. Sciatica, and Skin Di.i•eases ; in the special aihnents'of the weaker sex they are invaluable. Sold by most dealers (but look on package for the seven words, " Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People," that mean genuine), or send direct to the Dr. Medicine Co., 40, Holborn Viaduct, London, 2s. 9d, for one box, or 13s. 9d. for six boxes, post free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>PHOTOGRAPHIC NOTES. By Veronica. PHOTOGRAPHIC EXHIBITIONS.</head>
        <p>PHOTOGRAPHIC NOTES. By Veronica. PHOTOGRAPHIC EXHIBITIONS.</p>
        <p>The season of exhibitions is now in full swing. The two great shows that almost equal in importance the annual London Exhibitions, the Scottish National has just been held at Wishaw, and the Northern, which is open until the end of the month, at Manchester. After these important shows, we have a long list of exhibitions, reaching well into the summer, iu all parts of the country ; although of minor importance, most of them attrart exhibit.; from tbe lending workera. At the pre...TA time, in London, there is a very fine exhibit at the rooms of the Royal Photographic Society, by Rudolf Duhrkoop of Hamburg. This show is of special interest, as it emphasises the work of a man who was never oriizinally trained for professional photography, m but ho has come through to the very top of the pro- fessional by the strong artistic style that be has established for himself, and all those who have the opportunity should certainly pay this exhibition a visit. There is another exhibition, although a very small one, at the offices of "The British Journal of Photography," Wellington-street, Stand. At the present time, it is of more than usual interest because the subject of the exhibition is " Scenes and figures on the Coast near Messina," photographed by Count von Gloeden. I went to this exhibition expecting to find many photographs of the towns and buildings along the coast, which have recently suffered by the earth- quake, but I found that such was not the case. There are a few scenes portraying the beautiful coastline in the Messina district. Count von Gloeden is very clevcr with his work in the way he introduces nude figures of Sicilian boys in his landscapes. It is some- thing much out of the ordinary, and for those who appreciate artistic work, the figure studies, with their almost classical subjects will be found of especial interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>LACK OF BRILLIANCY.</head>
        <p>LACK OF BRILLIANCY.</p>
        <p>I was recently looking through many photographs that had been sent in for a competition, and one thing that struck me very forcibly was the lack of brilliancy in several instances of work which .was otherwise technically good. Now, what was the reason of this? Of course, there are many suggestions to make for this defect, such as fog in handling the plate in the dark- room, unsafe light, etc., but I am rather given to think that it is attributable to reflected light from the inside of the camera, and also to the use of a lens that has practically no lens hood. This type of lens is very prevalent at: the present day, especially amongst some of the best makes, and if any of my readers ara likewise troubled, let them try the effect of using an improvised hood to their lens, and I am sure they will find a wonderful improvement in the pureness of Om blacks of their pictures by so doing. For my own use have covered a narrow strip of thin cardboard with black velvet, which I wrap around the lens, fixing it to the lens with the aid of an india-rubber band, allowing it to project in the form of a hood to the extent of half an inch. One thing to guard against is that you put this band on the right way, so that the pile of the velvet does not reflect the light into the lens, but away from it. If you find you have reflection from the inside of your camera, owing to the dead-black varnish having worn away, or become shiny, this can be easily rectified by giving another coat of dead-black varnish. You can get this from dealers, or should you have any trouble you will find several formulm in " The Photographic Annual," 1909, under the "workroom" section.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>ATMOSPHERE.</head>
        <p>ATMOSPHERE.</p>
        <p>Of course, there are many times when you are taking photographs that clear blacks are not wanted. This is especially the case when you want to render some of those charming atmospheric effects that you get with the sun shining through the early morning mist, or after a shower of rain. When this mist is about, some photographers evidently lack the appreciation of such effects, as I was recently out with a friend of mine, and when this atmosphere was very much in evidence, he remarked, " I wish this mist would clear away so that I could use my camera," but I pointed out to him what flue effects are possible under these circumstances, and very much against his will got him to open his camera and expose a plate. lie was afterwards most surprised at the charming picture that be got.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>AUTOCHROMES.</head>
        <p>AUTOCHROMES.</p>
        <p>One of the finest collections that have hitherto been produced of colour photographs is now on view at the offices of The Lumiere Co., next to the British Museum in London. The collection comprises a wide range of subjects, from portraits to snow scenes. Many of these autochromes of Alpine scenery with lake scenes are really beautiful, and the LurniereCo., seem to have now reached perfection, by givina such faithful reproduc- tion of colour by photsgraphic means.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <p>PROFITABLE HOBBIES. BY H. SNOWDEN WARD.</p>
        <p>A USEFUL BOOK, GIVING IDEAS FOR THOSE ON THE LOOKOUT FOR SOME SORT OF HOBBY, WHICH WHILE PRO- VIDING ENTERTAINING HOME EMPLOY- MENT, CAN ALSO BE MADE TO PAY FOR ITSELF'. FULLY ILLUSTRATED. PRICE GD. NET. POSTAGE FREE, SD.</p>
        <p>DAWBARN &amp; WARD, LTD., 6, FARRINGDON AVENUE, LONDON, E.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>LORD BURNSIDE'S COMING OF AGE.</head>
        <p>LORD BURNSIDE'S COMING OF AGE.</p>
        <p>BY LADY VIOLET GREVILLE, Author of "Nurse Cicely's Renunciation," ".4, Sailor's Bride," "Mrs. Weston's Grandchild," "The Lost Letter," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>The village of Burnside buzzed with unusual excitement. Its inhabitants might be seen occupied in all kinds of frivolity, nailing up calico bands, fixing wreaths, and hanging tiny lamps warranted to twinkle like little glow-worms at night. Flags floated from every available vantage-ground, fluttered on trees and house-tops, and peered out of windows and chimney-pots. An atmosphere of agreeable idleness pervaded everyone. The old women stopped to gossip with their neighbours, the children huddled in groups, stared at the strange sights; young men shouldered ladders and exchanged observations, while their elders carefully scrutinised the clouds and the heavens, for was not Lord Burnside coining of age? Lord Burnside, whom they had all known since a child, who had been absent travelling for some years, and who, now erect, fair, manly and sunburnt, had come among them again. In reality, Lord Burnside was more than onc-and-twenty, but the rejoicings on his majority had been put off by special desire, Loth of himself and his parents. Neither wedding nor coming of age had occurred in the family for many years. Time had set a humdrum seal on-tho quiet parish. The aged could only remember, while the young looked forward to the future impatiently. And now, at last, something was going to happen. There would be much to see and to bear, while new and exciting incidents might surely be expected. The tenants and neighbours chose some really valuable presents, partly out of proper pride in their young lord, partly from a lively sense of gratitude for favours to come. A banquet was to be followed by a ball, and the ball by triumphal arches, illuminations and fireworks. • So the whole village assembled under a sense of holiday, and prepared with zest to enjoy the pleasures provided for it.</p>
        <p>The party at Burnside Castle consisted of the usual ingredients. Lord and Lady Strathallerton, the parents, a number of relations, a few grandees, and the lovely Lady Flora Arundel, who, since the return of young Lord Burnside, had been given to him, by public rumour, as his bride. Lady Flora was certainly intended to grace a peerage. She carried her head, crowned with gold-brown hair, high enough for a diadem ; her figure was slimly fa-hioned to carry robes and trains ; her large blue eyes were bright enough to dazzle men, her carriage infused with dignity and grace. Lord Burnside might often be seen talking to her at balls and parties, and the world, which sees what it wishes to see, pronounced it a good match. Yet Lord Burnside had never spoken Of love. He admired and liked her, and was flattered by her evident attention to him. His heart was still untouched, and no one knew this better than his little child- I hood's playmate, May Deere. She lived across ' the fields in the old parsonage house, with its wild garden (where the briar rises grew in untrained . luxuriance), and the quaint, old ghost-ridden garrets, in which o's two children, trembling and open-eyed had warxered hand-in-hand in the dim twilight, starting in a luxury of terror at every sound and shadow. Long years had elapsed since then, but to May, Lord Burnside was still her , childish companion, pla3-fellow and friend. "May," he said, on the first day they found themselves alone after his return, "it is nice to be home again. You haven't changed a bit. You're just the same queer, shy, bright little thing as , ever." "A</p>
        <p>1 you, Burnside, are unchanged, too.• "I'm older and wiser, and certainly not ha'f so mischievous. I couldn't. rob a nest or climb in at the kitchen window, or hide in musty garrets now." "But you would tell the trath, just. as you did the day we went fishing without leave, and father caught me with the rod, and you took all the blame on yourself." "Why, that was only my duty, May." "But people don't always do their duty," she answered, with a siv,h. • "Isn't it splendid the reception the people are going to give me?" he said, breaking off a rose and biting the end of the stalk. "I am sure I have never deserved it." "Perhaps we don't always get our desees ; other- wise some of us would fare badly." "Not you, May But now I must</p>
        <p>The:</p>
        <p>...ort you, T. go. want Ine back, tlicrc ia bl) i&amp;011 to arrange." May agreed with a sensation of regret, for it was very pleasant to her to have Lord Burnside beside her on the bench under the big beech tree and . among the roses. He stretched himself slowly-to his full height and looked down kindly on little May in her white gown and plain straw hat, with the ribbons of Eton blue, his colours. What a nice, confiding, innocent little girl she was! Then he went hack to Lady Flora, with her exquisite trailing toilets of Parisian make, and spent the " evening looking into her lovely face. The banquet was at an end. Lord Burnside hed gained all suffrages, even those of the stolid old and the critically young farmers, by his manly, I fluent, fearless speech. May, from her vantage- , ground in the gallery, comrared him to a young Adonis, standing there so straight and unembarrassed with all eyes directed upon him. The Lady Flora! looked also, and thought of him as the leader of the House of Commons' for she was ambitious as well as well-bred, and would never have been satidled with a mere figure-head of a husband. And now the applause ceased. The tables were cleared away ; the comp tny adjourned to the old oak gallery, where the ancestors and tapestry hung on the walls, and were reflected in the shining floor. The fiddles struck up gaily, the girls white dresses gleamed against the dark walls, orders and decorations sparkled on the men's breests, and Lord Burns Wei clasped Lady Flora in a long, luxurious, languor-giving valse. Lady Flora danced well. She was the bele of the ball, and as such I obtained a fair amount of homage. But her most frequent and best-loved ' dances were given to tho hero of the evening, and many a Moment was spent sitting out in shady nooks and behind leafy screens. May, with the keen eyes of affection, saw it all. She saw the smile rise to the lady's proud lips, and the look of satisfied elation in her eyes. She saw the frequency with which, notwithstanding the claims made on him by others, Lord Burnside eeturned to Lady Flora. She noted his keen ardour In talk, and the perfect harmony of the young couple's eteps. May was not jealous, she was too humble, but she sighed, for she knew sha loved him hest, loved him leen enough to resign him to another, provided that other loved him too. Of Lady Flora's affection she doubted. All that beauty and cleverness and worldly wisdom could give she had, but still May fancied she had not crept into that inner sanctuary of the heart where those we love nestle securely. All that evening May wattled, her soul in her eyes. After supper Lord Burnside claimed her hinself, and for a few moments she forgot all, except her happiness. Then they talked, and as they talked May thought she saw her partner look round as if in search of . someone. She rose instantly. "What! going May? Tired of me already ? " "It is not that—but--. You have so many other people to dance with," she faltered. • "But no one I like so well as you, May—my oldest, dearest little friend."</p>
        <p>The words warmed May's heart, which had suddenly grown cold ; but, Jae a prudent, girl, she would not listen to the insidious voice of pleasure. "I think we had better go back." "As you phase, hut, May, I wanted to talk to you so much, to ask yiur advice." May wavered a minute, then she composedly opened her fan and said: "To-morrow you can tell me what, you want to say." Lord Burnside looked disappointed, but he did rot insist, and the minutes afterwards he was twirling round with lady Flo a. By the end of the evening May had gone home, escorted by a maid and alantern, and wrapped rrosaically in a lar:e, white woollen filmy!.</p>
        <p>' The next day came the presentation. Lord Burnside strove to forget, girls, their ways, and their fascinations, and to fix his mind seriously on crop§ and cultivation, commerce, and the prospects f the country. Ile made two or three speeches, cairn, well thought out, and thoroughly national in tone. The audience eheere,i and bawled themselves hoarse. They liked Lord Strathallerton, his father, a kind landlord, whom they knew yell, inuntnsely ; but they liked his young and hand- 'some son, untried and unknown as he was, even better. Their enthusiasm waxed higher and higher. here was their own young lord, born and bred in the place, without any past of errors or mistakes, just a fair, fresh sheet, of paper, on which time would inscribe the characters they chose, ready to riierr hand, proud or their admiration, well Mtn a for Parliament. Allusions to his virtues grew more and more marked, compliments more flowing and fulsome, the whole hall became a mutual</p>
        <p>admiration society. Lady Strathallertrin, his mother, broke down and shed real tears, mid all tiie mothers in the place shed tears also. It was a truly touching, gratifying, wholesome, and heurt- stirring scene. And there above them, stood the young man, a trifle flushed with his efforts, but brave, eloquent, and kindly to the end; while in the corner Lady Flora smiled and took all the compliments as addressed to herself, but little May, hidden behind the doctor's portly wife, tried too, furtively, and in silence. The night was clear though dark. A myriad of stars glimmered in the sky, bonfires threw out flashing lights, lanterns quivered on the moor, and a rolling pillar of smoke swept across the valley. :In a triumphal procession the Party from the Castle drove through the village, the horses un- harnessed, the carriage drawn by a hundred willing, sturdy, perspiring youths amid the brazen clamour of bands, drums banging, fifes tootling, men shouting, preceded by a group of dancing Chinese lanterns; which swayed and bobbed up and down as they moved. Again and again Lord Burnside waved his cap, again and again the ladies fluttered their handkerchiefs, again cad again n halt vas made for more hearty cheering, more manly speeches, more grateful responses. Such a night had never been before in Burnside, such unanimity, such enthusiasm ; the humblest cotter lit his candle, the poorest contributed his mite, even the joys of beer were for the moment forgotten, as every parched throat bellowed out its sounds of acclamation.</p>
        <p>The .bonfire rEllied, picturesqueness increased. Sudden irradiations of lurid light fell on the spectators, outlining features with startling brilliancy, plunging all again immediately into darkness. Strange shapes hovered about, neigh- bours hardly recognisinc, each other. Lord Burn- side's pure profile stood out against the gloom, till be resembled some ancient warrior saint. Bang! Fizz! the fireworks began. It was the people's homage to their lord, their raptnrous'ideal of pleasure. Unfortunately, the man to whom they confided the business watered it. The first few rockets fizzled and `inizzled away into impotent smoke. Groans of execration arose from the crowd. Lord Burnside ran nearer and shouted directions. "Keep back, my lord, keep back." The warning rang unheeded. A magnificent rocket flolted upwards, burst, and fell gracefully in a shower of stars. Another and yet another followed. Things were mending. All eyes were directed upward watching the dazzling gleams of light as they faded into the darkness. Then May observed Lord Burn- side sway and fall heavily to, the ground. She flew to him and rested his head on her lap; others grasped the situation and offered help, till quite a little group collected. A kind of blank despair seized on everyone. What had happened, was he killed Why did he lie there silent and motion-. less? The proceedings came to an untimely end, the last rockets were fired off to an unheeding audience. Lord Burnside was carried home, and it then transpired that he had been struck by the stick of one of the rockets, and that he lay in a critical condition. The party at the Castle broke up unceremoniously, and prepared at once for departure, wagering like a covey of partridges; only May remained, rale and trembling in the ante-chamber, waiting for news. ."You Must go home," said her father. "I cannot, I cannot. Oh, let me stay just till I hear how he is," pleaded May. The Vicar stayed, for no one knew when his services might be needed, and he felt no objection to his daughter's company, remembering how old was her intimacy ,with the young heir. May's secret. was safe, no one noticed her, no one sus- pected, all the sympathy centred on Lady Flora, who had left the house with the rest of the ecmpany. "Poor young thing," people armed, "how terrible for her, and just on the eve of a proposal too, for, of course, next day the marriage would. have been announced, and yet now she would not even dare shew the feelings of a brisk.. Some considered that reticence under the circum- stances was unnecessary, and that she might have claimed her place beside him ; others, on the contrary, applauded her dignity end decorum, but no one heeded May. In the ccld dawn of morning the vicar returned to his house, and Mafflung her- self crt her bed, with a Cull, fixed pain at her heart.</p>
        <p>The doctor a latest Witte in said that the stick of the rocket had inflicted a violent blow on the eyes and brow, and that concussion, with much pain and inflammation, were the reeult. At present it was impossible to ray how serious the injuries might be. With this poor comfort Lord Burnside's friends were forced to be content. Day by day May and her father waited for news, and day )4 day the answer was the same. May's eyes grew large and sombre, and her cheeks whitened and fell away. The suspense was terrible ' • she would a most rather have known the worst. She wondered how Lady Flora felt, and whether she too endured Lhcac regonio• f ao,spni, _lt wr,. hardl, ha a woman, and forced to be silent where one loved. The village, a few days ago so gay and bright, was now wrapped in silence. People went up and down, and spoke almost under their breath. Would the young heir live or die? Was he to be cut off in his prime thus prematurely, or would Providence be merciful and answer their prayers. Everyone prayed, the vicar in the church prayed, the early congregation prayed. May prayed alone in the silence of her chamber wrestling on her knees for his life. The tension grew almost unbearable. Soon there must be an answer of some kind. At last the answer came. Lord Burnside was con- valescent, but he was blind! A veil had been drawn over his eyes, and never again would he look forth with that confident clear gut) of his upon the beautiful world.</p>
        <p>'His parents tried to rejoice, for their son was spared to them. But in church on Sunday Lady Strathallerton, still young and handsome, with the slight sprinkling of grey in her hair, wiped away furtive tears behind her Prayer Book; the vicar's voice trembled as he gave out the text: "He bath made the blind to see," and tried to give it a spiritual meaning; and the military erectness of Lord Strathallerton's figure shelved the firm repres- sion of paternal feeling. May was dazed at the news. Burnside—her phydellow, the bright ideal of manhood that had flashed like a meteor across bar humble path—blind! It was inconceivable, mon.trous I</p>
        <p>And yet it was true. Slowly Lord Burnside learnt to pace along the terrace, to snitT the scent of the flowers in the morning hour, and to turn his sightless eyes towards the sunset. He looked hand- somer than ever, pale and interesting ; but he was helpless, glad to lean on a stalwart arm or grasp some friendly hand stretched out to save him from an ugly fall. By degrees, he tried to pick up the threads of his life, to interest himself in business, in politics, in the affairs of the neighbourhood. But it was all so new, so strange ; so many things, simple and easy as they had seemed formerly, had to be learnt, so many things forgotten. September and the partridges arrived. The sound of shooting reached his ears ; but not for him the tramp across the wet turnips, or the crisp ping of the gun. A few friends came and went, killed the birds, talked to him,cheerily, read the papers, and discussed the latest news. But when their pleasant talk had died away, when they had gone, with portmanteaux and gun-cases, to merrier scenes, he felt more alone than ever. They only came out of pity, and they were glad to go. Solitude, that was his fate for ever; and in youth, loneliness seems the one unendurable fate.</p>
        <p>Then he remembered Lady Flora. She had loved 'him, he was sure. How her eyes had softened as they looked at him, how sweetly she smiled, how splendidly their steps had united. He remembered that last entrancing valse, how she had returned the pressure of his hand, and how the unspoken words of love had trembled on his lips. If he had only (I t'med her then, she would be his now, his own, his bire, his dear, daily companion. lie thought and thought until the longing to have her near him (never to see her again—good Heavens ! the idea was terrible!) ; still, he would hear her voice, feel her caresses—for she loved him : he knew that.; it was through his own remissness she was not beside him already. The kindest of messages had come from Lady Flora, her inquiries were constant, so his parents assured him. The delay must be remedied at once, but how ? lie could not write—then the full horror of his position dawned on him, and he wept fee'dy like a child. Someone must write for him. But who? lie could not dictate a love-letter to his parents, ,to his servant, not even to a male friend. His most secret thoughts, the tenderness of a -nan's love could only be written by a sympathetic soul—someone young enough to understand and too affectionate to cavil.</p>
        <p>Then he remembered . May. May would under- stand, sympathise, and do as she was told without questioning. He sent for May, who came dressed in her white frock, looking as sweet and as innocent as ever, with the faint blush of pleasure and expectation on her cheeks, as pink as the blossom whose naive she bore. Perhaps if he could hare seen her, he might not have set her such a task. But his eyes were closed, and his thoughts clung to another.</p>
        <p>"I am so glad you are better," May faltered. "Better, yes, but blind. Can you realise it, May? Never to fee anything again, neither sky, nor sea, nor friend's face--"</p>
        <p>It is terrible," si,e said.</p>
        <p>"My friends must be everything to me now. I hare sent for you, because you're such a sym• pathetic little soul, and will understand."</p>
        <p>- "What can I cio:;?" May suddenly feared some dreadful calamity. "I want you to write a letter for me to. a friend —to Lady Flora," he added, with a constrained laugh. _</p>
        <p>May drew paper and pen towards her. She knew now what it was she feared.</p>
        <p>"I --you know, perhaps, we were almost engaged the night of my accident. I put off speaking, but I meant to speak, because I care for her, and want her f( r my wife."</p>
        <p>"I see," said May, quietly. "Will she have me now, now that I am a poor helpless creature, and can never see her bonny face again—will she? What do you think, May? "If she loves you she will," said May, firmly. .„..</p>
        <p>"Then write."'</p>
        <p>Lord Burnside dictated a page or two of manly love, modest, earnest and sincere, imploring Lady Flora to overlook his infirmities and accept the unswerving devotion of his life. May copied it all out in her best hand, wrote it firmly without a blot, then coaled and sent it. Lord Burnside patted her hand gratefully. "Thank you, May, thank you, you've been a good little friend to me and I shall never forget it..." _</p>
        <p>"-If only I could do anything for you, anything really great, 1:--"</p>
        <p>TGeiV her voice broke and she stopped. But Lord rurnside, who had noticed nothing, and only thought how kind and helpful she was, finished the. sentence for I.er.</p>
        <p>"You have done great things to-day, for to your. good offices I hope I shall owe my wife." "You will let me know? "May managed to say,. fairly calmly. "Yott,shall be the very tirst to hear the news." Then May crept. back to the Vicarage and spent ts. solitary and miserable afternoon.</p>
        <p>so selfish," she moaned, sobbing, "ea, selfish. I ought to be glad—glad if anything can make him happy, and yet—" Poor May's con- science was Tery tender, and it pricked her sorely. Silence for some days, then a message from 'lord Burnside: "Come at once."</p>
        <p>May hurried to the Castle, fearing yet longing to. hear the great news. She was to be the first to, know, he said, and now the moment had come. She found Lord Burnside lying back in a chair on, the terrace in the warm September sun. He looked. paler and thinner than when last they met, and his; face bore a pinched and weary expression. "May," he said, as her light footsteps approached, "come at last ! I wanted you, May, and I knew that you would not fail." • "Have you heard? Is it-" "Read." Lord Burnside held out a letter. "She won't have me. l'm not good enough fur - her. It was all a mistake; she never cared. The letter was beautifully written on smart coronet ted paper. The writer's hand had not trembled. There was neither blot nor erasure. It was evident she had scarcely been moved at all. La•ly Flora wrote, civilly but coldly, that she, thanked Lord Burnside for his kind opinion of her, that had he propose] at the time of his coming of. age she would probably have thought it her duty to accept him, but seeing he had delayed his declara- tion till after his accident she could not feel sure of his love, nor certain that her feelings of pity and regard were strong enough to carry her through, what trust now te a difficult and trying position as nurse and companion to a blind man. "Oh, it, is cruel, cruel! She must be absolutely heartless," cried May, dropping the letter on the grass as though it were some stinging insect. "How can she write sn ? "</p>
        <p>"She does not care for me, that is all. No one, will erer wish to marry a blind man. You see, it was presumptuous of me eYer to think so. Well, there's another illusion gone. They are all drepping from me now"; and Lord Burnside smiled sadly.</p>
        <p>"Oh, no, you must not believe it. Lady Flora mny not care, but there are others. You are so noble,. so patient. Oh, let me help you." "You dear child. You are good to me, indeed, but you will marry, too, little May, some day, and then you will forget me." "Never!" May's voice vibrated with tho - vehemence of her denial.</p>
        <p>"You don't mean what you say. I know now; many things are clear to me. I realise •I am cnly a man, no longer Lord Burnside, the fortunato heir with all the world before him. I shall never be loved for myself now. It was selfish of me even. to wish that a woman should share my sad fate, cut off from pleasnres, different from anyone else, looking inwards only." "But lookinn, inward f1u,...) fo pcnce." "No, only regiet, bitterness and solitude." May sighed. They stun moment in silence. "How flimsy life is," said Lord Burnside presently. "It is all externals, even love. Love• only thinks of one's surroundings." "Then it is not love," answered May, stoutly. "Horribly crippled, disgraced, aloof for ever and ever." He felt for May's hand. "Except you, dear, you're different. Why are you different? You do not shrink from me?"</p>
        <p>"Can't you guess?" "No—at least— 0 May, don't let me make another mistake—be presumptuous again. Help tie. dear, I'm groping for the truth." "You've found it," said May, gently. "Have I? May, ttly dearest, how pitiful you: arc IP</p>
        <p>"It is not pity, it's love," whispered the girl ate Lord Burnside drew her towards him.</p>
        <p>The coming of age festivitiesw;Wresumed after a while, but this time they ended with a wedding. [Tina END. 1 •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>DANGER OF BRONCHIAL COUGHS:</head>
        <p>DANGER OF BRONCHIAL COUGHS:</p>
        <p>Peps Reach the Root of the Trouble.</p>
        <p>All bronchial troubles are serious because they lie so near the vital organs. Generally it is the common bronchial cold with its nasty cough and " made-up " feeling that, neglected fur drys, lays one• up at last with pleurisy or pneumonia. The introduction of the Peps treatment lals rendered obsolute the ever-satisfactory method of trying to cure throat and lung troubles by means of liqu d medicine.. Not only do these old-fashioned cough-mixtures frequently contain dangerous drugs like opium, morphia, chloral, but, being liquid, they are merely swallowed into the stomach and cannot, therefore, even touch the lungs where the trouble lies. Herein are the two slistinguishing points about Peps. First, they do net contain any trace of opitun,laudnum, or any other danger( us drugs occUring in old- fashioned cough medicines; sec-nd, they are a breathe-able remedy. That is, as a Peps tablet,. removed front its preserving silver jacket, is made to dissolve on the tongue, certain valuable fumes are given off which, mixing with the air we breathe, ate• at once taken down the windpipe and passed straight into the innermost recesses of the lungs and chest. The soothing effect of these Peps balsams on the throat and bronchi is marvellous and brings act merely strength to the chest, but by repairing the, delicate membrane torn by constant coughing, fortifies the windpipe a,..ainst cold-germs " and against further attacks of di,_ease.</p>
        <p>Beci.u.,:e of ti eir absolt.te freedom from narcotics, Pei s can be freely used by young and old; and because of their uniquem as, co venience and unequalled efficiency, these w ndeiful breathe-able tablets should be kept alw ,ys hanOy to Iv,- r.l (IT coughs, colds,_ hr nchi:is, sore throat, hoarseness, wheeziness, &amp;e. Of all chemists at Is. I,ld. or 2s. 9.1. a box. Avoid imitations.</p>
