        <p>DOL</p>
        <p>COMMOTION AT THE POLICE STATION.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court, on Monday, Kate Loyden, of Ellesmere-street, was charged with using obscene language in A rpley-street at a quarter to nine the previous night. P.C. nennett stated thethe was in the charge office at the Police Station when the prisoner came to the outer doors, and shouted "You can't look a man up without lynching him," also using strong adjectives. She was referring to a man who had been taken into custody a few hours before. Witness went to her, she threw a whisky bottle at him, saying "1 f you don't lock me up I'll break every window in the place." Dennett there- upon arrested her and placed her in the cells, where she made a commotion all the night through, and thus prevented the other prisoners from sleeping. Witness also had to remove prisoner's clogs to prevent her from breaking the cell windows, which she had threatened to do. .</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable stated that when a man named who was in the Court as a prisoner for steal- ing, was locked up, the female prisoner always did some- thing in order to be locked up also. It had happened on several occasion. Prisoners' father said he would be very thankful if the bench could seod her away to a home. He could do ,no good with her, she had a good home but she would not stay. She was 21 years of age. When asked by the clerk if she would consent to go to a home prisoner replied," No Sir, I will not go to a home."</p>
        <p>Mr. Firth, police court missionary, said the prisoner would have to consent to remain six months before she -would be eligible for a home. The bench decided to bind prisoner over for six -months, and to find two sureties of ES each, or in -default six months imprisonment with hard labour. Prisoner: Thank you, sir.</p>
