      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>33 YEARS' LIVER DISORDER.</head>
        <p>33 YEARS' LIVER DISORDER.</p>
        <p>Neuralgia, Biliousness, &amp; Nasty Taste. -- lady Crateful to Chas. Forde's Bile Beans.</p>
        <p>For .thirty years Mrs. Ann Elizabeth Worthington, of Sefton Villa, Little Haywood, Stafford, was made 'miserable by a disordered liver. " I was reduced to a state of great weakness by my continued illnesses," she told a Stafford pressman, "I suffered almost continuously, having very few periods of ease. My life was often a real misery, and at times I was so irritable and depressed that I felt I could quarrel with my own shadow. It was -exceedingly difficult for me to do my work, for I was frequently so ill that I could hardly crawl about the house.</p>
        <p>"There was a nasty taste in my mouth, and I was ,oppressed by attacks of languor and flatulence. Dreadful bilious attacks used to come on about every month, and I had severe cutting pains of neuralgia in the head. I also suffered terribly from spells of dizzi- mess, and saw specks were floating in front of my eyes. _</p>
        <p>Constipation was annther very trying symptom of my complaint, and I also suffered from pains between the shoulders. During the long years I suffered I was voniinually trying medicines, all of which either made me feel worse or took no effect at all. I cannot say that I derived benefit from any of the many prepara- tions I took, and naturally I lost hope and came to regard myself as incurable.</p>
        <p>"But a.hout twelve months ago I was persuaded to try Chas. FORDE*S Bile Bean's, and a wonderful improvement was soon brought abant in my geneial health I felt better in every way,and as I continued to 'take Chas. FORDES Bile leans I found I could do my work again, and my appetite and strength returned. " Chas. Forde's bile Beans removed every symptom of my complaint, and now I feel healthier, stronger and more energetic than I have done for a very long -time. Chas. Forde's famous medicine has worked a great change in me, and I am grateful for my splendid cure."</p>
        <p>Por purifying the blood, stomach, bowels 'and liver. and restoring the healthy functions .01 the body, Chas. PORDE'S Bile Beans have earned the highest reputation- Of all chemists in sealed wooden boves,l/1; and 2/9. Avoid worthless, cheap imitations,sold loose or in pennyworths. These things can only 'make your condition worse, so be sure to see '•eII7IRLF3S RORDIPS" on the label before Putting Your money down.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>TEMPT OF MEAT.</head>
        <p>TEMPT OF MEAT.</p>
        <p>James :Jackson, and John Silas Thompson, labourers. -both'residing at 61, Pierpoint-street, were charged at Me Borough Police Court on Mond tv, with stealinq from a basket, a shoulder of lamb, tiro mutton chops and two beef steaks, the property a R. C. Fletcher, butcher, Iforsemarket street—The Chief Constable stated that on Siturday evening abont 7.110 William Brown, whose father is the manager of Mr. Fletcher's shop left his bicycle outside No. 14, Pierpoint-street and went inside the house to obtain a match in order to re-light his cycle lamp which had gone out. While the lad was inside the two prisoners were seen by a little girl to take the meat from a basket that was attached to the front of the machine and decamp. They were afterwards apprehended, and admitted having stolen the meat.—Jackson, who had been before the Court on eight previous occasions was sentenced to 'one ealemier month with hard labour, awl Thompson. 'who is an army reservist, was fined in the costs of the ease.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>STRENGTH</head>
        <p>STRENGTH</p>
        <p>COMES FROM</p>
        <p>WELL-DIGESTE9 FOOD.</p>
        <p>The power to think well, work well, sleep well, and .enjoy life depends mainly upon the ability of your digestive organs to extract strength and nourishment from food. When digestion fails, as in dyspepsia and indigestion, the body is starved, no matter how much food is eaten. It is also poisoned, for food as eaten remaining in the stomach ferments, producing poisonous gases, which, being absorbed, into the blood, shatter the nerves, dull the brain, create disease, and give rise to headaches, languor, loss of appetite, -Palpitation, flatulence, and other disorders of the blood and nerves. When'the stomach, liver and kidneys fail to per- form their functions perfectly, there is no remedy that will so soon restore them to health and vigour as Mother Seigel's Syrup. As a digestive tonic and stomachic remedy it has no equal. This is the testi- mony of thousands. Mrs. E. Wells, of Salhouse, near Norwich, writing 00 September 10th, 1908, says :—" In the early part cf the year, after a violent attack of influenza, I found that my digestive powers, hitherto good, were quite upset, caning me great pain after eating. This was il..eulm.panied by windy pressure about my heart. the value of Nfother Seigel's Syrup,l resorted 4.0 !t at once with most beneficial effects. A few doses .10u:wed me, and after a short course of it all traces of I.9.digestioo left me. In addition, my general health distinctly improved."</p>
        <p>Mother Seigel's Syrup is also prepared in Tablet form as Mother SeigerS Syrup Tablets. Price 2/9.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>CHARGES AGAINST A DIRECTOR.</head>
        <p>THE CHARGES AGAINST A DIRECTOR.</p>
        <p>MR. HENSHALL AGAIN COMMITTED FOR TRIAL</p>
        <p>More Charges Pending.</p>
        <p>B'AIL REFUSED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Louis Henshall, Runnymede, Stoclann Heath, director and departmental manager of Messrs. R. A. Nhylor, Limited, timber merchants, Howley, who had already been committed to the Liverpool Assizes on charges of forging inVoices, again appeared at the 80r0t4.-.1 Police Court on Saturday to answer further allegatinns. The magistrates on the Bench were Mr. J. W. Hayward (in the chair), Aldermen Bolton and Sinethurst,', Councillors Evans and Wilkinson, Dr. Markel, Messrs. IL Roberts, W. Furness, R. T. Fairclough, A. J. Jolley, W. Roberts, C. Jackson Holmes and 11. Thornton.</p>
        <p>The first Charge dealt with was that of having as a director of R. A. Naylor, Limited, unlawfully and fraudulently taken for his own use and benefit the following sums belonging to the company : —l4th November, 190 S, .100; 18th November, £100; 12th November, £79 7s. Mr. Gordon llewart (instructed by Messrs. Tatham, Worthington and Co., Manchester) again prosecuted and Mr. Rigby Swift (instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel) appeared for Hr. Ilenshall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>ALLEGED APPROPRIATION OF NOTES.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED APPROPRIATION OF NOTES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hewart stated that the three charges now brought against the prisoner were that as a director of R. A. Naylor, Ltd., he fraudulently applied for his own use and benefit certain moneys of the company. Two cases related to the sale of scrap metal to Mr. Charles Starkey, a Warrington metal broker. On November 14th last year Mr. Starkey paid to the prisoner on behalf of Messrs. R. A Naylor, Ltd., a sum of 1100 on account of moneys which Mr. Starkey owed to the company for scrap metal he had pur- chased. On November 18th Mr. Starkey paid to the prisoner a further sum of £lOO. These two sums were put in the prisoner's own pocket. In order to trace one of the transactions, he (Mr. Hewart) would call officials. Ir,m local banks, who would prove that on November 14th, a certain 1,100 note was handed to Mr. Starkey from the Union Bank of Manchester. Ur. Starkey gave it to the prisoner before eleven o'clock, and before twelve o'clock that day prisoner had paid it into his o*n bankiug account. The third charge —which came before the magistrates first— related to the 1,79 7s. which was paid by M. Thomas Hewitt, builder, Warrington, on account of moneys which he owed to the company. The money was paid to the prisoner, who appropriated it to his own tve Frederick Gandy, cashier at the Warrington branch of the Union Bank of Manchester, Ltd., produced the bank note register showing that a note for £l.OO was paid to Mr. Alfred Starkey on November 14th, 1903. The number of the note was 00294 Manchester, Ist April 1907.</p>
        <p>-Charles Starkey, metal broker, Cockhedge. Warrington, stated that in the early part of November 1908, be went to the premises of Messrs. Naylor. Ltd., to pur- chase a quantity of scrap iron. On Thursday,November 10th be saw the prisoner at the office of the firm at 5.45, and made him an offer of 50s. per ton for the scrap iron, £.4 per ton for shafting, £4 per ton for spilt pulleys, £ll per ton for lead, sd. per lb. for brass, and GJ, per lb. for copper. Witness told prisoner he could not remove the scrap for a few days and that he would give him £lOO on Saturday morning. Mr. llenshall accepted the offer, remarking that witness could start art soon as he liked when he had given him the 2100. On Saturday morning, November 140, witness got a bank note for .£lOO from the Union Bank of Manchester and at 10.43 he banded it to the prisoner who gave him a receipt signed "R. A. Naylor, Ltd.,*L. II." On the following Monday afternoon witness began to move the scrap and on Wednesday November nth he paid the prisoner another note for £lOO and obtained another receipt signed in the same way. By Mr. Swift: Witness bought a large quantity of materials from the firm and the payments of £lOO were on account. It would not be known until he had cleared away all the scrap, what was the full amount he owed. He had paid altogether £293. Wheti he paid the second £lOO Mr. Hensball said: "We will have a settling up when you have cleared away all the stuff." He did not remark that witness appeared to be in a ereat hurry to pay. Witness never had an invoice until he had paid the £2OO. There was a s4le note which witness's partner signed. Besides Mr Henshall, he saw a good many people at Messrs. Naylor's, including the engineer, Mr. Challener. He carted the stuff away in broad daylight, and everybody knew he was taking it away. It was done quite openly. Ile saw Mr. Anderton Naylor once or twice whilst he was on the job:</p>
        <p>Charles Albert Littler, clerk at Parr's Bank, Warring- ton, said he attended the Court on subpcena. On referring to his book he found that a note of £lOO was paid into the prisoner's account at the Bank on November 14th. The number was 25v 03294, issued Manchester, Ist April, 1907. Ho had taken from the books a copy of the prisoner's banking account which he now produced. The entries in the account were made in the ordinary books of the bank in the usual course of business. lie had compared the copy with the original entries and it was correct. The 11th November was a Saturday and the Bank closed at 12 noon. By Mr. Swift: In November last prisoner was entitled to overdraw his account to the extent of close upon £4OOO. At that time he was in fact overdriwn to the amount of about £1,500. As he wos entitled to another £5OO, for practical purposes he would have about £5OO cash in the Bank.</p>
        <p>Mr. EL. Roberts : lie is entitled to overdraw up to the amount of his guarantee, but it does not stand as cash. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift: For the practical purpose of getting hold of £5OO cash it was as good to Mr. Ifenshall as if it were actually cash. Mr. Roberts : But your description is not accurate. The Clerk: The learned counsel asked the witness and he agreed with him. Witness : I did not understand it in that sense. Mr. Swift: For practical purposes was it as good to Mr. Ilenshall as £5OO cash in the bank ? Witness : I say " No." Mr. Swift: Would you have honoured a cheque up to £5OO if Mr. Henshall on the morning of November 14th had drawn one for that amount ?—Yes. Mr. Swift was asking further questions on the point, when Mr. Hewart interposed with the suggestion that the cross-examination was hardly relevant. Mr. Swift was seeking to get the witness to say that it was exactly the same thing for a man to have 590 sovereigns to his credit in the hank as to have £5OO left between the overdraft he had got and the overdraft to which at the extreme limit he might be entitled. Mr. Swift : The witness has never been asked to say anything of the sort. In each question I have put to him I have been most ca;eful to say "from a practical point of view." Thomas:He 3 111, builder, Hewitt-street, \Carrington, stated that for some time be had done business with Messrs. R. A. Naylor, Ltd. In November of last year the firm had accounts against him for materials supplied and work done to the amount of over £lO9. On November 12th he went to the offices of the flrm and made a payment to Mr. Henshall of £79 7s. That was the amount due on his July and August accounts, less discount. Mr. Henshall gave him the receipt produced which bore is initials. Witness paid the amount in notes the money (£7O) and cash. He believed there was a R. 50 made note and two £lO notes. By Mr. Swift • . moneythteo pa;•Tmr. Hentaebosoilltatiinotohne. When he paid cashier Nees not present Ile had very oftenßOX paid money . nd got receipts. SECRETARY IN THE CR OSS-EXANI I NATION. to Mr. Henshall ED John Edwards,Nl. The Hollies Ljrnm, director, secretary and o cashier received tfieit.offie Ai Ltd., stated that ought alestso be o paid A Ltd. on account of the firm on Naylor,,to iru no ratter who received themd body. Sometimes they recpiP tezector, clerk, or any full particulars—and at ethers the invoice when it receipt book, which was always used the duplicate investigations which led to e accessible. Until the , the two sums of £lOO paid byMPr roStarkey was paid or ! offered to the company. After the Investigations had ! begun he received a letter from the prisoner enclosing a ' cheque both, of which were now produced. The letter read as follows :- 1 "Dear Mr.Edwards,—lt was the farthest thought from my mind to misuse the £2OO from Starkeliv, though possibly things bear that construction. In this had reported to everybody that the sale was made,case oquite casually I used the notes,and you will find on my desk the weight notes, and summaries, and when the whole deliveries were made op the total %could have been paid in. I reported receiving Hewitt's t 79 7s. to clear up to a point; and enclosed please find cheque to cover both items.—Please oblige me by not sending any letters home. Find some other way of cotumuni- eating with me.—Yours truly, L. Henshall. Witness, continuing, said the company did not accept the cheque, and on December 2nd, another letter was received from Mr. Henshall, enclosing two separate cheques for £2OO and £77 Os. . " Deer Sir,—Adverting to the cheque for £277 os. which you would not use, you may be right in regard to the £2OO, but the payment by Hewitt is yours, and it .is only fair you should have the money to use, and I venture to send you two separate cheques, so that you can use the collected account. Should you decide upon doing this, yen will oblige nle by returning the first cheque to Chester Post Office. "The Chairman, R. A. Naylor, Ltd."</p>
        <p>Witness said the company had not presented for pay- ment any of the cheques. Another letter was received from the prisoner, as follows, dated December 15th, from Stockton Heath :- 1 " I find the enclosed memo, dealing with the sale of I scrap, &amp;c. Possibly you have cleared the transaction. The terms were pash. There are usually a few Christmas clearances to friends. Would you like rue to give particulars ?—Your faithfully. L. HENSHALL." A further letter read as follows : "Dear Sir,—Mr. T. G.Brooks sends me the enclosed, informing me that you are making inquiries with regard to his account with us. In respect to this or any other accounts in qaestion,l shall comfort my mind by giving your committee any information, and if you were pleased to let me go through the books with you I think I could point out tie items so madly displaced. lam miserable in mind and body. The enclosed repre- sents the mortgage I arranged for Mr. Brooks. I think lest November the works were pressing him for money. He had his house free,the ESS I lent him,and it passed to the office. Likewise the £l2O came through Mr. Steel about September 10th, and I think the amoune was paid in. Brooks says he has no receipt for this amount. The balance of his account I thiak depends upon a settlement by a Mr. Barton, for whom he was building. Yours truly, L. Ifenshall. I don't ask for any reply, but I shall call at Chester Post Office on Saturday to see if there are any letters." Continuing, witness said he bad the ledger of IL A. Naylor, Ltd. On October 26th, 1908, the amount of indebtedness of Mr. Hewitt to the firm was £205 10s. 4J. The amount of his account for July and August was .£Bl 7s. 3d. The company did not receive any money from Mr. Hewitt in respect of another of that sum. By MnSe lit: There was a note in the ledger regarding the £Bl acciunt to the effect: "Paid to Mr. Flenshall ." It was written by Maurice Owen, who told him he made the note when Mr. Fienshall was suspended. Mr. Henshall had been to the office since he was sus- pended. Between 12th November and the end of November witness saw the entry of Hewitt's account in the ledger, but as he knew Mr. Henshall had all to do with the account, witness did not g i into the details. Witness first saw the note referred to in the ledger after prisoner's suspension on November 25th. Maurice Owen was a book-keeper, and it was his duty to enter up in the. ledger payments made by Mr. Hewitt. The moneys received were entered into the cash book and posted in the ledger on witness's instructions on the same day. The entries in the cash book were made by witness. Mr. Swift: Has it not sometimes happened that Mr. /Umbell bad received payments from customers and paid them into his own bank, afterwards paying the firm with his own cheque?—l don't know of such an instance. Have you known of Mr. Ffenshall paying any customer's account with his own cheque ?—No. Have you had any cheques from Mr. Ifenshall ?—I have had cheques, presumably where he has been financing them. I don't want presumption, I want knowledge. Have you received chequas from Mr. flenshall to p.ty accounts owing by other persons to Naylor's ?—Where he has accepted the responsibility for the accounts. You mean where he has guaranteed the accounts?—</p>
        <p>_ LIE ve you had other cheques where he has not guaranteed them ?—Not that I remember. I suggest that it has occured more than once, when Mr. lienshall has reaeived cash on account from customers he has taken the cash as it came in and has subsequently paid the firm the total amount ?—Never to my knowledge. Mr. Starkey paid two separate sums of £lOO. The contract ultimately amounted to nearly £3OO. Do you know that Mr. Ile:Isbell took those two £lOO notes to hold them or use them, intending to hand them over to the company when the matter was finally settled ?—I don't know about his intentions.</p>
        <p>Did you hear anything about them ?—When be was suspended, not before. As soon as he was suspended ho told you about them himself ?—Yes.</p>
        <p>And he alsoiold you about Hewitt ?—No, he has not spoken to me about Hewitt. Was it at the meeting at which be was suspended that the matter Of Starkey was mentioned ?—Yes. • Who was in the chair 2—Mr. John Naylor.</p>
        <p>T dare say be will know more about it. than what you will ?—No. _ _</p>
        <p>Will he—know whether the Fuspension male before the investigation into Starkey's matter or after ?—lt was part and parcel of the same thing. _</p>
        <p>Which came first ?—The accusltion:</p>
        <p>Had the accusation with regard to Stark./y been made before the punishment of suspension ?—The suspension followed as part of it. Who moved the resolution ?—The Chairman, Mr. John Naylor. Had there been R discussion about what John Naylor owed the company ?—No, None ? —No.</p>
        <p>At no meeting ?—Not st that meeting. Had there been at a meeting before ?—I cannot say, from memory. Surely you can remember whether at a Board meet- ing there was a discussion as to what the Chairman of directors owed the company ?—I don't see what that has got to with toe present case. Fortunately for everyone, I think, you are not the judge of that m ttter. Will you kindly answer the the question as truthfully as you can : Had there been a discussion at the Board meetings—at any meeting within six months before—as to what John Naylor was owing the compano-?—lchad been mentioned.-- - lied Mr. lienshall takett a very prominent part in denouncing John Naylor for what be owed Lb" com- pany ?—No. Had he denounced him ?—No. Had he said be was going to put his foot down and that this matter should not go on like this ?—No. Had he complained of what John Naylor was doing? —Ho complained of the amount owing by Mr. John Naylor, and Mr. John Naylor paid it. - When ?—ln October. How much did he pay last October ?—Am I required to answer that?</p>
        <p>Yes—He paid £1,252. In cash ?—lres, he paid in cash. Ily chelue?—No. What sort of cash, gold ?—I c3uld have had it by cheque. Did he pay in gold ?—No. Did he pay in notes ?—I don't know how it was paid. Oh yes, you do, it was paid to von. How was this £1,250 paid by John Naylor last o..:tober to the com- pany ?—lt was placed to our credit. Where ?—lle paid it at Parr's Bank. I receive a credit note from Parr's Bank to that effect. For £1,252 ?--I am not quite sure whether that is the correct amount.</p>
        <p>You have sworn it was. Mr. 11. Roberts : Ile might have told us a little earlier, I think. Mr. Swift : He might have told us a great deal earlier. Was &amp;edit ever given for £1,252 by John Naylor? You know Parr's Bank books will show it. Be careful what you say. How much was he owing ?-- £1,252. Our cash book will show the exact amount. Is that ail ?—That is all. \Vas he not owing a very great deal more than .£1,252 ? There was an amount due to him and the balance was R 1,252. How many accounts had John Naylor got with this company ?—One account. Was there an account of Unsworth ?—I don't know. Was there any account of Woodward ?—I don't know. Was there an account of Armstrong ?—There was. Who was Armstrong ?—Armstrong was Mr. John Naylor.</p>
        <p>Is Mr. John Naylor here to-day P—l don't know wlwther be is here now. lie was here the other day. Did Mr. Heashall object to John Naylor having two Of Mee accounts with the company P—No, I don't know that be did.</p>
        <p>Don't you know perfectly well that the whole cause of this trouble, the whole cause of the suspension and the vole reason for the prosecution is that Nlr. Henshall said he would not let John Naylor go on in the way he had been going on ?—No, I don't know any such thing. But Henshall never was suspected and his con- duct never was inquired into until ho attacked John Naylor. Did he wAnt the bank guarantee reducing ? I Yes. Ile was liable on that to how much ?—I don't , know that the extent of his liability was, 1 I thought you were the cashier as well as being a director of the company. I suppose you were brought here because you know least about it weren't vou?—No. sunpose you will agree that John Naylor knows most ?—No. Doesn't-he know more than you ?—No. am not surprised at the Bank overdraft getting pretty big. (Laughter.) Was not Mr. flenshall clamourin,, throughout October and November to have John Naylor's credit stopped and to have the Bank draft reduced ?—Mr. John Naylor's credit was stopped except in the case of one account. When did you stop his credit ?—Tt was not a question of stopping it. Ile did not require it. He no longer kept on these bogus accounts ?—They are not bogus. Mr. Ilewart : My friend has no right to use that, word. Mr. Swift : Well, what is the right word ? I am quite open to being corrected. What is the right word</p>
