Mr. John Naylor and his counsel. would not. Mr. flewart : The witness, when his attention was Further questioned, witness said there were six directed to the matter by one of the justices, said the directors present at the meeting—Mr. John Naylor, Mr. account in this name of Mr. Armstrong was an account Ilenshall, Mr. Shakeshlift, Mr. Astley Mr. J. A. Naylor , with an existing individual—Armstrong was a builder— (son of Mr. John Naylor) and himself. ae that it was entirely aboveboard and was guaranteed by It was not much use Mr. Ilenshull saying anything Mr. John Naylor, and that in no other sense was it Mr. there after he had got across with John Naylor, was it ? Naylor's account. There is not the slightest, ground —Certainly. We were there to listen to reason. If Mr. for the suggested analogy. Ilenshall had had an explanation to give, we would have only been too pleased to receive it. PRISONER COMMITTED FOR TRIAL. But you never asked for any reasons.—He did not Tao magistrates retired to consider their decision and offer them, and when a man is accused of stealing £2OO on their return into Court the Chairman said they had and he has an explanation to offer he surely does not decided that prisoner be committed to the Assizes on wait to be asked for it. all the charges. Who made the accusation ?—The accusation was made by Mr. John Naylor. A PICTURE TRANSACTION. What did he say ?—fie said money had been received On resoming after luncheon, prisoner was further by Mr. Henshall on certain dates and had not been charged that on December 15th, 1003, being a clerk to accounted for, that they had not been entered up in the Robert Anderton Naylor, he stole £s4,the money of his ordinary receipt book of the company, and that Mr employer. Ilenshall had retained them. . Mr. Hewart said the facts of the case were compare- lie did not say to Mr. Henshall " You have stolen tively simple and illustrateda method of which some that £2OO ?"—He did say "You have falsified the books examples had already been given. It appeared that, in: of the company." 1903 the prisoner had some dealings with Messrs. ' With regard so this £2OO ?—Not with regard to the Chas. Rowley and Co., Ltd., picture dealers and framers, £2OO, but with regard to other affairs. Manchester. lie sent some pictures to be framed, some What did Mr. erenshall say ?-1-le had no reply to for himself and some for a friend, M. Wood, and there ' make. came a moment at which the sum of £lB 13s. 34. was Didn't Mr. Henshall say, while they were talking due from him to Messrs. Rowley. He had paid £lO on about this Starkey's business " I will tell you all about, account and had, i also t men paid four guineas by ~ 11r. ' that ?"—I have no recollection of it. Wood for the wore done for him, or t might possibly , Do you know whether he and Mr. John Naylor had a be thst he received that methsiottstring likeafter .e 3 wardB or afterwards. However, long talk after the meeting ?—I don't know, there was a balance Did Mr. John Naylor tell you that Mr. Llenshall had from him to Messrs. Rowley. What he did was to get , accounted for Starkey's £2OO ?—No. It' a cheque for £54 drawn upon Mr. Naylor, as if there ; I Did he tell you that Mr. H had told hint e enshall was some account owing by Mr. R. A. Naylor, and he ' had told the book-keeper that Hewitt had paid err s-- had the cheque sent to Messrs. Rowley. tt was really No. more than he owed the firm, and Mr. R. A. Naylor In answer to Me. Howart, witness said Mr John ow. i owed them nothing_ at, all. The result. was that the Naylor was the " Dear Mr. John " referred to in the prisoner's indebtedness to Messrs. Rowleyv was sirs. letter from Mr. Henshall which Mr. Liewart read at the charged, he got his own pictures for nothing, he got previous hearing, four guineas from Mr. Wood, and £1 Gs. 3d. profit into Mr. Ilewart ageie read the letter which conclude! : the bargain. The way he got the cheque was by making aylor : "R. A. N " In the links of a lifetime's service I trust there is out an imaginary invoice Manchester. 1, 'o contract for Esq., to T. ~ some mutual feeling of regard for each other." Rowley Now Cross, Is that the same John Naylor against whom my friend stable drain, water tank and chemical works, Bushy i suggests the prisoner hid been making charges ?—The Old-road, to. Mr. Rowley's order, £5l. L. H." Then ' seine John Naylor. analysing the Invoice, he marked it "outside invoice With regard to the Armstrong account, my friend has £s.l..".wher whereas fact it was a payment for the pictures. used the word " bogus account "—I don't like it. Herbert,William Ryder, secretary to Charles Rowley (Doubter.) Ltd., picture dealers, Manchester, stated that the firma Mr. Swift : lam sorry to use any word that Air. had at various times sold pictures to the prisoner and , Edwards does not like. (Laughter.) framed pictures for him. He produced a letter of I Mr. Ilewart : Was it a bogus account or was it a October o 10th, 19. 03,, from the prisoner to his firm, genuine account ?—Lt was a genuine account. stating that, he as sending five water colours which Was there anything concealed or other than honest, required suitable selcct frames' the gilt and glass to be of about it ?—Everyone in the office knows what the the best. Witness also produced ledger account with account is. the prisoner showing that on December lst, he supplied Is Armstrong a builder?—He is building for Mr. John him with picture frames of the value of £2 155., and on Naylor. December 15th prisoner paid them £lO cash and was ' Is the account guaranteed by Mr. Naylor ?—Yes. supplied with pictures to the value of £4B 13s. 3d. A whatovei about that account which Is there anything letter was sent to the prisoner and on December 15th e causes it rightly to be called a bogus account ?—Nothing prisoner paid a cheque for .C5l signed " p.pro. R. A. whatever. It goes through the books of the company in Naylor, John Naylor." The £54 was moreithan he the ordinary way like any other customer's frame account. owed and on Docember 30th he purchased a picture You say the prisoner never denied that he had had trame price £1 15s. 64, On December 16th witness Starkey's money, Was he asked whether he bad had Starkey's money ?—No. He was accused of having it. When ?—At the meeting at which he was suspended. Wits that the first time in your hearing that the matter</p>
        <p>credited his account with ss. for a picture case, and in Janua-y 190.5-with ss. for Another case. In the 'mean- time an invoice had been sent to the prisoner in December, 1903, for £4B 138. 2d. After prisoner had paid the £54 cheque he sent the firm a letter stating that they had a small balance to his credit and he would be sending a picture or two to be framed, after which matters could be adjus!ed. There were some. further transactions and a balance still remained. On February 4th, 1905, they sent prisoner the balance of £ll Gs. 3d. and he gave them a receipt for it. By Mr. Swift : They had prisoner's private address as well as that of the firm. He could not say whether the goods were sent to prisoner's house or to the works but he did not think it was likely they would be: delivered at the works. They would probably be left at the station. Edwin Wood, builder and contractor, Sadler-street, Widnes, stated that prisoner showed him some drawings and he agreed to buy two of them. Prisoner was to get them framed. They were fm a aed and delivered to witness, but he could not tell by whom the work was done. tie paid the prisoner for them by cheque for £lO 15q.. datld January 27th, 1901. By Mr. Swift : lie thought the £lO l 5;. included the pictures as well as the framing, but he was not quilt° certain. John Edwards, cashier and secretary to Messrs. Naylor, stated that in December, 190:3, and until December, 1905, ha was employed by Mr. It. A. Naylor as cashier. He produced an invoice for £5l male out on behalf of I'. Rowley, New Cross, Manchester, pur- porting to be" to contract for stable drain and water tank at Bushy Old Road." It was in the prisoner's handwriting. Witness found the invoice amongst the ordinary invoices of the firm at the time. At the foot was the analysis of the invoice "outside contract £54" in Mr. Elenshall's handwriting, meaning that the sum was to be bebited to that department. Witness treated it as a regular invoice of the firm and drew a cheque payable to T. lto‘fley for £54. The invoice was treated throughout as a regular account of R. A. Naylor's. From December 15th, 190:3, when the cheque was paid up to the present time,the transaction had been treated as one of the firm's and they had paid the money. In 1903, prisoner passed the invoices of the firm in the ordinary course of his duties. By Mr. Swift: It was customary for the late Mr. R. A. Naylor to give Christmas presents to good customers and they were entered in the books as donations.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift: I want you to find me in the books some donations so that we may see what sort of things they were.</p>
        <p>On referring to the purchase ledger account witness said an item of £lB 15s. was entered for Christmas boxes consisting of geese. Mr. Swift : Supposing the pictures had been got for Mr. Naylor to givt away as prfsents, you do not say there was anything wrong in them being paid for out of Mr. Naylor's money ?—Not if Mr. Naylor intended it. Have you ever had any conversation with Mr. Naylor about the matter ?—No. You have not the remotest idea whether Mr. Na3lor knew about it or did not know ?—No. Mr. 'lowan : Did you ever knaT of Mr. Naylor giving a man a stable drain, a water tank, or chemical works for a Christmas present ? (Laughter.)—No, Sur posing Mr. Naylor did know this £5l was for pictures can you suggest any reason why the invoiee should be "for contract for stable drain Scc. ? "—No, I cannot suggest any reason.. Walter Mather, chartered accountant, Manchester, stated that he bad examined the books of the business of R. A. Naylor for the year 1903 and found that an invoice for £54 had been entered, payable to T. Rowley, of Manchester, and a cheque drawn for that amount. It appeared from the books that the cheque was paid' by Mr. IL A. Naylor. The Chief Constable stated that he charged the prisoner with the offence aud he made no reply. On being cautioned, prisoner replied: "Not guilty." Mr. Swift submitted that there was absolutely no evidence that Mr. Robert A. Naylor did not know perfectly well about the transaction, and why the cheque was drawn. It might be said it was easy to say that., because Mr. Naylor was dead and could not be called. He (Mr. Swift) never knew it to be suggested as a reason for convicting a man of a criminal offence that when the Case for the prosecution broke down on one point he might still be convicted because the witness who could have proved it was dead. It might be that Mr. Henshall got the advantaqo of Mr. Naylor being dead, but certainly the prosecution bad left the case in this state—that there was no evidence whatever that Mr. IL A. Naylor, who was said to have been robbed of this money, did not know perfectly well all about the transaction. Under those circumstances he asked the Bench to say there could not possibly be a conviction in the case and prisoner Oiould not be committed. After a short deliberation, the Bench committed prisoner for trial on this charge also.</p>
