      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>BAIL REFUSED.</head>
        <p>BAIL REFUSED.</p>
        <p>At this stage it was decided to adjourn the case until to-day (Saturday). The clerk intimated that two charges of having on Februury 20th, 1904, stolen £5OO and on 11th February, 1905, £lOO were down for hearing. Mr. Swift asked for prisoner to be liberated on the same bail as before (24,000). Mr. Hewart said his instructions were to oppose any application for hut. There were three charges of forgery, three of larceny as a director, and one of larceny as a servant; and information had been laid with regard to further charges of .2500 and £lOO. He submitted that was not a case in which bail should continue to be granted. Mr. Swift said he really could not understand the attitude taken up by the nrosectition. The cases that they had been inquiring into that day were not new ; they had beard about them all along ang knew they were comine. Mr. Efenshall had always surrendered to his bail when called upon, and the Bench could not with- hold that fact from theirconsideration.lle utterly failed to see the rhyme, reason or common sense of Mr. Hewart's application. Why should Mr. Henshall stay in prison for a week? He had not been convicted of any offence, and he was entitled to go free until the moment he was found guilty by a jury, unless the magistrates were of opinion that for some good cause which had been shown to them he would be likely to endeavour to flee the country. There was no suggestion of that kind. He was now on bail, amounting to £l,OOO, and the firm had obtained an injunction against (qr. Henshall to prevent him dealing with £3,000 worth of securities which he bad in the hank. He contended it was a preposterous suggestion, and he hoped the magistrates would not consider it for a moment. After consulting in private, the magistrates decided to refuse bail.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift said that under ordinary circumstances the prisoner would be taken to Manchester but provison could and would be made by the Constable to keep him ‘Varrington if the Bench would allow that course to he adopted. He was particularly anxious that Mr. Ifenshall should not be taken away where his solicitor could not communicate with him. No doubt the Bench regretted as much as anybody else having been com- pelled to come to the conclusion they had done, and would give every facility they could to the prisoner. He thought the Chief Constable was willing for the accused to remain in Warrington. The Chief Constable : That is hardly correct. I leave it entirely to the Bench as to whether Mr. Henshall should go to Manchester or stay hero. As a matter of fact I think it is a case where Mr. Hershall should go to Manchester and the Warrington police should not have the custody of him for seven days. I would rather be relieved of the responsibility. If the Bench desire him to remain here I will acquiece. The magistrates agreed that prisoner should remain at Warrington.</p>
        <p>A Refreshing Drink and a Sustaining Food.</p>
