        <p>towards getting rid of the bonds of red tape which have so often maddened and disgusted the free- and-easy Colonial.</p>
        <p>Ou another page we .print</p>
        <p>COMPULSORY the first of a-shOrt series of MILITARY TRAINING, articles on the proposals of the National Service League for compulsory military service, by Mr. B. S. Townroe, the young gentleman who has collabor- ated with Miss Crosfield in the authorship of a new sketch in two acts entitled "The Stitch in Time," which is to be produced this evening in the dining hall at Messrs. Crosfields' works. It seems to us, if Mr. Townroe will forgive the raillery, that he believes two stitches in time are better than one. The sketch has, we believe, a strong N.S.L. flavoer, and as already announced the N.S.L. is being backed up by special pleading from Mr. Townroe's pen in the "Examiner." Our readers are well aware that compulsory service has always been adversely criticised in these columns, but we are desirous that the subject should be fully debated. Mr, Townroe invites criticism, and we hope our readers will not hesitate to express their views. National Service is being as vigor- ously " pushed" as Tariff Reform, and while we do not consider the d ,ctrines of both equally harmful we certainly think that neither should be swallowed holus bolus.</p>
        <p>The complexion of the Tory THE TORY PARTY'S Party at present is any. INTERNAL DISORDER. thipg but healthy. It reflects only too truly the grave disorders within and suggests the possibility of a constitutional breakdown. The cause of the trouble is "free food," a diet which agrees admirably with the Free Trade digestion but which inevitably makes the Protectionists and Tariff Reformers liverish and difficult to associate with. These latter gentlemen, having captured the Tory organisation, have decided to purge the Party of all free food and Free Fooders. The fiat went forth in the "Morning Post "on Monday that</p>
        <p>"The Conservative Central Office will decline to give its official support to any candidate who does not unreservedly support the Leader of the Unionist Party in the policy "laid down " at Birmingham in November 1907. The plain meaning of this is that Conservative and Unionist members and candidates must in future toe the line—that wavering uncertain line drawn by Mr. Balfour—or stand to be pilloried and excommunicated by their one time friends. The Confederates, as the militant Tariff Reformers are called, mean to have their own way and they have already marked down sixteen Unionist objectors to the Birmingham programme who will at once be told to " stand and deliver " their views on the Fiscal question. If they do not they must go. The Tariff Rbfdrmers mean to relieve their party of the fear that on a division on a future Tariff Reform Budget some fifty of its members might be found neutral or even in the opposite camp. Possibly they also fear some degree of indefiniteness in Mr. Balfour's proposals unless he is urged on by a party purged of scrupulous Free Fooders. We do not doubt that this grim sacrifice of many of the intellectuals of Unionism will make for unity, but the unity may be of a different kind from that which usually implies strength. A uuited party of Protectionists may, and probably will, find itself peurile. The Free Fooiers of the Unionist side have hitherto been passive in their resistance. This blow in the back is already rousing some of them to activity, and if they cannot find a congenial home in the Liberal ranks they can at least bring a not inconsiderable acces- sion of strength to the Free Trade fighting line. Meanwhile, Liberals can afford to smile complacently at the tangle the once great Con- servative party is getting into through Tariff Reform. At the next election it will have to stake all on one throw, and the chances of success do not seem to inspire even the Tariff Reformers with much confidence.</p>
        <p>Scotsmen and Burns BURNS THIRD admirers the world over JUBILEE. celebrated yesterday the third jubilee of Burns's birth. A Burns concert took place in the Albert Hall, London, in which a great-grandson of the poet played the organ, and the Burns Clubs met to drink to the "immortal memory on the anniversary date. Burns relics were brought out and silently regarded, and a million members recalled that they stood together, not only as admirers of the most eloquent of national poetry, but as witnesses of such human principles as are embodied in "A man's a man for a' that." Tne Scot may be clannish, but that is a good thing; and when the coannon bond is in sentiments like these one can only wish s greater Success and a wider inclusiveness to the confraternity. With the restoration of the Auld Brig of Ayr, the Sir William Wallace Memorial, apid the Scotch History Chair in Glasgow University, there have been movements enough recently to occupy the whole race of Caledonia, and quicken the historical and national sensibilities. Without doubt the Scot is immensely fortunate both in his history and in the instinct which makes him responsive to all its best traditions. For the Southron who lives over-much in the time present it would be a revelation to turn to the Scottish calendar and see how every day brings reminders of heroic achievements in politics, literature, religion, science and war. Mr. Carnegie attributes in some part the indomitable quality in his outlook to his youthful conununion with the Scottish Chiefs ; and wnile tastes may differ in respect of reading, there can be no question that the Scot comes back reinforced from his wander- ings in the past to deal a goad stroke in the present and, it may be, to rnake a real contribution to the future age.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court. Solicitor (to witness in compensation case) : Your husband is employed at the same ironworks as the applicant ? Witness : Yes, but not in the same compartment.</p>
        <p>* * * Flattery is usually calculated to put one in a good humour, and three small boys who arrived late at the suffragist meeting at Stockton Heath on Thursday and made their way to front seats looked distinctly pleased when the lady speaker observed : " What a long time it takes you gentlemen to settle yourselves." They had probably not been placed in this category before.</p>
        <p>* * * Alderman Bennett has soon taken the plunge from the Ministerial deck of the new Conservative Ship of State. He will boldly introduce a Tariff Reform Bill designed to federate the Empire and provide for imperial defence, at the next meeting of the Debating Society. How the critics will fall upon him ! We can hear in imagination the rending of the carcase—not of the Alderman, but of his bold, bad Bill.</p>
        <p>I s * * * Lord Northcote's suggestion Quite a feast of oratory was forthcoming at the A COLONIAL OFFICE that the clerks of ti e , Women's Suffrage meeting at Stockton Heath on REFORM. Cilonial Office should be Thursday evening. For a full two hours the lady permitted to acquire some 1 speakers had possession of the platform and no-one Colonial training is interesting. British adminis who attended would, we imagine, deny that the trative methods are curious, and hitherto one of addresses were both eloquent and convincing. We the principles upon which appointments of clerks I have never heard the case for the vote more ably put, to the Colonial Office have been made has been 1 and that a great impression was made upon the I that these persons should have no knowledge of 1 audience was evident from the fact that the resolution was carlied unanimously. i the lands with whose affairs they might * * * 'have to deal. The conception of the functions We comm end to the townspeople the Mayor's appeal, i of the Colonial Office has changed lately. : published n another column, for subscriptions for the Before Mr. Chamberlain's occupancy of the 1 relief of the deserving poor in Warrington. Having ! post of Colonial Secretary, the ideal aimed I caused inquiries to be made, his Worship has :at by the Colonial Office administrators seemed I ascertained that great distress prevails in the to be to provide a salutary check on 4 town among the families of the unemployed. 1 the ill-timed and presumptuous advance of tha He has, through the agency of the Distress i Colonies. Tnere was a lack of sympathy of which Committee, been able to provide relief for some Colonial Governments rightly complained. Now-a- of the most necessitous cases, but the fund at his 1 days all that is changed, and the Colonial Office i disposal is now nearly exhausted, and the relief must has learnt to study and appreciate the interests I soon come to an end, unless he can obtain the help of and the point of view of the Colonies whose affairs 1 the townspeople. The Mayor's efforts have been it has in its keeping. Lord Northcote would like ' directed to the relief of women and children by the to see the policy further extended, so that the i distribution of coal, bread, milk and small quantities clerks and other officials might have practical of groceries, and great care has been exercised by the i officers appointed to see that the gifts are not wrongly experience of the services with whose conduct of I applied. We hope his Worship's commendable effort affairs they will have to deal: By this means the , will meet with general and cordial support and that way would certainly be made more easy for subscriptions will prompt ig be sent to the Borough i co-operation, and one more step would be taken Treasurer, Bank House.</p>
        <p>Nothing fresher than the paint marks on the river wall is reported in connection with the Warrington Bridge scheme. The Sub-Committee met on Thursday and discussed various proposals with extreme volu- bility, but nothing was done. It still remains to be decided whether the bridge shall be 60 feet or 80 feet wide, and whether it shall be built on the ferro- concrete principle. The General Purposes Committee is to consider the scheme on ilonday. We shall be delighted to report a forward m3ve in our next issue.</p>
        <p>The Right Hon. Augu-stine Birrell, K.C., MP. entered on Tuesday on his sixtieth year, having been born near Liverpool on January 19th, 1850, son of a Nonconformist minister. Educated at Amersham Hall School and Trinity Hall, Cambridge, he joined the Bar in 1875, and practised in the Chancery Courts. Until 1889, when he became 11. P. for West Fifeshire, he was better known as a successful author than as a politician. Since 1905 he has represented the North Division of Bristol, and is the Secretary for Ireland under the present Government. * t *</p>
        <p>A. man who takes alcohol simply because he likes it is acting disloyally towards his country.—Sr victor liorseley.</p>
        <p>I cannot call it generous alms-giving when people are driven to give by fear of public opinion.— Prebendary Webb.Peploe. Young women think that unless they are married their lives are not a success, but as a matter of fact I think the reverse is exactly true.— Mr. Taft. The country is governed by three Houses —the House of Llnsdowne, which has settled the Licensing Bill; the Church House, which had much to do with blocking the Education Bill ; and the Public-House, which governs the country generally.—Sir Walter Poster, M.P. * * *</p>
        <p>The Liberals in our local Parliament have set their Tory friends n good example in Parliamentary pro- cedure. It will be recalled tint when the Liberals introduced their last King's Speech, the leader of the Opposition (now the Premier) promptly challenged a division and Mr. Howes, the Liberal Whip, aptly observed "An insult to the King !" Last Tuesday evening the positions were reversed and the King's Speech of the Tory Government was under dis- cussion. There was, however, no attempt on the part of the Opposition to emulate the tactics of their opponents, tempting as the opportunity of tit-for-tat might appear, and they allowed the Address to pass without a single dissentient. After this, Liberal loyalty should be unquestioned. * * * Mr. Austen Chamberlain having " wobbled " for years with regard to the coal tax, has at last appar- ently decided definitely against it. Giving, like Jr. Wyndham," merely a personal opinion," he has informed a correspondent that" he does not think it likely that, having once been withdrawn, the tax will be proposed again by any Government, as far as he can forecast in the future." He still, however,adheres to the utterly erroneous opinion that the removal of the duty was in effect " a present to the foreign purchaser of two million pounds a year," which does not make it easy to understand why he is not in favour of its re-imposition ; but we may surmise that the question of miners' votes has something to do with his decision.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <p>IMS than a month inter-</p>
        <p>THE venes between the present PARLIAMENTARY date and the reassembling PROSPECT. of Parliament. Ministers</p>
        <p>will in the meantime be exceptionally busy, for the autuca session of last year, and the brief season of rest which then became imperative, have greatly cramped them for time in which to prepare their programme. The great measure of the year will be the Budget, of course, It will be in itself a whole series of measures in a much more complete and real sense than usual. Great things are expected of Mr. LLOYD-GEORGE as Chancellor of the Exchequer. He is confronted with a financial situation of extreme complexity. Tne revenue for the last few months has been far from realising expecta- tions; but a much greater revenue is required ' than that which was estimated for in the last Budget. The 0:d-Ago Pensions have to be put 'on a sound basis for one thing, and the Act will probably be so amended as to make it include a larger number of persons, and therefore a larger expenditure, than was originally contemplated. Many undoubted cases of hardship have declared themselves, acd the Government may be trusted so to deal with the Act as to make hard cases uncommon at least, though it will scarcely find it possible to construct such a measure as will not permit them to arise. But the Budget will be chiefly interesting because there have for some time past been rumours that the House of L3rds intends to throw it out. Thia seems to us scarcely probable. The Peers are not too frequently guided by wholly reasonable considerations ; but they will scarcely be so mad, we imagine, as to take a stop which would not only disorganise the natioeal services but would suggest that they had challenged the Howe of Commons to adopt the readiest and easiest means of dealing with themselves, the stoppage of supplies. Tile rejection of the Budget would be a tremendous step, certain to be followed by events from which the House of L3rds would not emerge in its present state Whatever happened. The nature and txtent of the powers of the House of Lords are rather extensively misconceived, and mainly, perhaps, by the Lordo themselves. Certainly they have been very greatly and gravely exaggerated of late; and perhaps out of this fact arises the idea that the rejection of the Budget would fall within the category of ordinary events. On the contrary, it would be a revolution. The House of Lords exercises no control whatever over the principal affairs of State. It has nothing to do with the Army or Navy, or with the Civil Service, or with the provision of means for upholding the throne; but the Budget has to do with the whole of these things. It is inclusive of the whole finances of the nation, as its name implies. It is not probable, to say the least, that a Lansdowne House meeting will resolve on taking the perilously irresponsible step of creating such chaos as must result from the rejection of the Budget. But we shall see. The Government is being much blamed in some quarters because it does not propose to "tackle " the Lords at once. There would, of course, be no question of the immediateness of the conflict if the Budget were thrown out; but otherwise the Government seems well advised in resolving to carry out as much as it can of its programme. To many beneficent measures that it is desirable to carry into law the present attitude of the House of Lords is rather favourable than not. As we have seen during the last three Sessions of Parlia_ ment, the Peers are moved by two strongly con- trasted lines of feeling, each of which is favourable to certain kinds of social legislation. Tney are timid to the point of fear in regard to a conflict with the great trade organisations, and therefore it was possible to secure the passage of the Trade Disputes Bill and the Old-Age Pensions Bill. Then they compound for the rejection of important measures like the Licensing Bill by giving a reluctant assent to others that are not so important but, nevertheless, are extremely useful and beneficial. Whilst they are willing to pay toll of this kind, the Government is not only justified in taking it, but is judicious in doing so. The House of Lords question remains the predominant question meanwhile, as Mr. Aswan has told us. That is a position from which it can only be dislodged by a great surrender on its own part; and this is not at present to be looked for.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>POLITICS OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>THE POLITICS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>[FROII OUR LONDON CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>A striking manifesto appeared in the "Morning Post " on Monday, urging that the " Free Food Group" should be cut off from the Party, and the significant announcement is made that the Conserva- tive Central Office will refuse tl give its support to any candidates who do not abandon all reservations to their acceptance of the Protectionist creed. Such an announcement raises many speculations as to the terms on which Mr. Balfour holds the leadership of his Party.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>STOCK ARGUAIENT.</head>
        <p>A STOCK ARGUAIENT.</p>
        <p>It has come to be quite the stock argument of Opposition speakers that the Liberal Government has convicted itself of the desire to cling to office at any price by declining to dissolve at a time and on the ground dictlited by the House of Lords. Mr Winston Churchill and Lord Crewe have shown the absurdity of this contention, but, indeed, it only required to be stated for its absurdity to appear. A united Cabinet, an enormous majority, and a policy which by the achievements of the past three years has given ample pledges that the Liberal Party is by no means impotent to give effect to its ideas, are scarcely the conditions under which immediate dissolution should be necessary because a number of by-elections have slightly increased the size of the Opposition and the House of Lords has set itself to thwart the purposes of the Government whenever it has dared. If the House of Lords is so confident that it is the chosen champion of an indignant nation against an arbitrary House of Commons iv hich does not represent the wishes of the nation, its remedy is in its own hands. As Ur. Churchill has said, if the Lords want a speedy dissolution they know where to find one. They can bring the Government of the country to a standstill by rejecting the provision which the Commons make for the financial service of the year. Unconstitutional as such an act would be, it would scarcely abuse the spirit of the constitution more flagrantly than sorne of the adventures which they had already undertaken. Mr. Churchill did not exaggerate when he declared that Lord Lansdowne, in his leadership of the House of Lords during the present Parliament, had put forward claims in its behalf more far-reaching than ever were made by the late Lord Salisbury, and that no Tory leader in modern times had taken so high a view of its rights. THE DOMINATING ISSUE.</p>
        <p>If it is asked why, when the House of Lords has interfered so unscrupulously and disastrously with the legitimate activities of the House of Commons, there should be any delay in appealing to the country, the answer is plain enough. When the appeal is made to the country it will not be upon the merit of any one measure which the Lords may throw out. It will be upon the general issue whether an irrespon- sible Chamber is to have the power of nullifying an immense preponderance of votes in the House of Com- mons, whenever that preponderance is on the Liberal side. The Conservatives seem to expect that as soon as the House of Lords rejects a Liberal measure, Parliament should be dissolved, and then, after a General Election has proved that the claim of an unrepresentative Chamber to voice the wishes of the people more accurately than the people's representa- tives was absolutely ridiculous, the whole process is gone through again over the next important Liberal measure, and so on ad infinitum. The issue between the veto of the Lords and the will of the people is not one which can be approached in this futile, piece- meal fashion. It has to be presented in its entirety. And there is no substance whatever in the suggestion that the Liberal Party is shirking that great issue because it did not hasten to scrap such a fine machine for social reform as the present Parliament at the first indication of the Lords obstructive proclivities. VIE LIBERAL RECORD.</p>
        <p>The Liberal- Government is invited by the Opposi- tion to regard itsaif as crushed and discredited ; because the House of Lords has thrown out useful measures which the Government had introduced in accordance with the wishes of the constituencies expressed at the last General Election. The most effectual reply to such an absurd travesty of the real events is such a review of the Government's achieve- ments as Mr. Churchill took at Birmingham. In South Africa Liberal policy has produced results which have not only falsified all the prophecies of hostile critics' but have surpassed the wildest dreams of the most robust optimists. The detraction of Party has been silenced before thf strong and skilful administration of. Foreign and Indian affairs which have distinguished the term of office of a Government which, in the unanimous utterance of Conservative politicians, was incapable of administering Imperial affairs with dignity and safety. If, as Mr. Churchill said, it is claimed that our policy abroad has pros- pered better than at home, there is a single and a simple explanation. " Abroad we have enjoyed full responsibility, a free hand, and fair play; at home we have bad a divided authority." The House of Lords has usurped the privileges and rights of the people's representatives—" a fettered hand, and the ! reverse of fair play. We have been hampered and 1 we have been harassed. We have done much ; we could have done much more."</p>
        <p>THE SOCIAL FIELD. If there was no field of usefulness which was not closed to the Liberal Government by the malicious obstruction of the House of Lords, then it would be imperative to dissolve at once. But it is scarcely credible that the House of Lords will dare to exercise its blighting influence in the field of activity which Mr. Churchill has indicated as still open to a Liberal Government —the social field. Much can ha done, there is reason to hope, in spite of the House of Lords, for the organization of industrial conditions.</p>
        <p>There is "a large mass of largely preventable and even curable suffering" with which the Liberal Government can deal without being hopelessly' obstructed—however much it may be hampered and embarrassed -by the House of Lords.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>TWO FLAGRANT MISREPRESENTATIONS.</head>
        <p>TWO FLAGRANT MISREPRESENTATIONS.</p>
        <p>There are two cards which the Conservative plat- form orator is always ready to play when in doubt. One, as we have seen, is the inquiry why, if the Liberal Government objects to the veto of the House of Lords, it does not at once dissolve. The other is the arraignment of the Government for shaking national credit. When we observe the financial crisis in which the Protectionist United States have been. involved, and the desperate struggles of Protectionist Germany to cope with a recurring revenue deficit, it is rather puzzling to understand why Great Britain, which has had no such troubles, is supposed to be suffering from any diminution of national credit. Still, we cannot believe that Lord Cromer, who is s statesman of unusual judgment and experience, would either lend currency to a statement which he did not believe, or accept a myth which had no foundation whatever. If the Liberal Government had done anything to shake com- mercial confidence, we should look to find the truth ofi its delinquencies in Lord Cromer's indictment rather than elsewhere. He evidently has a sincere belief. , that the confidence of the investor has been impaired. But he does not help us much to discover how this - has happened. He voted against the Licensing Bill,. he says, not so ,mrich because he disapproved of it9l provisions, many of which he acknowledges to have been excellent, as because he believed its defeat would i I allay a panic amongst certain of the investing classes., Does not this admission unconsciously give us a clue ?,. A. panic may be raised in two ways It may be raised - by something that has actually happened, or by sheer misrepresentatian. It is the :Opposition which has raised any panic which really exists; it is the Opposition which, by attributing intentions awl motives without any real justification to the Liberal. Government, has produced any alarm that there may 1 really be. - ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>RESTORING CONFIDENCE.</head>
        <p>RESTORING CONFIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Is it, or is it not, true that the frequency of conflicts] between Capital and Labour have done almost more than anything to restrict the flow of capital into nevr enterprises? Certainly, we have been told that is so often enough by capitalists themselves. But }fr.l Churchill can fairly claim, as he did at Leicester, that, "a new, a strong, and a happy tendency in industrial, affairs" has been set up by the Liberal Government,' owing to the machinery which the much-maligned-. Liberal Government has provided for a settlement of industrial disputes. So far from discouraging Capital 1 to enter into new channels, there is no Government- which has done more to give security to Capital le one of the most important conditions which deterne its activity. 1 mi</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>BUDGET RUMOURS.</head>
        <p>BUDGET RUMOURS.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>As for all the fabrications by which the Oppositiorti have endeavoured to persuade the nation that the nelal Liberal Budget will inflict a gross injury upon trade-pi there never was anything more flimsy or unreal. The. whole case against the Government is simply this : that they have refused to take a wholly unprecedented departure from precedent, and announce beforehandi what taxes the Chancellor of the Exchequer wl impose. Why 'a Goyernment, merely because it i Liberal, should be expected to do anything so advised, or to break any tradition of finance, at th_e4 peril of beiag denounced for creating a panic it impossible to guess, except that the want of stir legitimate weapon of attack often prompts vehemen partisans to adopt the wildest methods.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events. 1 The church anniversary in connection with the - Baptist Church, Golborne-street, will be celebrated. to-morrow (Sunday), when the Rev. R. E. Letherell will preach on the following subjects ; —4l orningrA ..j " What's the good of a Church ?" ; evening, " Jobs' Bunyan's Anvil."--In the afternoon Mr. John Upsoli will address a service for young men and women... On Thursday evening the congregational tea meetin will be held. 1</p>
        <p>The annual tea meeting and entertainment in coni nection with Emmanuel Church will be held in the, Institute, Knutsford-road, on Thursday evening nat. A soiree in connection with the Warrington Women's Liberal A,laociation will be held at the Lion Hotel Rooms on Friday, February 16th. Personal. . We understand that the marriage arranged betweety Mr. Maesmor Harrison of Grappenhall, and Miss: Sleigh of Chester, will take place on February 3rd at the Warrington Parish Church.</p>
        <p>Dramatic Society's Performances. The Warrington Dramatic Society will present tbi well-known comedy " The Lady of Ostend " at the Royal Court Theatre, during the week commencing February Bth, in aid of the Infirmary, Nursing Association, and St. John's Ambulance. The Society' has already raised over £2,000 in this way. Local Wills.—The Late Mr. J. Potter. The will has been proved of the late Mr. Jose Potter, of 5, Suez-street, v.Varrington, until recent co-proprieter and manager of the Royal Cot2ll, Theatre and Royal Theatre of Varieties, Warringtolk and New Grand Theatre, Rawtenstall, at £8,786.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BRNF.FIT.-UN'DBR TEIB CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRB.—Advt. Education Committee.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Educatioli Committee will be held in the Town Hall on Mondar evening. The annual increases of teachers' salarie!,^ in accordance with scale, will be submitted, the total: amount of the increases being £1,038. Pressure htor ing been brought to bear by the Board of Educatiott a sub-committee has been instructed to seek a furtherfl interview with Lord Lilford's agent, with a view tol. securing from him a firm offer of a suitable site for 1°: school in the Wellfield-street district.</p>
        <p>Warring ton Musical Festival. Complete s uccess is confidently anticipated for the. second Warrington Musical Festival which will 10 held in the Parr Hall on Friday and Saturday'," February sth and 6th. About 1,200 entries hais been received and many keen and interesting contest!; are promised. The Friday evening has been 64 apart for purely local events and the programme will' include church or chapel choir contests for mixed a male choirs of 25 to 35 voices and 16 to 20 voicer respectively, children's choral contest and children's ' action song competition, the latter being confined W Band of Hope children. On Saturday, when the open events will be decided, the outstanding items will be the chief choral contest (mixed choirs) for £2O and, the challenge shield presented by Mrs. A. H. Crosfield,', and the second choral competition (male choirs) which the first prize is £l2. Special interest centre; in the male voice choir contest as the two local choirs have decided to compete. The Committee have been: fortunate in securing the services of two such eminent adjudicators as Mr. S. Coleridge Taylor and Mr Harri, Evans. Tickets can now be obtained from Messrs•'' Wadsworth's, Sankey-street, Mr. E. Wilkes, Legli' street, and members of the Committee.</p>
        <p>Warrington Band of Hope Union. The quarterly Council meeting of the above Unica was held in Gregory's Cafe, Buttermarket-street, oti Thursday evening. Mr. Thomas Dale presided in tb absence of the President (Mrs. Dodds). The minul of the Executive Committee were submitted and cell firmed which showed that a great deal of go 4 and useful work had been done. The arrangtri meats for the coming May Festival are %%lel in hand and the Committee trust that thel; efforts will be even more successful than previous years. Mr. G. Whitfield presented his repo as delegate to the United Kingdom Band of He ; Conference held at Leeds, which was much app. ,r I e ciated, and a vote of thanks was accorded for pi services. It was resolved that a United Public MOO be held in Parr Hall on Temperance Sunday as la , year, the names of several prominent speakers bein submitted to be approached as speakers. The vror of the Great Forward movement which has baelN inaugurated by the Lancashire and Cheshire Band il Hope Union was the subject of much discussion an&amp; it was urged that an effort be made to 0 every Band of Hope Society and Sandafi school in the district to co-operate in thA, this great effort to double to membership of the Bala' of Hope and to raise the sum of £20,000 for temper' ance work amongst the young. Many representative; of the various societies who were present, reporte? that their respective societies were doing all in the'. power to achieve the object in view. It is intended tl3 arrange for a system of house to house canvassing be done in February,and the Union is making a spec! a appeal for workers to assist in this great work. 'AI friends of the movement willing to assist are invitV to send in their names to the Hon. Secretary Mr. 1 , Gandy, 25, Whitefield-road, Stockton Heath. LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs &amp;Colds. Reliefe. Asthma and difficult breathing. 90., Is. lid.—Advt.</p>
