        <p>Warrington Women's Circle. Mrs. Ben Few presided at Sunday's meeting which 'was of a musical character. Miss Maud Hipkiss sang most sweetly "The Voice of the Father" and "0 for the wings of a dove." Violin duets " Sitnple A.veu " and " Wiegenlied " were given by the Misses Pooley, and the other soloist was Miss Millard who gave "The Gift " and "I have heard of a land," tlrs. Simpson offered prayer and Airs. Hampson gave the address.</p>
        <p>Buckley-street Band of Hope. On Monday,an entertainment was held in connection With the above. There was a good programme, the event of the evening being a very pleasing duet by Mr. J. Longworth and Mr. P. Kean, which reflicted great credit on them, and for which they earned rounds of applause. Other soloists were the Misses F. Adams, A. Whalley, Katie Bate and Emily Vale, and Mr. T. Allan. There was numerous recitations from A. Aspivall, Kate Spencer,M. Emery and H trold and Alf. Harter. The speaker was Mr. J. Knowles.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BEWEFIT.-U-NDER Tint CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Young People's Society. Oa M mclay evening the openinp.,., meeting of the second session of this society was held in the Memorial Building, an-I took the form of a social. A. series of "Table-aux Vivants " were given by several of the lady members, and songs were rendered by Messrs. W. Parker, H. Bailey and Mn. E. A. Sanders. A Pianoforte duet was played by Miss Bateman and Mr. T. F. Wallington, and a humorous selection was given by Mr, E. A. Sanders. ANNUAL SALE of Tea Services. Dinner Services, etc., great bargains.--J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge Street, Warrington.—Advt. Penny Popular Concerts. There was again a crowded audience at the concert in 80ld-street Wesleyan Schoolroom, on Saturday evening, when the whole of the programme was pro- vided by the Woodville Concert Party, described as " Liverpool's favourite entertainers." Songs (sentimental and humorous), duets and chorus were given in a style which won the hearty approval of the audience, and encores were frequent and enthusiastic. The Rev.T. Miller presided, and during an interval in the programme, delivered an address.</p>
        <p>- - PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER TIIII CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASFIERE.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Launch of a Coasting Steamer. A steel screw steamer of about 350 tons built to the order of Messrs. James Henry Monks (Preston) Limited was successfully launched from the ship- , building yard of Messrs. Scott and Sons, Bowling, Glasgow, on Thursday, the 21st inst. Her dimensions are as follows, viz. : 137 x 23 x lift. ; her engines of about 550 1. h.p. are being supplied by Ross and Duncans, Govan, Glasgow, and she is intended to attain a speed of 111 knots. She is built to Lloyd's Highest Class 100 Al. and is fitted with the most molern improvements to make her suitable for the general coasting trade. While moving down the ways she was named the "J. and J. Monks" by Mrs. Richard Mandsley, of Blackburn. BARGAINS in Silver-plated Teapots, Cruets, Pre- serve Dishes, etc.. during Sale.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. The Skating! Rink. The hum of "I„the roller skates can now be heard daily at Messrs. Artingstall's Buildings, near Wilder- spool Crossings, where a well-appointed rink has been opened this week. The large attendances indicate that the sport is likely to become highly popnlar in this district. Ample accommodation is provided for local enthusiasts and many merry hours can now be spent " rinking " to the strains of a local band. A learners' room will doubtless prove a great con- venience to those who are not yet proficient in the " art," and skates are provided at a nominal charge. Refreshments are also served. It is the intention of the management, if the rink meets with the anticipated success, to arrange carnivals, the proceeds of which will be handed over to local charities. The rink is open from 2 to 5 o'clock in the afternoon and from 7 to 10.39 o'clock in the evening. LINSEED COMPOUND' (Aniseed, Senega, Squil tte.), for Coughs. Of Chemists only.—Advt. ,Cleanly Caught. '1 am cleanly caught, I had come to steal all I could lay my hands on" was the reply of John Flannagan of Holt-street, when he was found upon the enclosed premises of J. G. Bayley, saddler, 4, Winwick-street, by Sergeant Dean on Sunday night last. When the prisoner was brought before the magistrates o n Monday, the Chief Constable stated 'that about 10 20 on Sunday night Mr. Danka, cycle dealer, Bewsey-street, heard a crash of glass at the rear of his premises. Thinking someone was endeavouring t) break into Bayley's shop he went in search of the police. Returning a few minutes later with Sergeant Dean and Constable Armstrong they found a glass window had been broken at the back of Bayley's premises. The sergeant had the premises surrounded, and then entered by the broken window He found the prisoner on the second floor, crouching in a corner. The Sergeant asked him what he wanted there and he replied as above. Prisoner was taken into custody. He (the Chief Constable) asked for a remand of eight days in order to make inquiries con- cerning the prisoner. The remand was granted. CLEARANCE SALE of Table Lamps, Hanging Lamps, etc., great bargains.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Presentations to Messrs. Bradbury and Fuller At a meeting held at the Lion Hotel on Sunday evening, Messrs. Bradbury and Fuller, late resident manager and stage manager respectively of the Palace and Hippodrome, were the recipients of handsome presents from members of the staff and friends. —Mr. Norman Latham, who presided, said he was sure that everyone present would be sorry they had occasion to call the meeting, as it meant that they were about to part with two real friends ; such friends were few and far between. (Hear, hear.) Having congratulated Mr. Bradbury on his recovery from his recent illness, Mr. Latham wished him and Mrs Bradbury and family and Mr. and Mrs. Fuller and family every success and happiness. (Applause.) He was glad to be able to say that bath gentlemen were remaining on the Macnaghten circuit. The great source of regret was that they were not retnaming at Warringtca. Mr. Widdop, conductor of the orchestra, presented qr. Bradbury with a purse of geld, with his sine,ere wishes for his future welfare, and remarked upon the happy relations which had existed between him and the stuff.--A presentation, also of a purse of gold, was made to Mr. Fuller, by Mr. Sanders, who voiced the good wishes of the staff and of Mr. Glencross, of the Electrical Department. Other speeches of a eulogistic character were made by Councillor Singleton and Messrs. T. D. Amos, Ivan F. Smith, E. Pierpaint, Lister, A. Freeman, H. Frankish, Smiley (Royal Court Theatre), S Daintith, and J. Stinley. —Messrs. Brad- bury and Fuller suitably replied, and expressed the deep regret they felt at having to leave the town where they had made so many friends.—Votes of thanks were accorded to Mr. John Lee for the use of the room and to Mr. Latham for presiding. DURING SALE,RARE BARGAINS haBrD.SS and Iron Fenders, Coal Vases, etc.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington. "Child Life in India."</p>
        <p>The above was the title of an interesting lecture delivered by Miss Katheline Dixon on Wednesday evening in the Friends Meeting Room, Buttermarket- street. The chair was taken by gr. Charles J. Holmes. The proceedings were opened with a hymn after which the Rev. C. Harvey Cook offered prayer. Mr Holmes in welcoming the audience, said such meetings as that tended to bring closer together people of different religions and creeds. Referring to India I,.fr. Holmes remarked that Great Britain had to bear a great responsibility and it was their duty to uphold that responsibility. Miss Dixon in the course of her lecture referred to the bize and population of India. she said it had a population of 350,000,000 of which 250,000,000 were heathens, so there was plenty of work for the missionary yet. Once every year nearly all the missionaries of the country joined together and planned out different portions of that country in which to work. Illustrating the size of India, the lecturer said that it 'would make 18 British Isles, or 19. Japans, or 7 Germanies. Miss Dixon when she is out in India superintends a • sahool of about 400 orphan children, and gave many instances of young children being found on the road side, forsaken, being brought up at the school. The boys were taught the mecanism of machinery and howl to plough, and other forms of work, while the girls were taught household work. During the rainy season in India, snakes and lizards abound, it is often dangerous to go out doors for fear of receiving bites from them. On one occasion, the lecturer woke UP one night and found a snake had crawled into the bed. Luckily she sprang out in time, but the snake followed her, and after an exciting time she was able to dispatch it. Many native costumes and mins were on view and were inspected with interest. Miss Dixon was warmly -thanked for her lectures and a collection was taken in aid of the Friends Foreign Missions. LINSEED COMPOUND 'for Coughs and Colds. Of proven efficacy. Of Chemists only. 9id.,15.1id.,25.9d.-Advt</p>
