      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>THE MEMBER'S NEW YEAR</p>
        <p>GREETING.</p>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>PEN KETH.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>WORKMEN'S COMPENSATION.</p>
        <p>County Court Cases at Warrington.</p>
        <p>Publican's Sudden Death.</p>
        <p>- --..--- Mr. Thomas Alfred Malkin, licencee of the Ship Inn, Walton, died suddenly on Thursday morning. The deceased, who was 49 years of age, had not been very well of late but it was act thought that there was any cause for alarm. He bad held the licence of the Ship Inn for fifteen years, prior to which he was a men‘ber of the Cheshire Constabulary.</p>
        <p>Take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, for Coughs and Colds; it never fails, is. lid., 2s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT. DEBATE ON THE KING'S SPEECH</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT. DEBATE ON THE KING'S SPEECH</p>
        <p>CONCLUDED.</p>
        <p>The King's Speech was again under discussion at the meeting of the Warrington Parliarngntary Debating Society held in the Assembly Room,Canservative Club, on Tuesday evening,and after a fairly interesting debate the usual " humble address of thanks " was adopted without dissent. There was a good attendance, particu- larly on the Alinisterial and Opposition beuches,but the Socialists had only two representatives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>QUESTION TIME.</head>
        <p>QUESTION TIME.</p>
        <p>Mr., J. JARMA.N (L., Burton-on-Trent), asked the Prime Minister, in vio;v of the amendment passed to the late Government's Land Values Bill and the speech thereon by the hon. member for Paisley, that it was 1" unjust and inequitable to tax agricultural land, as it is , the raw material from which the food of the people is produced" he will give the House the assurance that it is no part of the policy of his Majesty's Government to impose further taxes on the food of the people. The PRIME MINISTER, in reply, said he apprehended that no sensible man would object to pay a slight tax on food if he had the wherewithal to pay it, rather than he short of food as was often the case at present. (I fear, hear.) There was no inconsistency between the policy of the Government in resisting the taxation of agricultural land and that of re-arranging, if necessary, taxes on food by a svAeru of preferential treatment.' The former would raise the price of wheat with no corresponding advantage to the consunier,but the latter, if it raised prices, would also raise the wages the work- man got, and so he would be able to pay it. (Applause). Mr. JOHNSTONE (L., Dumfries) asked the - Minister whether he accepted as correct the definition that Protection meant " to ward off " or " repel," and if not would he give his definition. The PRIME MINISTER, in answer, said he could not accept that definition as correct for this reason—Pro- tection was a noun, and to " ward off" and " repel " were both verbs. (Laughter.) It they were going to define Protection they must define it by a noun. Dictionaries defined the word as " an act which pre- serves from injury," and the verb " to protect " meant to keep in safety. (Applause). THE DEBATE ON THE ADDRESS.</p>
        <p>• Mr. \V. NIGHTINGALE (S., Normanton), resumed the debate on the address in a speech of greAt vigour and earnestness. He said he looked upon the King's Speech as a catalogue and was waiting for the goods to be trotted out. (Laughter.) Ile thought that South Africa and Ireland should be left to manage their own affairs. With regard to home affairs the workers were getting sick of the plausibilities of parties who fattened and battened on position and left them to grovel in the dirt. They talked about schemes for federating the Empire, but where was the Empire when the manly people bad gone? Socialists were aiming to build up a strong democracy that would fight to the death for the welfare and safety of their homes. fie was not a " peace at any price " man. The new Cabinet was a splendid list and he hoped it would be as good as it looked. (Laughter.) Referring to dissensions in the Conservative ranks on the fiscal question, Mr. Nightingale said men who dared to remAin true to Free Trade principles had to be hounded out by a lot of nincompoops who were , not fit to unlace their shoes. (Ministerial " Oh, oh's.") An to the education question, the Government had better leave working folk alone. The Socialist Party were working for a democracy that would astonish the universe. They were universal humanitarians, and they were working for the world because they believed man- kind to be one great family. " When you get to the other tide," added the member for Normanton, amid laughter," you will have to mix." Mr. WIAIDURTON (C., Bristol West) supported all the measures forecasted in the King's speech. He said the re-adjustment of taxation would do away with a good , deal of unemployment. It was unfair that a shoemaker 'should have to pay a tea tax and that the country should allow foreign hoots to come in free. (Applause.) Mr. E. ENGLAND (L. Warrington), congratulated the Conservatives on the large Government they had formed which seemed to contain the cream of their party in Warrington, but he sympathised with them on having to follow one of the most brilliant Governments_ (laughter and applause)— at least they on that side of the House were excvtionally proud of their achieve- ments.</p>
        <p>Mr. ITEktm (N. Kilkenny South) : So are we. Mr. ENGLAND, continuing, said the late Government had certainly shown how to pass Bills. In spite of the charges hurled against them of being Socialistic, he claimed that they had carried out the best traditions of the Liberal party—" peace, retrenchment and reform." (Opposition cheers.) Last week the Member for New- port claimed that the Conservatives were the party of backbone. He believed, however, that fossils even had backbone, and unfortunately they knew that in the Con- servative party there were still fossils, (Lawdlter.) He warned the Government that as the Session went on they would be weighed in the balance, and woe betide them if they were found wanting. (Laughter.) Deal- ing with the King's Speech, Mr. England took exception to the reference to South Africa—that it " will prove to be the beginning of a period of improved welfare." When did the improvement take place ? When the Liberal Government took ollice. (Laughter.) Had they not been faithful to their promises? ("No " and " Yes.") He contended that Yellow labour was really now a thing of the past in South Africa. Alluding to , Alderman Bennett's appointment as Colonial Secretary, the speaker suggested that his proper place in tne House was on the Independent benches. He went on to read a parody on"Excelsior."in which Alderman Bennett ' was pictured as a member " who bore, spite of sound advice, a banner with the old device 'Tariff Reform." , It was of such a length, however, that the Member was cut short by the Speaker ringing his bell, because the time limit had been exceeded.</p>
        <p>Councillor Furaoti SMITH (Chancellor of the Exchequer) said that possibly it was a merciful provi- dence that induced the Speaker to ring his bell. Whether it was poetry or whether it was prose he thought it would take the author of the wo7ds to define it. What it was aiming at was completely lost, as the last few verses they did not hear. (Laughter). What struck him, continued the Chancellor of the Exchequer, with regard to the remarks of the members for Normanton and Warrington was that they had taken advantage of some disused sermons which had no reference whatever to the subject before them. They seemed to he burdened with a great grievance, and yet they never indicated by a single hint how that burden was to be relieved. That made their case very weak. It was a case of" Take all I get and do as little as possible for it. The NUMBER for NORMANTON.—NO, no. Continuing., the CHANCELLOR of the Exermeultu. said he did not know whether the Member for Normanton was a direct descendant of Elijah or the great Mogul of India, but he seemed to have the whole universe under control, with Jupiter and Saturn thrown in. (Laughter.) He talked about the claims of humanity. They should begin with small things and try and remedy things in their vicinity. Let the world look after itself. If their good example was worth following they might expect that it would influence others. Socialists and others dissatisfied with affairs protested and grumbled, and whenever the least consideration was shown them and the hand of control was modified, they immediately went beyond bounds and broke all regulations with regard to law and order. They had seen that in Ireland, and they saw it that day and what was the result ? It pointed to this, that if they were not under some controPthey would be their own worst enemies. It was because they had not shown that they had control of themselves that we had to insist upon the laws of the country being follcwed. If they had a real grievance why did they not in a constitu- tional way ask for their grievances to be remedied ? That was the principle upon which the King's Speech had been brought before the House. HOME RULE AMENDMENT. Mr. FELIX MAGINN (N.,West Clare) moved an amend. mend to the Address, expressing regret and disappoint- ment of all Irish people that no indication was given of the intention to confer on Irelaed the grant of self- government. He said what his party wanted was the reversal or revision of the Act of Union passed 109 years ago. He took the Colonial Secretary to task for stating that Irishmen were always " whining " at the doors of the British Government for some reform, and said Irishmen were not usually credited with doing much whining. A " threat and a blow " was said to be more in accordance with their national character. No one could claim that the Act of Union had worked to the advantage of anybody. The Conservatives said i" Why make a fetish of Free Trade r He asked them "Why make a fetish of Unionism, which has proved an injury to us and of no benefit to England ?" The Union Iwas an act of injustice foully accomplished by bribery and corruption. It was a fraud I upon the Irish people. It could not be said that the Union was on terms of equality and it was never meant to operate in the interests of Ireland. No white race had ever benefited by being governed by anothe• white race. It destroyed the moral fibre of the governed race to a great extent and raised foolish notions in the minds of the governing race. The country was not advancing in prosperity, and since the Act of Union the population had declined by 20 percent. The constant flow of emigration to other countries, particularly non-British, proved that their people did not find happiness under the English Govern- ment. That could not be traced to any inherent dislike for the English. He asked the English people to try I and realize how they would have felt if the position bad been reversed. Mr. RIGBY (N. West Belfast) seconded and maintained that industry and agriculture in Ireland had been ,istrangled under English rule. Landlordism had been the curse of Ireland and landlordism was a product of the English Government. Mr. E. GORDON Duna ES'S (Chief Secretary for Ireland) said anything that threatened the unity of the United Kingdom and the consolidation of the Empire must necessarily have opposition from the members on that sideof the House. They were striving to do their best for Ireland and if the Irish Party would only support them they would give them many solutions to their dilliculties. The amendment was then put, and Mr. AWDERY (L.,</p>
        <p>:.-;:..7.-..i.r .:- : '. -''''..''''---i ..i •. - - i'',:::''','Z:_':.7.7:l l'::'7,-;:.</p>
        <p>Dover) challenged a division. The amendment • defeated by 45 votes to 33. IMr. R. G. PIERPOINT (L.,E3St lianchester) continued the debate and said the Governinent had not so much 1 backbone as they thought they had. He was glad, how— ever, that the member for Newport had admitted that, his party was tied to the beer barrel. (~ N i No, no „ from o the Government.) With regard to the position n India the white man there looked down upon the native as a dog. Were he (the speaker) a native,. and had ' I received a European education like many of the natives : have received, he would go back to India and raise up a 'rebellion (" Shame " from the Hinisterial benches.) He I need not refer to Ireland after the amendment. 1 There were several loud " hear, hears "to which the 1 member for East Manchester heatedly retorted: " What 1 are you ' hear, hearing' about ?" The SPEAKEft : The hon. member is not in order in addressing the House in that tone of voice: . Mr. R. G. PIERPOIST : When I get sensible 11:4errup : tions, I will try to treat them with courtesy. Continuing ! he wanted to know whether the Prime Minister could i be looked upon as the leader of the Tariff Reform Party. !Protection, he contended, was brought forward as an ! election dodge to cover up the Boer War. (Hear, hear). It came from Birmingham, its false which was noted for ifalse jewellery and was brought forward by a man whose coat was of many colours. (Laughter.) When the Government members said Tariff Reform was "work for all " he looked upon it as dishonesty coupled with foolishness. Wlinn Mr. Pierpoint resumed his seat, the SPEAKER. administered a stern rebuke to someone who had called out "sit down." "it I hear any hon. member ask another member to sit down, in that tone of voice " said the Speaker, " I shall immediately name him." , Mr. G. F. TAYLOR (Attorney General), said the absence of Mr. Upson from the leadership of the other side must prove a great loss to the House in general. ‘\ ith reFard to the Liberals having shown them " how to pass Bills " he claimed that they had passed those measures with the help of other parties. The Con- servatives would rather go out of office than pander to other sections of the House. The hon. member described as "vague and archaic" the arguments advancsd in support of the Home Rule amendment, and said they were based on what occurred seventy or eighty Years ago. The address was then adopted without a dissentient. rote.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>NEW LIBERAL LEADER.</head>
        <p>NEW LIBERAL LEADER.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Liberal members of the Parlia- mentary Debating Society was held at Atkinson's Caf6 on IVednesday, Mr. W. Furness presiding. A letter was read from Mr. John Upson expressing regret that, owing to pressure of work, he could not continue the leader- ship of the party in the local Parliament.--A vote of thanks was accorded to him for his able services, and the meeting expressed sincere regret that he was unable to retain the office.—A vote of thanks was also passed to Mr. J. F. Awbery for his work as deputy leader, and Mr. Awbery suitably acknowledged the compliment.— Mr. E. Wynne-Jones was asked to become the leader of the Opposition, and Mr. W. Furness the deputy lender. Mr. J. Howes, junr., and Mr. C. 11. Johnstone were re-appointed Whips, and Mr. Johnstone was elected the representative Whip on the Council of the Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>FEDERATION OF THE EMPIRE.</head>
        <p>FEDERATION OF THE EMPIRE.</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN BENNETI"S TARIFF REFORNI BILL. Particular interest centres in not Tu,3sclay evening's meeting of the Society as Alderman Bennett, Colonial S,3cretary, will introdce his eagerly-anticipated Bill For the Federation of the Empire. The following is the text of the measure :</p>
        <p>A BILL TO PEDER,ATE TUC MIME AND IMPOSE AN lUPERIAL TARIFF Fait DEFENCE. Be it enacted, &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>IMPERIAL CABINET. L That an Imperial Cabinet be constituted consisting of the Premier of the United Islingdorn, the Foreign, Indian and Colonial Secretaries, the Minister for War, thd First Lord of the Admitilty, the President of the Board of Trade, the Postmaster-General, one repreaenta- ttve of the Dominion of Canada, OEI3 representative of the Commonwellth of Australia, one representative of the Dominion of Nev Zealand, one representative of British South Africa, and one representative of the Empire of India, or such of them as intimate their assent to the proposed arrangement. 2. That such Imperial Cabinet be authorized to administer the fund for Imperial Defence, hereinafter provided for, and to deAl with any other questions of , purely imperial importance subject to the approval of their respective Governments.</p>
        <p>DIPERIAL TARIFF FOR DEFENCE:. 3. That a special ./ va m i.ore tax of five per !lent— over and above existing duties be levied in a the ports of the United Kingdom upon all foreign goods with the exception of such food supplies or raw 1 material as cannot advantageously be produced within the limits of the Empire, and upon the goods of any Colony or Possession which is not prepared to levy a similar tax. 4. That the proceeds of this special tax, wherever levied, be administer3d by thelinperial Cabinet for pur- poses of Imperial Defence. 5. That any dissentient state shall be entitled to representation in the Imperial Cabinet, but shall not participate in the preference afforded by the special tax. -C. That tha tax may be increased providad that each increase is accompanied by a proportionate decrease in the duties at present levied by the assenting states as between each other. 7. That similar fiscal privileges may be accorded to any foreign state which is willing to reciprocate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>111.</head>
        <p>111.</p>
        <p>IMPERIAL HOUSE OF LORDS. S. That no hereditary peers shall be created aft° the first of January next. O. That the maximum number of members of the House of Lords shall be fixed at six hundred. 10. That the existing hereditary peers shall, at the commencement of each Parliament, elect one third of their number to represent them therein. 11. That the following shall also be entitled to a seat therein:—</p>
        <p>(a) Princes of the Blood Royal ; (b) Any person who has, at any time, acted as Premier of the United Kingdom or any of the self-governhw colonies, Viceroy of India, Governor General of &amp;nada, High Commissioner or South Africa or Lord Lieutenant of Ireland ; (c) The Lords of Appeal ; . (d) Thirty-six representatives of the Church of England, the Church of Rome, the Estabished Church of Scotland and the Free Churches selected by each in proportion to its numerical strength. 12. That the remaining members shall consist of Life Peers chosen in eqaitablo proportions from all parts of the Empire, on the recommendation of the Respective Governments, for exceptional ability, experi- ence or public service in any department of national or imperial life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>PARLIAMENT 07 THE UNITED RINGDOII.</head>
        <p>PARLIAMENT 07 THE UNITED RINGDOII.</p>
        <p>13. That, on the first of January, 1910, a Parliament of the United Kingdom be established with power to deal with all matters exclusively affecting England, S.,cotland, Ireland and %Vales, such Parliament to con- sist of two Chambers, the first elected for not more than three years in succession by the constituencies which elect the Imperial House of Commons, and the second for six succes7ive years by the Cuuaty and County Borough Councils of England, Scotland, Ireland and Wales, each of which shall be eatitled to one representative. 14. That the aforesaid Parliament shall appoint a Cabinet, to consist of such Ministers as it may consider ' necessary. . 15. That such Parliament shall have full power, from time to time, to delegate any of its purely local func- tions to National Committees, or to Provintial or National Councils duly constituted for the purpose and that, from and after its election, the existing imperial Parliament shall devote its attention to imperial affairs, and that the Ministers of the existing iCabinet whose official duties are confined to the United Kingdom shall there-upon vacate their appointments.</p>
        <p>IMPERIAL HOUSE OF COADIONS.</p>
        <p>15. That from and after the first of January, 1015, the constitution of the House of Commons a hall be re-adjusted, its under : (11) The total membership shall not exceed 570. (b) Seventy seats shall be allotted to India and the Crown Colonies. (c) The remaining 600 seats shall be allotted to the United Kingdom and such of the self-governing Colonies as have signified their intention to adopt this Bill, the proportion of members to he allotted to each to be mutually decided upon from time to time. 17. That each part of the Empire shall elect or nominate its representatives in the manner each con- siders best adapted to the local conditions. is. That each Imperial Parliament shall meet in London, but that no Imperial Parliament shall sit for a longer term than six successive years. 19. That the Imperial Cabinet bereinbefore provided for shall be superseded, on the first of January, 1915, by a Cabinet selected by the Imperial House of Commons, who shall thenceforth have supreme control of our Imperial affairs and policy, it being a fundamental con- dition that purely local rights and privileges shall be strictly maintained.</p>
        <p>VI. IMPERIAL COURT OF APPEAL. 20. Any dispnte as to the interpretation of this Bill, or between the various assenting states, shall be decided by the Imperial Court of Appeal.</p>
        <p>The prodieal son, being tired of fun, Was fed up with his freedom—not half ! For a bad cold he'd got, so returned to the cot, With an eye to a fine fatted calf! But the old man, he said," Wotto, Bill ! ain't yet. dead? Got a cold, well, that's careless, I'm sure, It won't run to roast veal, but your cold you can heal. If you buy some Woods' Peppermint Cure."—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>PAINS IN THE LOINS AND BACK,</head>
