        <p>gain the confidence of each other and to be worthy of it. He asked the meeting whether they thought that England would be in the condition th tt it is to-day if the workingruon of En4lanl —the democracy—had used their own thinking piwers instead of letting others think for them. (Voices No !) The past was gone, the present was with them and the future was before them. They did not know what the future would bring, but this they did know : they could not all he heroes and thrill the atmosphere with some great daring venture but they could fill a lifetime with kindly efforts, and true. There was always noble service for noble souls to do. (Applause.) Mr. J. A. RANsoNtE, president of the school, said they must not lack unity. They . were working for the success of the school in the work of God.</p>
        <p>Dr. BrtowN, of Nfanchester,and formerly of W:trring- ton, said that they required to have broadmindedness in their schools.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. MORTON moved a rote of thanks to the chair- man,speakers and the artistes and it was scowled by Mr. lIARot,o GRAY.</p>
