      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>WARRINGTON ADULT SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON ADULT SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Meeting in Wycßite Memorial Hall.</p>
        <p>The first year's existence of the adult school, whose meetings are held each Sunday morning in the Ca KAcedemy-street, has proved to be a successful one: end to celebrate this fact a conference and public meeting were held in the Memorial flail of the Wycliffe Church last Saturday.—The Conference took place in the after- noon, and was presided over by Mr. J. A. Ransome, the local president.—The reports of the different officials were read, and ali exceeded the expectation of the class. In the reports it is stated that the school has an average attendance of 4n, which in itself manifests a keen desire on the part of workingmen for knowledge. There is a balance in hand of £1 Cs. ld.—Mr. Boyd, the organizing secretary of the Yorkshire Adult School Union, gave some very encouraging remarks to the members.—After the Conference about 90 members and friends sat down</p>
        <p>to at; enjoyable tea. In the evening the public meeting and concert was presided over by Mr. C. E.Perker.who was supported on the platform by Rev. J. F. Matthews, Mr. J. A. Ransorne, De. Brown, air. Boyd, Mrs. Ransome and Miss Yonde (Stockport). THEFT" OF A HORSE CLOTH. The Rev. J. F. MArrnews said his first word wasl Sarah Ann F,,st, of no settled address, was charged at the Borough Police Court on Saturday, with stealing e of congratulation that at last shortlythey had an adult II : from a cab standing in Crown-street, a horse cloth school in this town, and he hoped very the other section of the adult school movement, which valued at Is.. the property of Thomas Drinkwater. , was already pertly in inception, the women's side. Ills Prisoner pleaded not guilty.—The Chief Constable second word was also one of congratulation, that they stated that the owner of the cab was a Mr. Drink water, had already reached an average of between 40 end 50 residing in Ilaydock-street. About 1.39 on Monday afternoon he had occasion to leave the cab for a fe s• members. The Adult School was to make up, where the Sunday School lacked. The Adult School differed minutes. The rug was then thrown over the horse. Five from the P.S.A. radically. The P.S.A. was becoming : minutes later he returned and found the ruF missing. From information he received he went to Mr. Taylor's a sort of semi-concert. but he did not condemn it, in fact he did not condemn anything. That movement marine stores and found the rug on the counter. It was also an educational one. In Sheffield, whither in appeared that the prisoner went to Mr. Taylor's stores a short time he would be going, they started a school and offered what Mr. Taylor took to be a bundle of rags. 01 years ago, and in it they were taught to read and He gave prisoner lAA for them. lie afterwards found write. The religions element in the movement was one it was the rue the subject of the charge. Thomas which seemed to him to be worthy of co