      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>PARR'S BANK, LIMITED. ;</head>
        <p>PARR'S BANK, LIMITED. ;</p>
        <p>Director's Report.</p>
        <p>The 44th annual ordinary general meeting of this company, will he held at the Cannon-street Hotel. London, on Thursday, the 28th inst., at one o'clock, The following is the report of the directors : The directors, in submitting to the shareholders the balance sheet of the bank at 31st December, 1908, certified by the auditors,have to report that the position and prospects of the business continue to be in every way satisfactory. During the past six months the directors have acquired by purchase the old-established business of Messrs. Robin Brothers (Jersey Commercial Bank), Jersey. The amalgamation of the Whitehaven Joint Stock Banking Company Limited with this Com- pany has been successfully carried out. The authorized capital has been increased to £10,000,000, the issued capital to £8,898,900, the paid-up capital to £1,779,780 and the ri serve fund to £1,779,780. The balance brought forward at 30th June, 1968, including £2,874 4s. sd. by the Whitehaven Joint Stock Banking Company Limited was ...... £169,959 17 10 From this sum the directors have, in _ _ _</p>
        <p>connection with the issue of new shares upon the amalgamation with the Whitehaven Joint Stock Bank- ing Company Limited, transferred to reserve fund, to maintain that fund on an equality with the increased paid-up capital 43,280 0 0</p>
        <p>£126,679 17 10</p>
        <p>Including this sum the net balance of profits for the half-year, after writing down investments to below market value, providing for all bad and doubtful debts and rebating the current bills at five per cent. is.. . From this sum the directors recommend The payment of a half- year's dividend at the rate of 19 per cent. per annum payable, free of income tax, on 4th February £169,07- 2 0 A bonus of one per cent. payable free of income tax, on 4th February 17,797 16 0 The transfer to bank premises account The transfer to Officers' Pension and Provi- dent Fund</p>
        <p>Leaving balance to be carried to next account</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>206,876 18 0</head>
        <p>206,876 18 0</p>
        <p>£127,733 19 0</p>
        <p>A sub-blanch of the bank has been opened at Wood- house Eaves, Leicestershire, and a branch will shortly be opened at Golder's Green, N.W. The directors record with drep regret the loss the Board has suntained by the decease of Sir J. Christopher Gamble, Bart., Hr. Hussey Packe, and Sir Ewen Cameron, K.C.M.G. The directors who retire this time by rotation are Mr. Thomas H. Dixon, Mr. Cecil F. Parr, the Right Hon. Lo rd Farquhar, Mr.A.J. Fraser, Mr. S. J.Fraser and Mr. P. if. Eckersley,all of whom are eligible for re-election. The Auditors, Messrs. Turquand, Youngs and Company, London ; Messrs. Stead, Taylor and Stead, Liverpool ; and Messrs. Alfred Tongue and Company, Manchester, retire, and offer themselves for re-election. CECIL F. PARR, Chairman. 4, Bartholomew-lane, London, E.C. 18th January, 1909.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>WORTHLESS CHARACTERS.</head>
        <p>WORTHLESS CHARACTERS.</p>
        <p>Scene in the Warrington Police Court.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court, on Thursday, Edward Mullen, Dial-court, John Hargreaves, Bank-street, and Thomas Cummins, Rose and Crown-street, were charged with stealing from the Manchester Clothing IX-pot, Horsemarket-street, on Saturday afternoon, an overcoat valued at 17s. 6d. ; further with stealing from outside the Manchester Warehouse, Buttermarket-street, a roll of cloth valued at 40s. Mullen was also charged with wilfully breaking a glass at the Central El tel and also with assaulting Detective Sergeant Meabock and Detective Reddington. Hargreaves pleaded guilty to stealing the cloth. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable referring to the theft of the coat stated that a boy employed by Mr. Bayley Isaac, was in Lyme-street when he saw the prisoner Mullen with the coat over his arm. The prisoner's cap fell off and at the same moment the boy saw a ticket on the coat marked " reduced 12s. 6d." NlLllen went down Golden Grove-street and the boy who was on his way to the Farmer's Stores met Mullen again who asked him where his (prisoner's) cap was. The boy showed prisoner where it was,and asked him what was the matter and the prisoner replied " I have lost my scarf." The boy saw prisoner go into the Central Hotel and come out with the other two prisoders. They afterwards returned to the Central Hotel again. The landlord saw all three persons acting in a suspicious manner and subsequently found the coat in the back yard, George Robson, assistant at the Manchester Clothing Depot, $ tid at 4.30 the coat was hanging safely outside, and an hour later it wan mieeing.in the meantime it had been recovered by the police and he identified it as the stolen article, lie valued it at 17s. 6d. Horace Holmes (15), corroborated the Chief Constables statements and said that the prisoner Mullen told him nct to say anything about the matter, and when witness asked him what it was he replied that he had lost his scarf.</p>
        <p>John Berry, landlord of the Central Hotel, said that about five o'clock on the date in question his boy came to him and told him that three men were in the back ard and had stolen a coat. He went out and saw Hargreaves and Mullen looking in a trap under the shed. Witness sent them out and afterwards found the stolen overcoat under the trap. When he got back into the bar the three prisoners were drinking. The prisoner Mullen deliberately smashed a glass on the floor. Witness gave information to P.C. Burrows. Mullen denied smashing the glass wilfully. Sergeant Meacock stated that he went in search of the prisoner Mullen about 7 o'clock tbe same night and found him in his house in Dial-court. When Nleacock went to arrest him prisoner shouted " You are not coming in here without a warrant. Witness took him into custody whereupon he became very violent,kicking witness several times. Witness had to secure the assistance of Detective Reddington and with considerable difficulty conveyed prisoner to the Police Station. Witness charged him the next day and he replied that he knew nothing about it. He afterwards charged the other two prisoners and Ilargreaves replied " Not guilty," and Cummins "Not guilty to the overcoat." The charge of stealing the cloth was next gone into. The Chief Constable stated that the cloth was fastened outside the premises and about six o'clack it was missed. The value of it was estimated at £2,</p>
        <p>Clara Forster, living on Cockhedge, said she saw the prisoners Hargreaves and Mullen with the roll of cloth about 6.30 the sane day. Mullen was trying to throw it over his shoulder but it was too heavy.</p>
        <p>Hargreaves (to the Magistrates) : Why I could bare thrown it over the Police Station with you (meaning the Chief Constable) on the top. During the time the evidence was being given, Har- greaves was continually interrupting, and talking to the other two prisoners and had frequently to be called to order by the constable in the dock. P.C.'s Holland and Hewitt gave evidence of the arrest of Cummins and Hargreaves. A written statement from Mullen was read by the Clerk. It stated that he was drunk when he committed the offences, and it made him entirely unconscious of what he was doing. lie had made three attempts on his life, once with a revolver,once with a knife, and on one occasion by lying on the engine lines at a certain forge when tha engine was coming along. He hoped the magistrates would deal leniently with him, as it was hard to lead a straight life whets he came out of prison again. _ .</p>
        <p>„ - Hargreaves said he was guilty of stealing the cloth, the other prisoners had nothing to do with it. Corntnins said he knew nothing about the overcoat. While the Bench were deliberating, Hargreaves used the most insulting language to the Chief Constable. When asked by the magistrates if he would extend any leniency to any of the prisoners the Chief Constable remarked that Cummins should be dealt with leniently as he had been drawn into it by the other two, lie described them as villains, good for nothing worthy villains who never did any work. Mullen was sentenced to twelve months hard labour.</p>
        <p>On hearing this Hargreaves shouted "go on give us twelve months each." Hargreaves was sentenced to six months and Cummins to one month.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (J. Fairclough) to Hargreaves : I strongly Advise you to keep out of Warrington, on your lituration.</p>
        <p>Mu! lens muttered about a charge of larceny not being correct, and on being BE'ted to step down below he clutched the dock rails. Two brawny policemen,however, seized him by the coat collar and removed him forcibly down Lelow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>HIS SIXTY-FIFTH APPEARANCE.</head>
        <p>HIS SIXTY-FIFTH APPEARANCE.</p>
        <p>j Thomas Tye made his sixty-fifth appearance at St. ! Helens Police Court last Saturday on a charge of begging. He was described as a dangerous sort of character, who wonld not work. He Lad gone into a photographer's on Friday, and could not be got rid of until something had been given bim. Ho was sent to prison for three months with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>ST. HELENS LIBRARY COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS LIBRARY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>I At the meeting be the St. Helens Library Committee, on Tuesday, Alderman H. Martin was appointed chair- man in succession to tLe late Mr. H. B. Lacey, who had held the position ever since the institution of the com- mittee.—Mr. U. W. U. Thomas, LL.B„ was elected deputy-chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>COMPULSORY MILITARY TRAINING.</head>
        <p>COMPULSORY MILITARY TRAINING.</p>
        <p>ARTICLE I.</p>
        <p>The Proposals of the National Service [League.</p>
        <p>A FABLE. Aesop tells a fable about a boar, who was sharpen- ing his tusks against the trunk of a tree. A fox seeing this, asked the boar why he did so at a time when there were no hunters about. " That's quite true," said the boar, "but when there are hunters about, I shall not have time to sharpen my tusks."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>MORAL.</head>
        <p>ThE MORAL.</p>
        <p>The old fable of Aesop is applicable to modern England. The tusks of Great Britain are certainly not sharpened ; in fact they are not even strong and full grown. Where is the irmy for home defence ? The Regular Army is not a force to be tied to apron in trings at home. It has its work to do outside England. The real army for home defence is the Territorial one- undermanned, starved, partially trained, but composed of the few patriotic men, who are ready to work while the others shirk. But the Territorial tusks are only to be sharpened after the commencement of war. Mr. Haldane proposes a six months' training not before but after. the hunters are about. Mr. Haldane and the public might take a straight tip from the wise old Greek, Aesop. The tip is, "See to your army before the enemy appears."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>REMEDY.</head>
        <p>THE REMEDY.</p>
        <p>A conviction is now growing among thinking men, irrespective of creed and party, that the voluntary system alone cannot, and ought not to be depended upon to provide for the defence of this country, that " nation in arms" which Mr. Haldane has rightly set before him as his ideal. The Norfolk Commission recommended that the army for home defence should be " raised or maintained on the principle that it was the duty of every citizen of military age and sound physique to be trained for national defence." But the Government, the Press, the people took no notice. Nit.. Haldane dared once to ask for 900,000 men. Then he moderated his demands and asked for 300,000. By dint of personal speeches, whittling away of rules of discipline, baits of prizes, pretty uniforms, and all the other attractions the costs of which comes largely upon the Territorial officers, over 200,000 men have at last been persuaded to come forward and do their duty —a duty which should be shared by all who are fit for it. I Upon the Territorials in case of emergency will fall a heavy burden. " Mobilise " will flash over the wires. Next morning Sergeant Jones will be on the way to Aldershot, and Jones' employer will be tearing his hair at the unexpected loss of a good foreman. There will be terrible dislocation of busi- ness when the six months training does begin. Under compulsory military training the burden would not fall on a few, but on all, capitalist and foreman alike. Equal burdens break no backs. Accordingly the National Service League, the only organisation of the kind in Great Britain, is advocating .universal service, so that we may have a real nation In arms,' so that our homes may be secure, so that we may not tempt other nations to walk in and fill their pockets. But what exactly does the League propose ? How would its proposals affect for example Tom Brown, aged 17, clerk in a wood yard, now living at 21, Paradise- street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>PROPOSALS.</head>
        <p>THE PROPOSALS.</p>
        <p>1. Sound physical development for every British boy in every British school. 2. The inculcation of lessons of patriotism awl devotion to the country as part of the education of every British boy and girl. 3. The encouragement of rifle shooting for all boys and youths up to the age of 18. 4. A preliminary training of four months at the age of 18 followed by short annual camp trainings for the next four years. _ _ _</p>
        <p>It is not: intended, however, that any liability ai to training or service shall be enforced on anyone above the age of 18 at the time of the passing of the Act f Compulsory Training. Such liability would only come into 'force after the Act is passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>AN EXAUPLE</head>
        <p>AN EXAUPLE</p>
        <p>Now suppose an Act had passed through Parlia- ment to the effect that every British boy must be drilled at school and every lad, if physically fit, at 18 go into camp for four months. What about Tom Brown ? Next May, Torn will be 18 years old, and supposing the*Act is in force he will go before a doctor, who will thump his chest and make sure that he is a strong lad. Then Tom will go into camp— possibly somewhere on the sea coast of Lancashire, not too far from home. He will get his uniform free ; board and lodging free ; and pocket money into the bargain. He will have to work hard at his training of course, but he will be out of doors in the fresh air stoked with good food, untroubled by responsibilities, surrounded by comrades of his own age, with six- pence a day in his pocket. Discipline and order will mould his character for good, just as his slouch- ing pigeon-chested body will be moulded into that of a well set up muscular man. In the middle of September he will return home. His employer will be compelled by law to take him back at the woodyard. After this free holiday he will be more fit and clpable to do his work both as a citizen and as a father. Also these proposals if adopted will ensure the presence in the country at all times of at least a citizen militia —men trained, ready, efficient. So both the Torn Browns and the country will benefit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>IS THIS NONSENSE?</head>
        <p>IS THIS NONSENSE?</p>
        <p>Some readers may accuse all the above as being the product of mere imagination. It is not. It as a prophecy of the future founded on the past experience of other nations. Tom Brown's real name is Adolph Simon. He is a Swiss and all the Swiss are trained as citizen soldier.q. The above description is that of Adolph's life in camp. He declares it was one of the jolliest times in his life. I cannot understand why the English boy should not enjoy military training just as much and derive just as much physical and moral good from the drill and discipline. Great Statesmen like Lord Curzon and Lord .Milner, Socialists like Mr. Harry Quelch and Mr. H. G. Wells, our leading army and naval men, students of social welfare like the Rev. R. J. Campbell and T. C. Horsfall, believe that compulsory military training will do much for national security and national well- being.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>READER'S PART.</head>
        <p>THE READER'S PART.</p>
        <p>Now readers know exactly what the National Service League proposes, viz.: I. Military drill in all schools. 11. Four month's training at the age of 18 in camp. 111. Four years attached to the Territorials with a fortnight each year in camp. Next week I hope to explain why at this stage of English history it is necessary to introduce compulsory training and the following week I shall try to show the advantage of such a system. I hope those who dis- agtee will write to the Editor. We welcome adverse criticism and possibly the Editor will allow me space later to answer. objections.. Major S•rachan, 37, Imperial Buildings, Liverpool, Organizing Secretary of N.S.L. for Lancashire, will be glad to forward literature to anyone who wishes to study more deeply the most vital questions at present before the British race in so far as it affects closely every man's home and family and country. _ _ _</p>
        <p>B. S. TOWNROE. 4, Winmarleigh-street, Warrington. _ _</p>
        <p>[ rhe above is the first of a short series of articles in favour of Compulsory Military Training which we publish at Mr. Townroe's request.—ED. "Ex."]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>CROPS THAT PAID WELL.</head>
        <p>CROPS THAT PAID WELL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ernest Parke again records satisfactorily remunerative results from the manurial experiments carried out on his farm at Kinston with the co-opera- tion of Dr. Dyer. The season has locally been particu- larly favourable for mangolds, which in this case were Orange Globes, following a similar crop. Even the unmanured plot gave this year over 20 tons of mangolds per acre. Superphosphate (4cwt. per acre) and nitrate of soda (2cwt.) raised the yield to 39 tons, while when , the nitrate 'ens increased to 4cwt. the yield rose to 47i tons, and to as much as 56 tons where the dressing of nitrate was increased to 6csvt. On these three plots , the increase in crop attributable to the fertilisers was ! thus 18 tons, 27 tons, and 35 tons per acre respectively, I while the corresponding cost for superphosphate and nitrate of soda producing the increase was 345., 585., and 82s. per acre, the average cost of the extra roots working out at under 2s. 6d. a ton, plus the cost of pulling and carting. This was very similar to last year's result, save that this year the crop was heavier. Oats (following beans) have again turned out well. The unmanured land gave 50 bushels per acre with 22cwt. of straw. Superphosphate alone (3cwt. per acre) increased the oats to G7i bushels, and superphosphate and 1 cwt. of nitrate gave 75i bushels, with 33cwt. of straw, while an additional top dressing of a further hundredweight of nitrate raised the crop 1 to 86 bushels of oats and 40cwt. of straw. On this last plot the increase due to manuring was 36 bushels, or 4i quarters of oats and nearly a ton of straw, in retttrn for an outlay of about 325. in fertilisers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Free Church Council.</p>
        <p>i The annual interchange of pulpits n connection with the Warrington and District Free Church Council took place on Sunday at 19 different places of worship in Warrington and district. In the evening a United Communion Service was held in Golborne-street Baptist Church. The Rev. R. E. Letheren presided and an address was delivered by the Rev. T. Miller.</p>
        <p>Holy Trinity Church. At the Liverpool Consistory Court on Wednesday, the Rev. Edward Ellis Carleton, vicar, and Messrs. William Gill and W. Shirlaw, churchwardens, of Holy Trinity Church, Warrington, applied for permission to erect a vestry for clergy at the south-west corner of the church, and to open an archway through the outer wall of the church, between the present clergy vestry and the pro- posed new vestry, to take down the existing clergy vestry and erect a porch in lieu thereof, so as to pro- vide an additional entrance to the church. The estimated cost, £l5O, would be defrayed by voluntary contributions. A faculty was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>St Peter's Church.</head>
        <p>St Peter's Church.</p>
        <p>At this church on Sunday sermons on behalf of the Church Pastoral-Aid Society, were preached by the Rev and Hon. C. F. Cross, vicar of Strettou, who said the object of the soci,ty was the evangilization of England through the parochial system of the National Church. On Monday in the schoolroom, John-street, presided over by Colonel Buckton, who spoke in high terms of the work of the society, of which they bad evidence in this parish. Without this and other outside assistance it would be quite impossible to carry on efficiently the spiritual work of so large a parlih. Ernest and inter- esting addresses, with personal experience, were also given by the Rev. J. H. Garnett, curate, Captain Lund, C.A. Mr. Wratislow, and the Vicar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>PRIZE COMPETITION AWARD.</head>
        <p>PRIZE COMPETITION AWARD.</p>
        <p>A sum of £2O has been cil;44e-d-am0ng5t............ by Danysz Virus Ltd. (52, Leadenhall-street, London, E.C.) The word" Noratzeen " has been registered as a trade mark for Danysz Virus for the extermination of rats and mice. No competitor sent in this particular word, but words closely resembling it were received from Mr. John Gavin, Southwark Park-road, Bermondsey, S.E., and from Nlr. Albert Singer, 21, St. Mary's-road, Hoe-street, Walthamstow. The competitors sent in the words " Noratzine "and "Noratzene" respectively, and to each the sum of £5 has been awarded for submitting words, which in the opinion of Danysz Virus Ltd. deserved a prize, a further sum of £lO has been divided amongst some twenty other competitors. Particulars of further Prize Competition will shortly be announced by Danysz Virus Ltd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>FELL OFP THE BOX.</head>
        <p>FELL OFP THE BOX.</p>
        <p>Martin Mannion, a cab driver, who appeared in the dock with his head swarthed in bandages was charged at the Borough'Police Courtion Tuesday with being drunk in charge of a horse and cab in Wilderspool-road.—P.C. Fish stated that he was on duty at a quarter to six that morning when he saw the prisoner driving a cab along Wilderspool-road. He was very drunk. After the con- veyance passed witness, prisoner fell off the box and under the, wheel. Luckily the horse stopped, and prisoner was not injured beyond a severe cut on the face. He was conveyed to police stition and afterward to the infirmary where his wounds were dressed, and he was afterwards locked up. Prisoner said he was not drunk. He had not had any sleep for two nights. He was fined ss. and costs.</p>
        <p>After</p>
        <p>Years</p>
        <p>uffering with</p>
        <p>AIY DEAR CHILDREN,</p>
        <p>I must first of all wish you all a very Happy New Year. I hope you will all have a nice time in the future. It has just struck me that I should like to have my members' ideas of the best means of making other People happy. I will give a P.O. for 26 to the member whose letter I consider the best written on the above subject. .Just sit down and think what you could do to make others happy, and, even if you are unable to carry out the idea yourself, tell me what you would do if you could. Perhaps some of you have younger brothers and sisters, who find it very difficult to learn their home lessons unaided. Do you think it would be kind, and would make them happy, to give a few minutes' help to make it easy for them? I think you would find heaps of ways of carrying out some little kindness which will help someone's happiness. I want you to write me what you consider would be a kind action.</p>
        <p>Alany thanks to Mary E. Parker for gifts for the Poor. I have sent a mirror to Mary Holt. With love, UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>MliktNE</p>
        <p>" It's S 3 hard to be sick with the mumps, mamma! Tell me a story. A make-believe one that just couldn't happen! That will make me forget." And mamma took Donald on her lap and told him this story:</p>
        <p>Once there was a little boy who was lame, and very, very tired of keeping still.</p>
        <p>" It was a summer day, warm and beautiful. Overhead the clouds were so white and fleecy in the blue sky, and they floated so dreamily that the little boy's eyes followed them "' I wish I could sail a little way on. one!'</p>
        <p>"As he looked he thought, .` I will sail on one,' and he seized his little crutch and very slowly climbed a high hill at the back of his house.</p>
        <p>"The clouds floated at the very top, he knew, and when 4 reached it, a dear little. cloud was there waiting for him. "Up, over, in, he was nestled in the soft white cloud and ready to sail. " The air was cooler than on the earth, but he pulled a bit of cloud round him, and it was soft as a ramb's back. "And he sailed—far, far away! "Past green fields and over thick forests, over bright rivers and lakes, and his cheeks grew pink and his eyes shone like stars at all he saw.</p>
        <p>"And then it began to grow dark. "Lovely colours were in the sky, which changed to deep, dull blues—and then a tiny moon crept out and a few beautiful stars, and then this little cloud passenger grew sleepy and hungry, too, and said, Now, it is time for me to go back to my own mother.' "And it was wonderful all the time, for the cloud dropped lower, lower, until it was again at the top of the hill behind this little boy's house, and he softly slid out and over, and went down the hill to his own mother. There, that. is a story that couldn't have happened! Did it make you forget, Donald?" asked mother. But Donald was asleep. Another little passenger was starting toward the land of happy dreams.</p>
        <p>KATHERINE B. WALKER. NEW EVERY MORNING.</p>
        <p>Every day is a fresh beginning, Every morn is the world made new, You who are weary of sorrow and sinning, Here is a beautiful hope for you— A hope for me and a hope for you. All the past things are past and over, The tasks are done and the tears are shed, Yesterday's errors let yesterday cover; Yesterday's wounds, which smarted and bled, Are healed with the healing which night has shed. Yesterday now is a part of for ever, Bound up in a sheaf which God holds tight; With glad days and sad days and bad days which never Shall visit us more with their bloom and their blight, Their fulness of sunshine or sorrowful night. Let them go since we cannot revive them, Cannot undo, and cannot atone; God in His mercy receive, forgive them, Only the new days are our own, To-day is ours and to-day alone. SUSAN COOLIDGE. THE ADVENTURE OF A MOUSE.</p>
        <p>A mother-mouse, when her children had nearly reached the age at which it became time for them to seek their own fortunes in the world, cautioned them particularly against the traps and dangers that would lie in their paths. "My children," said she, "the cheese looks ;very tempting, and is even sometimes toasted, but beware of it; for it will bring mis- fortune to you." One time the whole family of younger mice came upon a trap. "This, I suppose," said the eldest and wisest, "is the trap against which our mother so carefully warned us. And yet," continued he, "the cheese looks • very tempting. I doubt extremely if there be any real danger in it. And even if there be, I think that, by a proper amount of self-control and wariness, one might avoid all ill-conse- quences. Because sonic have been caught, it does not necessarily follow that a like fate must overtake all. At least, I shall inspect the trap to satisfy myself whether there is really as much danger in it as our mother said. You know, she is apt to be over-cautious very often." And with this remark, in spite of the urgent warnings of his brothers, the over-wise mouse deliberately entered the trap. " I cannot see," said he, when he was within, "that there is any real danger, and it is very pleasant here. One need not eat of the cheese, you know." But even as he spoke the delicious smell of the cheese overcame his caution; he concluded there could be no danger in taking the smallest nibble. No sooner, however, had he touched the tempting morsel, than the trap fell, and he was a prisoner. "Alas I" said he to his weeping mother, who had hastened to the trap upon learning the fate of her son. " I now discover, when it is too late to repent, that the experience of age is safer than the presumptuous wisdom of youth."</p>
