      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>C DNTENTMENT.</head>
        <p>C DNTENTMENT.</p>
        <p>Some people are never content with their lot, let what will happen. Clouds and darkness are over their heads, alike whether it rain or shine. To them every incident is an accident, and every accident a calamity. Even when they have their own way they like it no better than your way, and, indeed, consider their most voluntary acts as matters of Compulsion. We saw a striking illustration the other day of the 'infirmity we speak of in the conduct of a child about three years old. fie was crying because his mother had shut the parlour door. "Poor thing," said a neighbour, compassionately, "you have shut the child out." " It's all the same to him," said the mother; "he would cry if I called him in and then shut the door. It's a. peculiarity of that boy, that if he is left. rather suddenly on either side of a door, he considers himself wronged, and rebels accord- ingly." There are older children who take the same view of things.</p>
        <p>THE WISE FLIES.</p>
        <p>A hungry spider made a web Of thread so very fine, • Your tiny fingers scarce could feel The slender -little line. Oh, what a pretty shining web It was when it was done, The 1 ttle flies all came to see It .hanging in the sun. • The hungry spider sat and watched The happy little flies. • It saw all round about its head, It had so many eyes. "I am getting very hungry," Said the spider to the fly, "If you would come into my house 'We'd have dinner, you and I." Round about, and round about, And round about went they, Across the web and back again, And then they flew away. • CHRISTMAS MORNING. It's very easy for big people to be Santa Claus, but it's not so easy for little ones, because they have to go to bed so early. I'd made mother a loNcely needle-book, and Syb I had worked a bookmarker for father. So, early on Christmas morning, we crept, ever so softly, and put them on mother's dressing-table. Couldn'toput them in the,stockings, 'cause there was none hung up. We- jumped into bed again, when, all of a sudden, the bel's began to ring, oh, lovely! We sat and listened. "Children! children!" mother called, "cover yourselves up, you'll catch cold." "Merry Christmas, mother!" cried Sybil, "have you found your presents? Daisy and me was Santa Claus!" Wasn't Sybil a goase! Who could fancy it was the real Santa Claus after that? But when father and mother kissed us for our presents they said Christmas fairies had brought them all the same. From Ernest George Coates, age 9. BABYLAND. There is a realm called Babyland—not very far away, Where dwell a race of tiny folk whose life is one long play; A land of broken dollies, and of headless horsemen bold, Where nobody has work to do, and nobody grows old. A. place where fairies are quite real, and "dollies talk and sing. Where buttercups are made of gold, and bluebells truly ring; Where poodle dogs and woolly lambs with melancholy looks Are taught their letters and their sums from tattered picture-books. We all have come from Babyland—that country dear and sweet, And some of us would fain turn back our old and weary feet— And play again with fairies, and with babies made of wood— In Mother's queen, and all her subjects good. Why is a pig's tail like the letter K?— Because it is the end of pork. Young Hopeful: "1 had a fight yesterday with the boy next door." Father: " Yes, his father called at my office Lo-day about it." Young Hopeful: " I hope you came out as well as I did."</p>
      </div>
