        <p>_ _ _ PARCEL POST. The rates of postage on parcels areas follows: For a parcel ... ... not exceeding 1 lb. 0 3 exceeding 1 lb. ~ 2 lb. 0 4 V/ I, ~2 lb. 11 3 lb. 0 5 f f f f 31b. f f 51b. 0 6 fl ff 51b. f f 71b. 0 7 ~ 7 lb. ~, 8 lb. 0 8 8 lb. ~ 9 lb. 0 9 1 f f f 9 lb. ~ 10 lb. 010 10 lb. t V 11 lb. 0 11 No parcel may exceed 111 b. in weight. The size allowed for an inland parcel is:— Greatestlength... ... ... ... 3 ft. 6 in. Greatest length and girth combined 6 ft. 0 in. For example—A parcel measuring 3ft. Gin, in its longest dimensions as much as 2ft. Gin, round its thickest part ; a short parcel may be thicker ; thus, if it measure no more than 3ft. in length, it may measure as much as 3ft. round its thickest part. The postage on a parcel must be prepaid. Mark each parcel " Parcel Post." A parcel must not be posted in a letter box, but presented at the counter of a post office, and the sender should see that the weight, Size and postage are in order before leaving. .....</p>
        <p>'PARCELS .(FOREIGN AND COLONIAL). Parcels are accepted for parcel post to most foreign countries under conditions similar to the inland regulations, except that for customs require- ments the contents must be declared. The customs charges can be prepaid or collected on delivery. 'The postage varies to different countries and must be prepaid. MONEY ORDERS (INLAND.) The postal authorities cannot issue a single money order for more than £4O, and will only cash them at the money order office at which they are made payable, unless they are crossed, when they can be paid through a bank. No fractional part of a id. can be contnined in a money order. The rates of poundage are :—Not exceeding £l, 21. ; £3, 3d.; £lO, 4d.: £2O, 6d. ; £3O, Bd. ; £4O, 10d. By paying an additional 2d. and cost of the telegram of advice, a money order can be telegraphed under certain restrictions. 111oNnY ORDERS (FOREIGN AND COLONIAL.) The maximum amount for which a money order may be issued for places abroad varies in each particular place. The poundage rates ate :—Not exceeding £l,3d. ; £2, 6d. ; and above that, 3d. for each additional £2 or portion of it. A fee of 6d. in addition to the ordinary poundage, together with cost of telegram of advice, enables money orders to be telegraphed to certain places abroad.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>POSTAL 'ORDERS.</head>
        <p>POSTAL 'ORDERS.</p>
        <p>These are obtainable at any money order office in the United Kingdom, and possess the advantage nye,- money orders that they can be cashed at any money order office. If crossea, tbuy t„ "id into a bank. They are issued for fixed amounts, rising by Gd., from 6d. to .£1 Is. The poundage fees are :—id. up to 2s. 6d.; Id. up to 155.; lid. Up to 21s. Before parting with a postal order, always fill in the name of the person to whom the amount is to be paid. Each order is numbered, as is also the counterfoil attached, which latter should be retained and name of payee filled in. The Post- master-General will entertain no further claim alter an order is once paid. Stamps to the amount of ad. can be affixed to make up odd sums.</p>
        <p>TELEGRAMS (INLAND). The Postmaster-General possesses,al most entirely, the privilege of transmitting,collecting and deliver- ing telegrams in the United Kingdom. The charge for a telegram is a minimum amount of 6d. which covers 12 words, and an additional id. /or each extra word. This charge includes delivery within the postal district if sent out for delivery from a head telegraph office, but if from a sub-office then within three miles only, beyond which a charge of 3d. per mile is made from the office door. All charges are, as far as possible, prepared by affixing stamps to telegram form. Address of person receiv- ing a telegram has to be paid for, but sender's name if written on back of form need not be. Five figures count as one word. The cost of a reply may be prepaid. Telegrams can be repeated on payment of half the original charge, which extra payment is refunded if error is detected. Telegrams may be posted in wall or pillar boxes, or handed to rural postmen, and are wired as soon as received at the nearest telegraph office. Late fees secure attention, in certain cases, to telegrams handed in after the, usual hours. The cost of registering an abbrevi- ated addresq, which must consist of two words, is Ll TELEGRAMS (FOREIGN AND COLONIAL) Must be written on special forms and are accepted in the language of any of the principal European countries. The rates vary, and are too numerous to detail here.</p>
        <p>POST OFFICE SAVINGS BANK. These are established at all money order offices. In ordinary cases the annual deposit must not exceed £5O, and the total amount £2OO. No person can have more than one account. Amounts placed in the bank on behalf of children under seven years of age cannot be withdrawn until the child reaches that age, and then only under his or her own signa- ture. The smallest amount received at a time is Is., and this may take the form of 12 penny stamps affixed to a special form. Each depositor receives a bank book in which the amount of each deposit should be entered at the time of paying in. If the deposit is £1 or over; a special acknowledgment is sent from the head London &amp;floe. Interest is allowed at the rate of 2i per cent. Any amount up to and including .£i may be withdrawn without Previous notice, on presenting a bank book. The bank book should be sent each year, postage free, on the anniversary of the date when the account as Opened, to tfoGrthpeurpCoosnetsroollfercohfectlkieinSgaavnindgasdldlainnk Departmentinterest. The withdrawal of money can bee xpeditedg by telegraphing,tel the depositor paying cost of ie):6Bn.';. ELF.PIIONE AND TRUNK LINE CIIARGES The fees for the follows : use of the Trunk lines are as For 25 miles or under „ 50 „ „ 100 „ „ every additional 40 miles or fr.actio.n.. 11- therso orifllo7weParnio"ra.nthde7s.a.an.ime.P6rdsi.cixe minutesl3etweecioi n vt ers,ht loounrsi as three minutes between 7 a.m. and 7 p.m., and a reduced charge is made for three minute calls.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>MOTOR CAR. AND CYCLE REOULATIONS</head>
        <p>MOTOR CAR. AND CYCLE REOULATIONS</p>
        <p>Each one must be registered with the council of a county orcounty borough, the fee being £1 fored motor car, and ss. for a motor cycle. A register a number isassigned to each, Which it is an offence not to keep affixed when driving. Each driver must possess a license, costing 55., to be renewed every 12 months, and obtainable from the county authorities, which may be endorsed or suspended for reckless driving or certain other offences. The highest speed at which a car may be driven on the public highways is 20 miles an hour, which is reduced to 10 miles under special local regula- tions.</p>
        <p>It is an offence under the Motor Car Act (1903), to drive a car in such a way on a public highway as Ito be dangerous to the public, having regard to all the circumstances of the case.</p>
        <p>In the event of an accident to any person due to the presence of the motor car on the road, the driver must supply his name and address, and the owner and number of the oar under heavy penalties. The driver must pro- duce his licence at the request of a constable.</p>
