        <p>nothall at the base of the pack, and proved themselves a reliable pair of reserve half-backs. Taylor, as usual, did a huge amount of work, and Cartwright fitted in fairly well. He wants speeding up a bit. Treharne was the best of the three-quarters, and played well throughout. Fish was poor, apart from his try, and did not look for the ball.</p>
        <p>I think most people were disappointed with the Trinity team. They have the reputation of being one of the smartest sides in Yorkshire, but they seemed a very ordinary lit on Saturday. The forwards were good, and the halves did smart things at times, but the third line was a long way inferior to that of War- rington. If our men are at full strength next Saturday when they return the call they should have a big chance of beating Wakefield again.</p>
        <p>The League table up to date is as follows : For Agst. Per</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. C. T. P. C. T. P. centage Oldham ...20 18 2 0 55 78 314 19 18 92 90-00 Wigan .19 17 2 0 75 98 444 25 21 122 99-47 Halifax ...20 17 3 0 51 79 339 17 22 100 85-00 Batley 20 14 5 1 28 49 203 22 28 128 72.50 WaltefildT2o 13 6 1 47 72 310 29 34 160 67.50 Hunslet l9 12 6 1 33 49 213 23 38 170 6578 Merthyr Tl 6 10 5 1 26 39 169 31 25 143 6562 Fl'dderstrld23 14 7 2 43 69 293 39 3R 192 6521 Salford 2O 13 7 0 49 52 254 29 41 181 6500 Runcorn -18 11. 7 1 21. 45 177 23 21 118 6052 Warring'n 20 11 8 1 30 41 212 24 35 153 57.50 Broughton 20 11 8 1 51 55 267 37 49 221 5750 Ebbw Valel9 10 3 1 23 51 199 31 40 182 55•26 Hull 23 12 10 1 54 78 342 39 54 240 54-34 Leeds 2O 10 10 0 33 61 259 38 43 205 5000 -Keighley 20 910 1 43 52 242 34 56 236 47.50 Dewsbury 20 911 0 40 41 212 37 49 221 45.00 Leigh 2O 911 0 SO 36 168 43 49 233 4500 St. Helens 19 711 2 31 44 194 43 53 245 4210 Hull K.R.19 711 1 45 49 237 43 65 281 3917 Swinton -.23 814 1 31 39 179 39 62 264 31395 Bradford 19 7 12 0 36 45 207 39 54 240 36.81 Mid-Rh'da 13 4 8 1 11 18 76 25 33 149 34'61 York 00 613 1 39 49 225 50 71 313 32.50 RochdaleHl9 512 2 13 28 110 34 44 200 31'57 Barrow ... 19 6 13 0 23 29 133 41 67 283 31-57 Treherbertls 410 1 14 15 73 15 41 153 30.00 Widnes l7 311 3 18 26 114 26 40 172 2647 Barry l5 2 13 0 8 14 58 50 89 367 13.33 Aberdare 13 112 0 16 23 101 45 64 282 769 Brarniey -17 116 0 23 21 109 57 101 417 5.88 Tbe match between Leeds and Bradford, which was abandoned owing to the storm, is not included in the It.boyc ta!bic.</p>
        <p>1.1</p>
        <p>although he was not now a commercial traveller he had' had someexperience in that walk of life and be recog— nized that on the work of the traveller the welfare of any concern very largly depended He could trace the success of many a firm to the valuable services rendered: by the commercial gentlemen who represented them - outside. The two great factors in commerce were to be able to manufacture by the best possible method, with quality as the main spring, and then to get a good man to sell their produce. It had been said that in these* days commercial travellers had diflerent conditions to face, owing to the growth of limited companies. That was quite true, but nevertheless he believed there were conscientious gentlemen at the head of concerns who wished to buy in the best market and it needed the persuasive powers of the commercial travellers to recommend the goods of their respective firms. The Warrington branch of the association had now nearly one hundred members and that spoke for itself of the interest that centred in the organisation. He hoped it would continue to grow and he was confident that if Warrington could always be represented by such gentle- men as were connected with that association it would be to the continued advantage of its various under- takings. (Applause). Mr. A. J. ALLBN responded and pointed out that last year the branch raised something like £3O for the children's summer camp. It was their intention this year to make a great effort on behalf of the benevolent institution.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. MERCRR proposed "The Vice-Presidentg," and Councillor W. B. ROBERTS, in responding, said he regarded the profession of the commercial traveller as a high one, because in these days of specialization he must be an expert iu his business and a well educated man, able to talk on all subjects. It was necessary every man should provide for the future, and the only way they could do was that was by combination and supporting their funds. (Applause.) Mr. J. T. KNowLEs, in proposing "The Visitors,' suggested that the Warrington branch ought to be- represented on the Chamber of Commerce, as was already the case in some towns.</p>
        <p>Hockey, Widnes beat Latchford First last Saturday by 2-0 at Widnes, though thereby hangs a tale. The g7;.",nd was in a deplorable condition and the home referee was more deplorable still, Latchford gave a good account of themselves under adverse circumstances.</p>
        <p>Stockport" A " were defeated by the second team at Latchford by 7-0 in a hard game. The home forwards were again in very good trim and goals were scored by Minards (2), Booth (3), Hackney and Pickles, 1 each. The visitors' efforts to get through were well frustrated by the Latchford backs, Knight, Tolo and Pickles being prominent in defence. To-day both teams go to Liverpool. The first oppose Liverpool University at Stanley and the second team Oxton "B " at Oxton. The first team is :—W. Unsworth, goal • C. Plinston, T. Dixon. backs; F. Newell, J. B. Wilkinson, J. Albinson, helves; D. Travis, H. Parker, H. Scott, B. Banks, W. Sponsley, otwa'd. • Referee, Mr. Cobb. Train from Central Station at 1.0. The second team is :—J. Appleton, goal ; J. Wilkin- son, W. H. Pickles, backs; L. Kenworthy, A. Knight, F. Tole, halves ; 11. Booth, C. Hackney, A. Allender, W. l'Ainards, A. Starkey, forwards. Referee, Mr. Warburton. Train from Central Station 1.50.</p>
        <p>MR. FRANK BOOTH'S OPERETTA. " Cinderella" at Walton. Our townsman Mr. Frank Booth is to be warmly congratulated upon the successful production of an operetta " Cinderella " which he has written and composed. The first public performances of the new work took place in the Walton Parish Room on Tuesday Wednesday and Thursday evenings and not- withstanding the smallness of the accommodation which precluded elaborate stage effects and limited the forces engaged, an entirely pleasing presentation was given. The room has been filled each evening and the audiences which assembled were highly entertained by the original and humorous- libretto wedded to bright and tuneful music, which Mr Booth has woven round the old-time story of Cinderella, and they were also loud in their praises of the admirable way in which all concerned in the enterprise acquitted themselves. Mr. Booth had got together a capable com- pany of lccal artistes who threw themselves whole- heartedly into the parts allotted to them. Miss Dorothy Trusloye achieved conspicuous success as Cinderella, the vivacious as well as the more pathetic side of the character being well realized. What especially charmed the audieno,o was her swoot eining, tasteful and expressive vocalisation of such choice numbers as " Someday," " Once more a lowly garb,' and the waltz song" Happy the love," forming one of the most attractive features of the operetta. Mr. Walter Moores was a gallant and dashing prince and he sang "Vanished as mist " with much feeling. Mr. John Millwood and Miss Alys Walsh were well placed as Signor Romero and the Baroness respectively and Miss Edith Partington was a charming Beesvving, the Fairy Queen. Mr. Tom Critchley and Mr. William W. Whittle made the most of their parts as the proud sisters, Clorinda and Miranda, and were responsible for a great deal of merriment. Their duet " A. modern maid " with its topical allusions quite took the fancy of the audiences. A word of praise is due to the children who played a prominent part in the operetta. Classified as to the boys as a chorus of cooks and as to the girls as a fairy band they had evidently been most carefully trained and had also proved themselves apt pupils, for in song and action they were equally pleasing, while the grouping was very effective, Their names are : Head cook, H. Brereton ; cooks, W. Barlow, J. Hargreaves, R. Banks, S. Gee; heralds, T. Barlow, E. Hargreaves ; page, A. Wilkinson ; Gossamer, Miss Milly Truslove ; fairy chorus, J. and E. Williams, C. and A. Bell, N. G. and V. Moulsdale. —ln the second act there was an acceptable diversion, oppor- tunity being found for special items of a miscellaneous and foreign kind by Mr. Walter Moores, Mr. W. W. Whittle and Mr. T. Critchley, all of whom proved good entertainers. The stage management was in the capable hands of Mrs. Darwall and Mr. Walter Moores. Mr. Frank Booth played the pianoforte score with taste and skill, and Mr. William Gornall lent occasional assist- ance with the cornet. The Rev. L. J. T. Darwall, who has taken great interest in the production, and who along with Mrs. Newcombe has contributed to the " lyrics,' ably conducted when the exigencies of the choral parts demanded this service. At the close of each performance the principals were called befoie the curtain and received quite an ovation. There were also loud cries of " author," and Mr. Booth was enthusiastically cheered on rising to acknowledge the gratifying reception of his operetta. Quite apart from the interesting performances of " Cinderella" above mentioned, there remains to be, j noticed the fact that the operetta is written and com- posed by Mr. Frank Booth, the organist at Walton j Church, and a well-known townsman That music,so bright and varied in character, and a book of lyries and spoken words so genuinely humorous and so largely original, should have their origin in our midst, are points in regard to which Mr. Booth is to be I heartily congratulated and Warringtonians may well I feel some pride. The operetta is by no means merely a number of ditties &amp;c. loosely hanging together. On the contrary the spoken words are : relatively few and unimportant, the fabric of " Cinderella" being a closely woven web of solo, : duet, concerted piece and chorus, each and all of which are to a great extent interdependent. If now and then some of the rhymes and root ideas of the book are Gilbertian in character it is implied that, following a good model, the words,the topical allusions, and the humour are often both as witty as the • verse construction is highly ingenious. The music, while singularly happy in realising the spirit of the lyrical story, makes no undue demands upon the vocal capacity ; of the average amateur, a fact which will commend the operetta to a wide range of small musical bodies ' who are ever on the look out for a novelty. The rhythms and musical forms requisitioned by Mr. Booth are especially appropriate and pleasing,the gracefulness of the Valse lente and the quaintness of the gavotte being used with good effect in both song and dance, while the pianoforte score often discloses a wealth of counter-melody and enriching devices as admirable as they are useful in enhancing the total effect. Some of the more serious songs such as " Someday" and "Once more in lowly garb,"which naturally fall to the lot of the forlorn Cinderella, are charming in a certain haunting yet pensive melancholy which is not unworthy of Sullivan in the same mood. The operetta is however, brimful of gaiety and sprightliness and the action and movement has been well and effectively arranged. Amongst recurring dances in which the whole company join there is one dainty solo dance for the Fairy Queen which is well calculated to delight, if not to turn the head, of any young lady aspirant to the honours of the amateur opera stage.</p>
        <p>Councillor WLLK INSOY, in responding, said that many years ago he was one of their brotherhood, travelling on the road for the business with which he was con- nected. He had amongst his bosom friends many gentlemen belonging to their order, whom he held in the gre.test possible esteem. He had always found them to be "Hail fellows well met." Commercial travellers were the pioneers of trade. They preceded the missionaries in foreign lands : they were the ambassadors of trade, and where trade and commerce took root civilization must follow. He believed that the North Pole would have been discovered many years ago if commercial travellers had thought that when they got there they could have obtained a good, substantial order to clear their expenses. (Laughter.) He wished the members of the 11.K.C.T.A. great prosperity in alt their enterprises and trusted that, under the stimulus of their efforts, the mighty heart of British trade and industry would beat with renewed energy, and would speedily bring j,hat great revival in trade which they hoped was norfar distant. (Applause). The toasts of "The Artistes ' and "The President," were also drunk, the latter being accorded musical honours. The Mayor- was similarly honoured whm thanked for presiding. During the evening songs were given in excellent by Mr. J. B. Poole Mr. F. Fieldhouse and Mr. G. H. Smith (humorous), and Mr. E. A. Sanders contributed two capital .ketches. Mr. G, Anderton was the, accompanist.</p>
