      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>QUESTIONS FOR TARIFF REFORMERS.</head>
        <p>QUESTIONS FOR TARIFF REFORMERS.</p>
        <p>XIV.—If Protection confers pros- perity and comfort upon a nation, how is it that our Protectionists tell us that it is necessary to keep out Con- tinental goods because they ar:. made by cheap labour P</p>
        <p>" Tariff Reform" is always contradicting itself. Sometimes we are told to keep out foreign goods, because they are made by "sweated labour," and at other times we are told that Protectionist nations enjoy a prosperity to which we are strangers. The fact is,(l course, that in all but the " new " industrial countries, wages are lower and hours of labour longer than in the United Kingdom. The Board of Trade in the Fiscal Blue Book gave the following facts as to wages : Average Weekly Wages in Fifteen Skilled Trades in Britain, America, Germany, and France, 1903. In the Capital In Other Towns. Towns.</p>
        <p>s. d. s. d. United Kingdom ... 42 0 ... 36 0 United States ... 75 0 69 4 Germany 24 0 ... 22 6 France ..• 36 0 22 10 And they also pointed out that, "besides the difference of wages level there is a difference in the number of hours worked per week." The Board of Tr3de added : "Generally speaking, the countries com- pared stand in the following order as regards the length of the current hours of labour : Germany, France, United States, United Kingdom." Germany has ,the longest hours of labour, while Britain has the shortest. L. G. C. M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Mn. G. H. W. STEEL writes to say that an attack of influenza has compelled him to postpone his reply to Mr. E. England's letter ou Tariff Reform, until next week.—ED. " Ex."</p>
