g a vote of thanks to i the speakers, said his position was that of the fair-play _ _ For Children's tacking Cough at night, Wood's i man. He could not for the life of him see why Great Peppermint Cure, is. 1. 2s. 9cl.—Adyt.</p>
        <p>The complete draw for the Ist round is as follows: Stanningley or Normanton v. Hull. Beverley v. Ebbw Vale. Cadishead or Pemberton v. Keighley. Rochdale Hornets v. Swinton. Barrow St. George's v. Barrow. Bramley v. Broughton Rangers. Wigan v. York. Widnes v. Huddersfield. Warrington v. Leeds. Hunslet v. Alid-Rhondda. Salford v. Dewsbury. Halifax v. St. Helens. Bradford v. Wakefield Trinity. Runcorn v. Egremont. Leigh v. Batley. Oldham v. Uull Kingston Rovers.</p>
        <p>It is remarkable how fortunate the Lancashire senior clubs have been in avoiding one another and in getting fairly comfortable home matches. Only Swinton, Broughton Rangers and St. Helens are called upon to go away from home, and of these the Rangers have the easiest task. Providence seems to have lent her aid to tha mighty. Perhaps she will knock their heads togethdr in the second round.</p>
        <p>The last time Warrington met Leeds in a Cup-tie was in the season 1900-1 at Leeds, also in the first round. 'Warrington surprised everybody by winning with ease, the score in their favour being 21 points to nil. Warrington subsequently ran to the final, only to succumb to Batley on the same ground. Fish and Dickenson were among the threequarters even then, and both have a considerable cup-tie experience to look back upon.</p>
        <p>There are symptoms of the cup-tie fever in the air already, and the Wirepullers look hopefully ahead. They still hold the record of being the only Lanca- shire team to twice secure the Cup, but even this record may be improved upon. They might make the total into three and thus get a lengthy lead over all competitors. There is every prospect of a strong team being available—perhaps the best the club has ever had—for it is hoped that both Shugars and F. Jenkins will have donned the jersey in time to qualify. The forwards just lack ctie thing—weight. With A. S. Boardman out the average will be nearer 12 stones 7 lbs. than 13 stones 7 lbs. Get the middle weight up to a level thirteen stones, and the pack, will want some beating.</p>
        <p>The League fixture to-day is at St. Helens, where Warrington have already been successful in a cup-tie. They will have a harder task to-day, for the Saints have improved their team and are playing a much better game. Still they are not yet in the front rank, and a proper display of form by the Wirepullers will bring back the requisite points.</p>
        <p>Tlü feaiii will be : —Tilley, full back ; Fish, Dickenson, Taylor and Treharne, three-quarters ; Lloyd and Jenkins, half-backs ; Thomas, Dowell, Belton, A. Thardman, P. Boardman and Naylor, forwards. O'Neill has had tp standdo wn owing to an injured toe.</p>
        <p>The "A" team will be glad to have another match, and with St, Helens " A" as visitors should pile up points. Postponed matches have injured their record, as they all count as draws, and on games played Warrington "A " are only a point better off than Wigan "A."</p>
        <p>The Lancashire Combination League table up-to-1 date is as follows :-- P. W. L. D. PM Wigan - 12 .- 2 -. 3 -. 27 Warrington l6 -. 11 -. 1 -. 4 -. 26 Oldham l4 _. 8 __ 3 _. 3 -. 19 Pemberton Wigan Elighfleld - l5 _. 7 .- G -. 2 -. 16 Leigh Shamrocks - ll _. 6 -. 4 -. 4 -. 16 Runcorn - 7 -. 8 -. 1 _. 15 Broughton Rangers 16 .- G .- 8 -. 2 -. 14 Salford - 5 .- 9 -. 3 -. 13 St. Helens Rochdale Hornets l4 -. 5 -. 9 -. 0 -. 10 Ince Widnes l7 -. 4 -. 11 -. 2 -. 10</p>
        <p>The "A " team selected for to-day is Mclntyre, full back; Ilockenhull, Jordan, Cartwright and Brad- shaw, three-quarters ; Lees and Daintith, half hacks; Chesters, Bradshaw, Harrison, Harmer, Hewitt and Gibbs, forwards.</p>
        <p>The second test match between the N.IT., and the Kangaroos will take place on the Newcastle United ground to-day. Brook's damaged side is better, and he will turn out.</p>
        <p>The Warrington officials will be interested to learn that Leeds have secured Dai Furniss, a young Cardiff three-quarter who was anxious to come to Wilderspool. I believe Warrington thought him too light though he is well spoken of in Wales. Leeds are reported to have paid him XSCI and given him very handsome terms for playing. Perhaps we shall see Furniss on the 27th of February.</p>
        <p>Another two valuable points were earned by Warrington on Saturday, when they exceeded expecta- tions by easily defeating Wakefield Trinity on the Wilderspool ground. Not that, in the ordinary way, there was any likelihood of Wakefield performing so unusual a feat as to beat Warrington on their own ground, but on Saturday the confidence in the homesters was lessened owing to the fact that three such important backs as Brooks, Jenkins and Dicken- son were absent owing to injury. Wakefield have occupied a place in the first four for some time, and their anxiety to keep there made them doughty foes: Add to this their reputation for being one of the most improved sides in Yorkshire and it will be seen that Warrington's prospects were not of the brightest. It was a source of gratification to all their supporters to see the way they rose to the occasion and trounced the Trinity by 14 points to 5.</p>
        <p>I pointed out last week that everything depended on the Warrington forwards if this match was to be won. With,a comparatively weak back division behind them it was essential they should secure the advantage in the scrum. The home six appeared to fully realist) this, with the fssult that the backs were given con- fidence and played in a fashion that no one could cavil at. When Wakefield, playing with the gale at their backs, secured a lead of 5 points in the first few minutes it looked very probable that Warrington would have a heavy deficit to face at half time. This was the crucial point of the game, and the Warring- ton forwards fairly rose to the occasion. Showing magnificent dash, they fought their way to the Wakefield quarters in the teeth of the gale. Once there they held on tight to the advantage until the score was equalised. The backs combined remark- ably well at this stage and it was a really superb bit of combination that enabled Treharne to score the equalising point behind the posts. The Trinity men made poor use of the wind afterwards and were held in check, so that the locals crossed over on level terms</p>
        <p>The match was practically won at this stage, for Wakefield were quite unequal to emulating Warring- ton's feat of scoring against the gale. Their forwards fought hard but Warrington Were always on top and rarely allowed the ball to leave the Wakefield half. O'Neill gave his side the lead with a try which he secured in the crafty resourceful way the Warrington crowd have learnt to expect from so experienced a player. He ran thirty yards at quite a moderate pace and fairly diddled the Wakefield men who were expecting a pass all the time. Fish got the next with a fine individual effort. He secured at centre, beat Bennett for speed, and then kicking over Metcalfe's head had the rest all to himself. The final point was secured by the home full back, Tilley, who crowned a grand display by following up his own kick and taking the ball over the line after Thomas had picked up and passed.</p>
        <p>For Influenza and Bronchial Coughs,Woods' Great Peppermint Cure. Is. lid., 2s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The forwards I have already given credit to. Next one Lloyd and Lees, who played really clever</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL TRAVELLERS.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL DINNER AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A highly successful function was the third annual I dinner in connection with the Warrington and District branch of the United Kingdom Commercial Travellers' Association which was held at the Lion Hotel on Satur- day evening. The branch is undoubtedly fulfilling a most useful purpose and its work is meeting with encouraging support. About sixty members and guests assembled at the dinner, and a capital repast was served by Host Lee. Alderman W. Pierpoint (President of the branch) presided, and amongst those present were : —The Mayor (Alderman W. J. Forshaw), Mr. R. Pier- point, Mr. W. F. Brooks (ex-chairman U.K.C.T.A.), Councillors J. Wilkinson, J. Fulton Smith, J. T. Locker and W. B. Roberts, Mr. W.Bibby,Mr. A. J.Allen (chairman of the branch), Mr. J. T. Knowles (vice- chairman), and Mr. W. G. Hayes (hon. secretary). The HON. SHCRETARY announced that he had received a letter from Mr. A. H. Croslield, M.P., in response to an invitation to attend, expressing his sincere apprecia- tion of their courtesy and his regret that, as he would be abroad, he would be unable to be present. Had it been practicaWe he would have been very pleased indeed to have accepted the invitation. Mr. Hayes added that Mr. Crosfield had been closely connected with commercial matters in this town for many years and if it had been at all possible ho would have been present at that gathering. Councillor T. S. Steel, the solicitor to the branch,also wrote regretting that through doctor's orders he was prevented from attending. He hoped the association would grow in numbers and usefulness. Mr. Harrington telegraphed from Great Yarmouth wishing his fellow members a very successfal evening. . .</p>
        <p>The 1-oyal toasts were proposed by the President and enthusiastically honoured. THE WARREN GTON OF BYE-GONE DAYS.</p>
        <p>Mr. IL Prmrtrom in proposing" The Town and Trade of Warrington "said he might claim to be, unfortu- nately, one of the oldest inhabitants of the town and he would like to look back into the past and compare the Warrington of bye-gone days with the Warrington of to-day. Mr. Pierpoint then quoted an inter- esting description of Warrington of 160 years ago, when it had a considerable market on a Wednesday, noted for lampary and all sorts of fish, flesh, corn, cattle, &amp;c., and £5OO worth of buck-a-back was sold every market-day. The malt here was so good that the ale brewed with it was said to be not inferior to the best in England. (Laughter.) As the town lay on the main road to Carlisle and Scotland it had always been a pass of the utmost importance in time of war and rebellion. In the river were caught sturgeons, green- backs, mullets, seals, sand-eels, lobsters, oysters,shrimps, prawns, the best and largest cockles in England, with other shell fish and mussels in such abundance that they servc;4to manure the ground. In later times they had qd now vanished. Not far from his own manufactured which helped to house which cloth was. sail Nelson's fleet at the battle of Trafalgar. Then Warrington built ships, and he cc;!el remember as a boy being present at the launch of the Ta.,--Aur, which was unfortunately wrecked, and its wrecking wrecked the concern, as far as regarded Warrington. Thera was the manufacture of copper, which was now absolutely defunct. The Town Hall was founded on copper shy,b which came from the neighbouring furnaces belonging to the Patten family 150 years ago. Glass was, he was happy to say, still going on, but it was not the great trade it used to be, when it was represented gone by blleyMkonreewfir:nlOt't4 lotfwthae ngorewa:t nii3zulritufiancttliimtee: of iron, wire, steel, india-rubber, &amp;c., and had none of the great chimneys with smoke belching forth all over the town, which was a sign of good trade. (Hear, hear.) They could congratulate themselves upon having such a variety of trades, to which they must attribute the fact that they suffered less from want of employment than nearly any town in the kingdom. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>NEW BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>THE NEW BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR, responding, said-th-e—T—o-wn Council had unanimonsly decided to have a new Warrington Bridge, and he was pleased that his proposal that the bridge bhould be 80ft. wide had met with such se,stantial support, both inside and outside the Council. (Applause.) They could not do with less than SOfts because of the enormously increasing traffic on the bridge. They wanted two lines of tramways, and to have those nothing less than 80ft, would do. They must provide for the future. Speaking generally as to the progress of the town, Warrington was not lagging behind any town in Lancashire. (Hear, hear.) He believed that the progress of manufactures, sanitary reforms, education, street improvements and everything connected with the welfare of the town was never better, and without speaking at all politically, the Cor- poration was never better constituted than at present. The office of a member of the Corporation was not an enviable one. Everything they did and everthing they said was occasionally subject to severe observation, but he was convinced that the Corporation was fulfulling its duty with the single-minded desire to do all in its power for the best interests of the town and the con- venience and comfort of its inhabitants. (Applause). Conncillor FULTON-SMITH also replied. He said Warrington was purely a commercial toNvn, and he thought that characteristio of it had been lost sight of by a good number of people, u4,.0p1.0 tuu.,l% abuut the smoke. Warrington as a commercial town was well known the world over. There was not a</p>
        <p>market but what Warrington was known as a trademark for excellency of manufacture, and not only for its excellency but for its intrinsic value. They had a many-sided town with regard to manufacturesand each one of the different trades carried on was par excel- lence the manufacturing firm of that particular trade. They had the glass trade, which, although not so exten- sive as it had been in the past, was of such a character that it was known that in Warrington they got the finest class of glass presented to the community, and he understood that a great deal of their manufacture was _ _ _</p>
        <p>employed in the Royal Family to such an extent that he thought they ought to have tha use of the Royal Coat of Arms. There were other interests well known and valued in the markets for their manufactures, and were it not for the beer in the glass the glass would not be so well known. (Laughter.) Whether the beer enhanced the value of the glass he did not know, but certainly the two went very well together' and he dare say the reputation of Warrington stood very much upon That combination. (Laughter.) Councillor Fulton- Smith concluded by referring to the increased ditliculty of the commercial traveller's work in view of the fact that large limited companies were rapidly taking the place of the individual proprietor. The commercial traveller was a man who deserved the utmost sympathy from all Indies. He wished the society every success. (Applause.) At this point the Mayor presided in place of Alder- man Pierpoint, who had to leave for home at St. Annes-on-Sea.</p>
        <p>THE U.K.C.T.A. Mr. HAYES proposed the toast of " rho U.K.C.T.A. and the Commercial Travellers' Schools and Benevolent Institution" and said he believed the association had done a great deal more than many of them realized to improve the lot of the commercial traveller. A great many children had passed through the schools and had turned out well—in fact there were very few, if any, failures. An excellent education was given, but unfor- tunately the schools did not receive the support they should do. At present they had to rely a great deal upon outside help. The Benevolent Institution was also doing excellent work, and there were 400 annuitants receiving up to £5O a year. Mr. BROOKS, in responding, gave an interesting and exhaustive address on the work of the association. He began by remarking that he was present at the forma- tion of the Warrington branch and he was sure that, officered as they' were, nothing but success could attend their efforts. The association was now in its twenty-sixth year. The old methods were not considered sufficient for the tip-to-date requirements of the Association and at the last conference they did away with the antiquated and cumbersome Central Board by which they had been governed and adopted the more up-to-date and business-like method of being governed, during the interim between the conferences, by an Executive Council. The Council was quite in its infancy, and on the 30th inst. they hoped to appoint a paid organizing secretary. He believed that as a result of that their ranks would be greatly strengthened. The new Council had inaugurated an employment bureau, which was now almost in full working order, and they hoped the members of the Association and the firms requiring travellers would make full use of it. The benefit society was commenced six years ago with a membership of 626 and an income of over £2,000. At the end of 1907 they had over 2,100 members and an income of over £13,000 per annum. (Applause.) That stood out as a record in the annals of benefit societies. Last year they enrolled 408 members with a share capital of £1,312. The total membership to date was 2,%60, holding 8,000 shares. Last year they paid in sick pay £2,600 ; since the society was established they had paid over £lO,OOO on sick claims. Notwithstanding that large amount the menibers had accumulated over £41,000, and nearly the whole of that was advanced on mortgage chiefly to members to purchase their own houses. He wished commercial travellers would study their own interests as a class more than they did at present. Their sole object appeared to be the interests of the firms they represented,and that largely should he so, but they had to consider the changed conditions which made it more than ever necessary for commercial travellers to have an effective organization. Referring to the proposed formation of a chartered association, Mr. Brooks said that was a step in the right direction, as it would raise the status of the commercial traveller. Since the inception of the schools 2,800 children had passed through them, and at the present time there were 300 in residence. The expenditure was about £25,000 per annum. Since the substitution of the penny breakfast collection for the dinner collection over £2,000 had been collected for those charities. He commended the Benevolent Institution as the best insurance a young traveller could possibly take psrt in. Pensions had been provided for necessitous travellers and/provision , made for widows and orphans, and the institution was a I thoroughly deserving one. (Applause.) THE WARRINGTON BRANCH.</p>
        <p>Councillor LOCKER. proposed" The Warrington an d District Branch of the U.K.C.T.A." and said tha</p>
