      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>,‘ t: ROYAL COURT THEATRE. ;1! " Raffies."</head>
        <p>,‘ t: ROYAL COURT THEATRE. ;1! " Raffies."</p>
        <p>k: ' .</p>
        <p>Vbe memorable week of grand opera at the Royal Court Theatre has been succeeded by another attrac- tion of special interest and merit. " Baffles," which is presented by Mr. Leonard Boyne's West End Company, is unquestionably one of the cleverest plays of our time and the exploits of the amateur cracksman rejoicing in thut name provide a thoroughly entertain- ing evening. " Amateur " as applied to Raffles' burglarious accomplishments is somewhat of a misnomer. In reality he is an expert professional, baffling the police by the upLto dateness of his methods and making rich hauls, bnt he is called " amateur " to distinguish him from the orthodox and often clumsy type of burglar. No-one would suspect Raffles of the daring coups which create consternation in the mansions of the rich. He is the idol of society ; the ladies are captivated by his personal charm,and. the men admire his prowess as a cricketer and vote him a real good fellow. When Lady Melrose's brilliant diamond necklace disappears, Raffles looks sublimely innocent and expresses pious- hopes that the offender will be brought to book. That he himself is the culprit no one has the remotest suspicion— except Curtis Bedford the detective, who is a cut above Scotland Yard methods. The affair quickly resolves itself into a duel of wits between the pair. Raffles gently twits the detective and. even goes the length of betting him £lOO that he will not restore the stolen necklace to its rightful owner in twenty-four hours. What is more, the cracksmah wins the wager. Curtis Bedford is wide-awake and it does not take him long to get on the scent. He almost brings off the arrest, but "there's many a slip" and Raffles—for he is indeed the thief—is not the man to give in when odds are against him., and he escapes in an ingenious fashion which wins the admiration of all—including the foiled detective. Raffles is acclaimed tlae hero of the piece. From the very first the audience admire him and as the plot unfolds itself they are so delighted with his pluck and resourcefulness, as well as with other decidedly good points in his character, that they would be quite disappointed if he came within the clutches of the law. The play enchains attention throughout the four acts and although for the most part merriment holds sway, there are some powerful dramatic situations as well as a touch of the pathetic. Mr. Eille Norwood as Rallies is superb. His sang 'raid when chaffing the detective is irresistibly amusing, while the way in which 119 cows a revengeful burglar of the Bill Sykes type by picturing. the horrors of the death sentence bolds the audience spell- bound. Altogether his portrayal is a brilliant one and he has had to respond to many enthusiastic "calls." Mr. E. W. Thomas shares the honours as Curtis Bedford. " Sport for sport's sake" is the spirit in which this fussy and energetic detective does his work and Mr. Thomas makes the most of a splendid part. Mr. Dalziel Heron gives a fine impersonation of Crawley, the burglar, and the scene in which lie threatens the imperturbable Raffles is the best in the play. Mr. Harold West is well placed as Harry Manders (Bunny), who, although his idol is so rudely shattered, remains faithful to "A. J." Miss Lucie Milner is a winsome Gwendoline Conran and Miss May Brooke achieves considerable success as Mrs. Vidal. The other parts are all in capable hands. As a curtain raiser" A Lesson in Shakespeare" is bright and laughable, the characters being admirably taken by Mr. Dalziel Heron, Mr. Harold West and Miss Beatrice Fitzgerald. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRA.MIIE.</p>
        <p>I TWO LA.NC.A.93IRE LX3.3E3 II LONTD3N? Next week the boards at the Royal Court Theatre are to be occupied by Hardie and Von Leer's London 4company in a play that has proved a great dramatic success, namely, "Two Lancashire Lasses in London," written by Arthur Shirley and Sutton Vane. We are told it is not a drama of impossible situations, ranting dialogue, and ridiculous comedy. but a play instinct with life —the everyday life of the great Metropolis in which its scenes are laid—and introducing characters that at once attract by their fidelity to nature, and whose actions (always prompted by some adequate and convincing motive) one follows with the keenest interest. The company is a strong one, includina such capable artistes as Miss Rosslyn Vayne, Miss Alice Defoe, Miss Stanley 'Burt, Miss Evelyn Shaw and Messrs. Chris Mason Arthur Elwyn, Jack Miller, W. Walmsley, and Dick Winslow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. S. DAVIES.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. S. DAVIES.</p>
        <p>Many of our readers will regret to hear of the deatil of another worthy Warringtonian in the person of Mr, Samuel Davies, baker and provision dealer, 15 and 17, Wellington-street, who passed away on Monday after- noon. The deceased gentleman, who was seventy years of age, had been in failing health for about a year and had been compelled to relinquish public work. He was a native of Warrington and received his education at the Blue Coat school. For some time he was in the service of the late Mr. Geo. Powlson, of Market-gate, the editor and proprietor of the old " Warrington Advertiser." He began business as a baker in Wellington-street in •1871, and the concern has been carried on with success up to the present time. Mr. Davies became a member of the Town Council on the 28th March, 1 900, succeeding the late Councillor Dr. Cannell, one of the representatives of the Howley Ward. He continued to serv&amp; on that authority until November, 1901, when, he was defeated by Mr. William Clegg, after a keen contest, by a majority of 24. Mr. Davies was also a: member of the Board of Guardians for many years and took a great interest in Poor Law work. On his ; retirement several months ago, owing to ill-health, h e- wes succeeded by Mr. Robert Lee. In politics Mr. Davies was a Conservative. He was a devoted mem- ber of the Church of England and was warml y- attached to the old Parish Church. In the time of the late Rev. W. Quekett he was one of the wardens and he had also held the offices of Sunday School superin-• tendent and sidesman. He leaves nine sons and two daughters with whom much sympathy is felt in their- cmcat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-01-23_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery on rhursday afternoon in the presence of a large gathering of relatives and friends. The first part of - the service was held in the Parish Church, the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis) and the Rev. T. W. Barber officiat- ing. The hymns" Now the labourer's task is o'er "and " Let saints on earth" were sung. Mr. F. M. Darby, Mus. Bac. presided at the organ and played the " Dead March "in " Saul." The last rites at the graveside were performed by the Rector. The chief mourners were :—Messrs. W. D. Davies, James B. Davies, Samuel R. Davies, Thomas W. Davies, Alfred E. Davies,. Frederick W.Davies and George L.Davies (sons),Misses Mary Elizabeth Davies and Beatrice May Davies (daughters), Mr. William Davies (brother), Messrs. Joseph Heron, James Heron and James Abram (cousins), Mrs. William • Davies and Mrs. Annie Davies (daughters-in-law), Miss Heron and Miss Nellie Martin. The Warrington Master Bakers'' Association, of which the deceased was a former Presi- dent and Secretary, was represented by Messrs. W. Strange (president), J. Eckersley, J. E. Hannan and E. Walker. The bearers were the following officials and members of the Warrington branch or the Amalgamated Union of Operative Bakers :—Messrs. W. Nicholls, J. Howard, T. Oultrarn, J. Gleeson, and G. A. Jenkins with Mr. W. Rathbone (district secretary). The deceased was a great supporter of the society and unfurled the new district banner on February 13th, 1904.. The Warrington Board of Guardians was represented by Messrs. W. Flannagan, W. C. Brooks, R. Bolton, A. Bottomley (clerk), and B.Shearer(relieving officer). The officers of the Warrington Parish Church in attendance were :—Councillor Craik, Councillor Locker, Mr. R. W. Collin and Mr. James Wood. Mr. A. Jolley and Mr. T. A. Sutton (hon. secretary) represented the Old People's Treat Committee, Mr. W. Holbrook, the Conservative Association and Mr. W. L. Williams, the Junior Conservative Association. Amongst others. present were : Councillor Rogers and Mr. T. Longdia (borough surveyor). Wreaths were sent by the follow- ing :—" Jim and Agnes " (Stockport), Mrs. Cross and Fanny, "Tom and Annie," Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Harrison, "Joe and Nellie," Mr. and Mrs. T. Chantler, " Beetle, Sam, Bert, Fred and George," Warrington and District 01T-Licence Holders' Association, Warrington Master Bakers and Confectioners' Association, officers of the Warrington Parish Church..</p>
        <p>REFERENCE AT TIE GUARDIANS' MEETING. At the meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians on Wednesday, the CHAIRMAN (Mr. George Mather) moved a resolution that the Clerk be instructed to convey an expression of the Board's deep sympathy with the family of the late Mr. S. Davies in their bereavement. He said Mr. Davies was a member of that Board for many years before he (Mr. Mather) became a member. He had also been a member of the Town Council and was a very useful and conscientious man whom many of them could wish to imitate and follow in his mode of life. The whole town would feel the loss of such a man. Mr. W. HOUGHTON seconded and said he had known the late Mr. Davies for very many years and had always. had the greatest admiration for his public work of all descriptions. Mr. JAMBS' EVANi supported. He said he had seen Mr. Davies's work on the Town Council and could quite endorse what had fallen from the previous speakers. He had also had some trade connections with :\lr. Davies, being in the same business, and he had always found him to act in a very honourable and creditable manner.</p>
        <p>The resolution was carried, the members standing as a mark of respect.</p>
        <p>Births, Marriages, and Deaths.</p>
        <p>All announcements of Births, Marriages, and Deaths are charged at the rate of is. 61. for not more than three lines, and 6d. for each additional line. MARRIAGE.</p>
        <p>1 BOND—BANKS.—On tho 18th instant at Manchester, - Harry Bendel!, second son of Henry Bond of Warring- ton, to Lilian Brook, daughter of John Brook Banks.</p>
        <p>IN MEMORIAM.</p>
        <p>In ever loving memory of my dear mother, TINSNA.TI OWEN, who fell asleep January 17th, 1397,aged 70 years. "Gone but never forgotten."</p>
