        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL. THE NORTHERN UNION. __— RESULTS OF SATURDAY'S MATCHES. THE NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE. G. T. P. G. T. P. *±Leedg 1 2 8 Bradford *l-Batley *Warrington 1 4 14 Wakefield Trin. 1 1 5 ; Hull Broughton R'ngers 3 3 15 Wigan *Oldham *Rochdale Hornets 0 1 3 St. Helens 0 1 3 1 *Salford *Widnes *Huddersfield 5 6 28 York Hull Kingston R. 1 2 8 Hunslet 0 1 3 *Barrovv "KANGAROOS" IN WALES. *WELSH LEAGUE 1 4 14 "!KANGAROOS " 2 3 13 Warrington Beat Wakefield Trinity. Warrington and Wakefield Trinity met for the first time, for several years, at Warrington on Saturday in a Northern League fixture. The afternoon was fine but the ground was in a very soft and greasy state ; owing to the sleet of the morning. A gale of wind I blew from goal to goal, thus rendering the conditions anything but favourable. Wakefield were well represerted, the only absentees of note being I Lynch and Newbould. Warrington wore rather severely handicapped through injuries to both regular halves, Brooks and Jenkins, and to Dickenson, the captain. There were 4,000 spectators. The teams lined out as follows : Warrington :—Tilley, back ; Fish, Cartwright, Taylor, and Treharne, three-quarter backs; Lees and Lloyd, half backs; Neill, Dowell, Thomas, A. E. Boardman, P. Boardman and Belton, forwards. Wakefield Trinity :—Metcalfe, back; Bennett, Ward, Sidwell and Simpson,three-quarter backs; Kershaw and Slater, half backs; Anton, Crossland, Beaumont, Parker, G. Taylor and Waken, forwards. Referee, Mr. A.Smith, Halifax. Touch judges, Messrs. T. Austin Wigan, and L. Longdin, Swinton. Wakefield won the toss, and Warrington had to start against the gale. Naturally the visitors were soon on the attack, and loose footwork sent the ball over the line, Tilley conceding a dead ball as Beaumont rushed up. Warrington tried hard to work clear but nippy work by Slater and Simpson drove them back. A scrum followed on the line, and the home forwards broke through. Unfortunately they lost possession, and the Trinity men crossing the oval smartly, Sidwell dribbled over between the posts, and Metcalfe added a goal. This early success for the visitors looked very ominous for Warrington, but a grand response was made. The Warrington forwards put forth desperate efforts, and by dint of fine footwork they invaded the Wakefield quarter. Here the backs showed their worth in some clever handling under difficulties. Both Fish and Treharne made good efforts to cross, but were well tackled. The locals were per- sistent however, and fine combination between Lees, Cartwright, Taylor snd Treharne ended in the latter scoring a beautiful try behind the posts. Fish took great care with the kick, and made the scores equal with a good goal. Wakefield made great efforts on resuming, but their pressing was poor and the home defence excellent. Simpson made one good run to the line, where Tilley brought him down. Half-time : 5 points each. Warrington at once took up the attack on the change of ends, but for some time the Wakefield defence held out, Metcalfe saving time after time. Lloyd dropped a pass near the line at one corner, and the game travelled across to the other side. Here O'Neill picked up, and by a sham pass deceived the Wakefield defenders so that he ran over unimpeded with a decidedly clever try. Thomas failed at goal. Wakefield forced their way to centre, where Fish dis- tinguished himself by a fine run and punt which beat the visitors' custodian and brought another try to Warrington's total improved form was exhibited by the , Wakefield forwards near the close, and Auton and Taylor were conspicnous in a fine dribbling movement which was only checked on the Warrington line. Tilley, however, forced matters at the opposite end, and following up a kick took the ball over for a try, but Thomas failed at goal. Result—Warrington 1 goal and 4 tries (14 points), Wakefield Trinity 1 goal and 1 try (3 points). RUNCORN IN A BLIZZARD. Football has never been played at Mount Pleasant, Batley, under worse conditions then were endured on Saturday. Hailstones, accompanied by thunder and lightning, greeted spectators as they arrived on the ground, and the start was delayed twenty-five minutes. A sensational start placed Batley three points ahead in the first minute, as Ward snapped a Runcorn pass and scored a try,which Oakland failed to improve upon. In the last minute of the first half tlie ball was kicked over the Batley line, and Sam Walker followed up and touched the ball just in time. Jolley failing at goal, the scores were level when tha team crossed over. Wattie Davies kicked a penalty goal immediately after the restart, but a terrible hailstorm then etopped the game after 47 minutes' play, the scores being : D-. 003. 1 goal and 1 try (3 points), Runcorn 1 try (3 points.) The match will probably be replayed. ST. HELENS EARN A POINT AT ROCHDALE. There were about 3,000 spectators at Rochdale to witness this match. St. Helens played a new left wing in Harris, from Castleford Half-acre. The ground was covered to the depth of an inch with snow, and there was a strong wind. St. Helens had the advantage of the wind in the initial half, and eventually after a good piece of dribbling, Greenwood grounded the ball behind the posts. The same player failed to land a , goal, the ball striking the cross-bar. As soon as ends were changed the home team equalised in sensational fashion. The ball was kicked across to Robinson from a scramble in the St. Helens half, and he whipped it on to West, who dashed over at the corner with an uncon- verted try. The Hornets attacked strenuously but failed to add to their score and the match ended in a draw of 1 try (3 points) each. WIDNES INDULGE IN HEAVY SCORING. The return fixture with Dewsbury was played at Widnes on Saturday, before 1,000 spectators. Dewsbury who had the advantage of a strong wind, at once attacked, and after some scrambling play near the line Ward scored a try, and Richardson placed a goal from a difficult position. Widnes worked their way into the visitors' half, and from a scrummage near the linb Ditchfleld kicked, and following up, scored. No goal followed. Half-time—Dewsbury, 1 goal and 1 try (5 points), Widnes, 1 try (3 points). In the first few minutes of the second half Widnes attacked, and Lally dropped a goal. The Chemicals kept up the pressure, and the referee awarded them a try for obstruction. Following upon this, the home backs executed a nice passing movement, and Ditchfield scored at the corner flag. Then for the first time in the second half Dewsbury got into their opponents' quarters, and after 13rayshaw had dribbled over the line he was obstructed, and Dewsbury were awarded a try in consequence. No goal followed. Two more tries were scored for Wideis by Taylor and Noon, and Jones placed two goals.—Result :—Widnes, 3 goals and 5 tries (21 points), Dewsbury, 1 goal and 2 tries (8 points). The Kangaroos. Results of the Tour to Date. For. Against. Opponents G. T. P. G. T. P. Mid-Rhondda Brad ford Rochdale Hornets 1 1 5 0 0 0 York 1 1 5 1 1 5 Salford 3 1 9 3 1 9 Runcorn Cumberland League ll 10 52 5 0 10 Leigh Dewsbury Yorkshire Hunslet Aberdare Ci'arringten Northern League hull Kingston Rovers 2 4 16 6 3 21 Lancashire Barrow 3 5 21 1 1 F. Halifax Swinton Northern Union Treherbert Wakefield Leeds Oldham England Widnes 2 3 13 1 0 2 Bat ley Welsh League Wigan</p>
